        <p>which is an item especially calculated to rejoice the heart of the impecunious, who, by picking up a bargain A Sensible Up-Bringing.—The Duchess .of Albany, dress length at one time, and paying the tailor's charges now the baptism of her grandson Prince Rupert of Teck for making up at another, often manage to secure a is accomplished which ceremopy was delayed on costume that would prove unattainable if the whole account of the illness of Sir Robert Collins, hopes to expense occurred at one time. And speaking of bargain spend some time in Germany with her sonthe Duke of dress lengths, one is reminded of the wonderful batch Saxe-Coburg-Gothet. The Duchess of Albany is greatly of autumn and winter patterns now being sent out by attached to her daughter-in-lays, Who is the eldest of the Lutas Leathley, of Armley, Leeds. First and foremost five daughters of Duke Frederick of Schlesing-Holstein. perhaps, come the famous " Wyllwyrwell " serges, All five girls have been brought up to excel in one or Melton cloths, Cheviot friezes,faced cloths and Tweeds. other of the arts, or domestic accomplishments. With a dress length of these fabrics a gift of some Princess Victoria, now Duchess of Saxe-Coburg, holds a silver article is forwarded. Then there are the Bo'ness diploma in the Royal School of Cookery, Berlin,Princess Tartans. striped Tweeds, broken hopsacks, Cheviot Alexandra is a skilled rniniaterepainter,Princess Helena twills, and numberless other materials, all marvellous is a qualified hospital nurse, Princess Adelaide holds a and delightful bargains. A box of patterns is sent free, certificate for Kindergarten teaching, and Princess and need not be returned, moreover all orders of ss. and Caroline is an expert shorthand typist—and vast num- over are forwarded carriage paid. berg of less exalted parents would tee well advised to A Cleaning Process. —Black and navy blue serge, and follow the example of Duke Frederick of Schlesung- all neutral mixtures, may be greatly improved and Holstein, in the up-bringing of their daughters. renovated in the following manner. First the garment The Prince and Princess Mary of Wales.— Whenever should be thoroughly brushed and wiped over with a the children of the Prince and Princess of Wales are clean cloth, then a stiff clothes brush should be dipped allowed to choose a. "treat,' it invariably takes the in ordinary vinegar, and vigorously applied. The form of a picnic, etaste inherited from their Royal garment should then he hung out in the air to dry, and mother, who has a great affection for at frisco meals. Some time back her Royal Highness had a special afterwards pressed with a hot iron and damp cloth. . MADGE. picnic motor car built; the small eg seats being so arranged that no danger exists of a child being flung out should the door open while the car is moving. The car is fitted to accommodate all the things necessary to comfort in taking a meal out of doors, including a folding table, and folding chairs. A Colonial Peerees.—Tbe festivities in connection with the coming of age of Lord Clifton, eldest son of the Earl of Darnley, recall the fact that Lady Darnley is one of our few Colonial peeresses. Many peers have married Americans, but Lord Darnley met his bride, Miss Florence Morphy, the present Lady Darnley in ; Australia. In 1884 he was one of the English cricket: team plying in Australia, and met Miss Morphy at the ; match in Victoria, the marriage taking place soon . afterwards. Lord Darnley was then Mr. Ivo Bligh and some seven years later succeeded his brother in the • earldom. His niece, the little Baroness Clifton, who is in her eighth year, is one of the few peeresses in their own •right, another of youthful age being the little Baroness Beaumont who is thirteen. Beauty v. Cbaracter.—A medical paper has come just ' a little too late with a question which would have afforded an admirable subject for a holiday season dis- cussion in one of the great London papers. It is that I of plain girls, whom the writer declares to be greatly superior in brains to those of their sisters to whom , Nature has been more kind. It is a little difficult to ' perceive why this should be so, but there may at any: rate be some foundation for the suggestion that plain : girls cultivate their brains more than the others. No ; doubt such a correspondence would have elicited ! letters from some husbands who probably would have • remained anonymotis—proclainsing that they had married plain women., and were very glad that they had I done so. Archibald Forbes, had be been still alive, might have been prevailed upon to say something on a subject concerning which he appears to have had a strong opinion. "1 have" he wrote," aconstitutional ; dislike to a pretty woman. I have never known a ; pretty woman who was not impertinently conscious of her charms, and who did net conduct herself as if she had some meritorious share. in the construction of her- I self. as a thing of beauty, instead of being as is matter I of fact wholly urreonsidered and unconsulted in that I operation." Die Marriage Problem.—Tn this country, and to an even greater extent in Arnerica,young people are left to I arrange their love affairs as best they can, but irarance parental authority exercises a good control over such ; matters. The parents 'first meet and if satisfactory, I arrangements are arrived at, the young people are intro- dueed and are expected to fall in with such a settle- ; meat of affairs. As to which is the better of the two! systems is a matter difficultto decide. Those who have i felt so disposed have had opportunities of studying columns of newspaper correspondence on the subject ; during the holiday season, but it has been mostly; unprofitable reading. Upon the whole one is inclined ! to agree with the view that the marriages which most I frequently end in disaster are those which have been; entered into on sentiment etione, and that the greater number that turn out well are the ones in which the business side of the .contract has not been altogether I missed. Young Women and the Conies.—This summer many I thousands of young women have left these shores with ; the object of seeking their fortune and making their home in Canada. Fewer go to Australia, although it appears that capable domestic servants can earn as eood wages there as in Canada. Good cooks can command 30s. a week, whilst the wages for other servants are in proportion, and in the Colonies there is no such thing as the cap and apron badge of servitude. "High wages, I comfortable places, abundant outings, and tirnei-off." These are the attractions offered by the Emigration Office of New South Wales, and the difficulty of getting these is not a great one. A girl must satisfy the Emigre- ; tioo. Again as regards her character, health and train- t ing, but it she ego do that, all she has to pay for her I passage is £3 with a deposit of £2, which is handed I back to her on her arrival at New South Wales. Some fifty of these girl emigrants are to sail in the Miltietees, the end of this month, and other parties are being. arranged for later passages, all of which are made under the care of a matron. Croquet Skirts.—Before the croquet season comes round again, it is to be hoped some more artistic device than the one at present in 'vogue, keeping skirts back will have have been thought out. Of course the' woman who is keen on croquet—and can afford the, luxury, has a special skirt built for this particular game, a skirt which is drawn back tts tightly as possible, with a small box-pleat behind, and no fulness whatever at the hem. ! But the great majority of croquet players simplypro- vide themselves with an elastic band, which is finished at each end with a clip and by this means drag all ful- ness tightly hack below the knees, the result being an ungainly appearance, and an exceedingly ungraceful walk. Autumn Footwear.—ln choosing coloured footwear for the autumn and winter, it is bests to avoid suede. It looks dressy, and is having a great vogue, both in greys and browns, but it soils readily and is to be avoided on that account now that the days of mud and rain is at hand. So also is the black patent leather boot or shoe, with upper of light brown cloth. This style of footwear looks very modish, hut is not to be recom- mended for those whe have to study ways and means. The brown or russet boot or shoe of medium weight is the more practical choice,for this time of the year. There is always a wide choice in dainty styles and desiens,and a very effective form of ornamentation for shoes of this leather, is the covered buckle finished with a tiny edge of gold metal. The Correct Shade in Gloves.—he white glove craze has quite passed away, and at the moment it is the rarest thing to see a well-garbed woman wearing white gloves, save with a white toilette, or one in which white VICAR AND BOOKMAKER. predominates. A delicate pens], and very pale greys and tans, are the leading shades, for dressy gloves suit The Nev. A. M. Mitchell, vicar of Burtonwood, who deep tans and mahogany, are chosen for morning and a fortnight ago tendered his resignation of the Earles- country wear. For the latter stout hut soft leathers town Education Sub-committee, because he was are selected, also the Biarritz termination with a little scandalised by the presence thereon of a bookmaker, on hidden elastic across the inside of the glove. I Tuesday night tendered his resignation as their repro- Obliging Tailors.—Now-a-days it is quite possible to sentative on that committee to the Burtonwood Parish Council. The resignation was accepted, and Mr. T. find a good tailor who will make up one's materials Hewitt appointed in his stead.</p>
