      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Death of a Forester.</head>
        <p>Death of a Forester.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery on Sunday,of Bro. George Wood ward a highly respected member of Court " Hope " 431 (Ancient Order of Foresters). .Bro. Woodward was 63 years of age and almost from its inception had taken an active interest in the workings of Court Hope." The cortege left the deceased's residence in Kerfoot-street, and was attended by ,many Foresters, and here the first portion of the service associated with the order was read by Bro. G. Maybury, P.D.C.11. The procession wended its way to Buckley-street Chapel, and on arrival there Miss Plinston played " 0 rest in the Lord" ("Elijah "). The first part of the funeral service was read by Mr. G. Barton, and the choir sang two hymns. On the body being removed to the hearse Miss Plinston phyed most impressively the Dead March in "Saul."- Six members of the Court "Hope " acted as bearers, and the members of the Buckley- street choir and congregation joined in the procession to the Cemetery. At the graveside Mr. Barton read the remaining portion of the funeral service, following which the choir sang a hymn. ,The ceremonial rites associated with the Ancient Order of Foresters were conducted by Bro. G. Maybury, and a most impres- sive ceremony was brought to a close by the choir singing another hymn and Mr. Barton pronouncing the Benediction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
