      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>WHAT TO WEAR.</head>
        <p>WHAT TO WEAR.</p>
        <p>When the London season is over, the most dressy class of gowns that many people in the swim wear pretty well all day long are relegated to occasions when parties are given or tea is served at a country house, and for the rest of the day plain tailor-made gowns, linen, or soft wool made short serve for country wear and for tra- velling. Of course, in the more fashionable re- sorts abroad and in Engiand people are dressing all day long; but there is a great art, says the Queen, in wearing effective, simple dresses, A plain white gown with a green yachting cap and scarf has a certain element of smartneal in it, though so simple, and while we may have more magnificence in our gowns in town, the chate- laines as well as the guests should contrive to throw a certain picturesque element into• their garments. A pretty hat with a flowing veil is singularly becoming to most people, though- we have taken very slowly to the pendent veil, which on the Continent last year everybody wore. A very pretty class of coat with short sleeves and hardly more than a brace back and front is improving to a blouse, and most of these have handsome enamelled ornaments on the front, and sometimes a collar of old lace. A din- ner gown of soft batiste muslin, with wide sashes and frills of lace for berthes, is just the thing for a young girl in the country. FROCK.</p>
        <p>CHILD'S SAILOR</p>
        <p>Very neat and practical is the smart little sailor frock which is Illustrated in our sketch. The design is of simple type, and the dress is suitable for little girls of from three to eight 3c ars of age. Serge and flannel are, perhaps, the two most suitable materials for the realisation of this attractive design; but holland, linen, or other washing fabrics may be used if liked. As re- gards colour, that is, of course, largely a matter of individual taste; scarlet, dark blue, and white, however, , are, in my opinion, quite the prettiest colours for the .purpose. The upper part of the</p>
        <p>frock consists ot a loose blouse of the material, with the fulness arranged in two deep tucks on either shoulder. Over this blouse turns back a prettily-shaped collar in some contrasting colour, edged with a few rows of braid, and finished with a knotted handkerchief in front. A small chemisette of the same material as the collar, and embroidered with some suitable design, Oa up the space between the edges of the collar. Little braided cuffs 'finish the simple bishop sleeves, the small gathered skirt being also com- pleted by several rows of braid at the hem. A smart belt of patent leather completes this useful and very pretty frock.</p>
