      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>LABOUR REPRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>It was cause for regret that they had to report decrease rather than increase under this head locally. The seat held by ex-Councillor Wright (whose death the Council deplored) had not been filled by a Labour representative. The Council 6wed much to Councillors Stott and Ball, who have done strenuous work in the Council and in Committee and they could rest assured that their services were appreciated. That also applied to labour representation on the Distress Committee, the Education Authoritiesand Board of Guardians.</p>
        <p>_ Concluding, they appealed to all delegates and through them to the great body of Trades Unionists to be in earnest. Their cause was not an ordinary one, and their day of usefulness was not past. There were millions of workers still unorganised, and the federation of those who were organised was urgently required. With that accomplished their sphere of usefulness would be greatly increased. Their cause was a just one, and if they were true to themselves and their principles, they would do more than formulate and pass resolu- tions. The outlook demanded political action. The reforms they required could only be obtained through such action, and let them 163 trades unionists take their share in the task. _ _</p>
        <p>The report was unanimously adopted, and the Secre- tary was cordially thanked. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <p>The following were elected (Awe for the ensusing year :—President, Mr. W. H. Richardson; vice-presi- dent, Mr. J. Moorfield ; treasurer, Mr. T. Howliston ; secretary, Mr. E. Plinston ; executive committee, Councillor F. Stott, Messrs. T. Plumb, J. Podmore, W. Hickinbottom and J. Haley. A vote of thanks, was accorded to the retiring chair- man (Mr. T. Plumb), on the motion of Councillor STOTT, seconded t)y. Mr. E. PLINSTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>"A CHURCHYARD COUGH." Declared Hopeless, but Cured by VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
        <p>"A CHURCHYARD COUGH." Declared Hopeless, but Cured by VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Churchill. of Dowlish Wake, Ilminster, Somerset, says "Nearly 30 years ago I contracted inflammation of. the lungs which left me Bronchial Asthma of a very severe type, my cough was so bad that people said if ever they heard a churchyard cough,' I had it, and at times the paroxysms were so bad that I could not lie in bed. Three doctors, said I would never get better, but at last I was induced to try Veno's Lightning Cough Cure, and soon found I was on the right track -at last, and now thanks to your extraordinary remedy my asthma is entirely gone,, and I am at work as hard as ever." Veno's Lightning Cough Cure is the purest, -safest. and most reliable remedy extant for coughs and colds, bronchitis, asthma, catarrh, sore throat, hoarseness, old- age coughs, children's coughs, and all chest and lung troubles. Price 911, Is. lid., and 2s. 9d., of all chemist and drug stores emrywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLER'S COMMISSION.</head>
        <p>COMMERCIAL TRAVELLER'S COMMISSION.</p>
        <p>Important Point Decided at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour:fudge Shiress Will, K.C.,George Brown, Ash Cottage, Lymm, seed Messrs. Bradbnry and Co., Ltd,,,, sewing machine and tool manufacturers, Oldham, and Mr. James Howarth. the receiver of such company, for £l7 ss. 7d. balance alleged to be owing for commission as a commercial traveller.—Wr. Browne appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Shimeld, (tessrs. Ashcroft, Maw and Shimeld. Oldham) defended.—Plaintift was engaged by the Compahv under an agreement providing that-he should receive £2 a week and 2 per cent. com- mission on all cash received for tool orders which he obtained; no commission to be paid after the deter- mination of the agreement. He recently left their employ to take up a fresh position, add his case was that the amount claimed was due to hire as com- mission on business which he had influenced. He admitted that he had not taken all the orders direct, but contended that as he had called emu the rarties and introduced them to the firm he was entitled to the commission. in most of the oases—in fact.in 19 out of 20—in which commission was paid, he did not obtain the orders direct.—The defence was that plaintiff was not entitled to commission in some cases because he did not obtain the orders and in others because the money was not received until he had left the flrm's employ and the agreement had been determined.—His Honour held that it was not necessary for the traveller to obtain the order direct. Tf it wine through his influence and good judgment. he ought to he paid commission. Further, he ruled that the reasonable construction to be placed on the agreement was that. plaintiff was not to paid commission on orders given after the determina- tion of the agreement. Tf defendants' contention were rightkat he was not to receive commission on cash paid after the determination of the agreement, it would be to the interests of the emnloTers, after receiving large orders to give the traveller notice and so avoid paying the commission. He gave indernegt for the plaintiff for the amount &amp;aimed with costs.—Mr. Browne asked ' for costs on the higher scale, as the case was • important to commercial travellers, but his Honour declined to allow them.</p>
        <p>Read the small print in the newspaper at night 10 inches from the eye, not farther, one eye at a time. Reic the following 10 feet away, one eye at a time :- TBL N F E I f you cannot do this you should at once consult i OHNST ON WATSON,</p>
        <p>Eyesight and Spectacle Specialist, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON. Dont lose your sight through neglect. Dont lose, your sight through wearing gla,ses for which you were not tested. Few people have eyes alike. Difficult cases and thoce who have never really been, suited wiit glasses ate specially invited to consult JOHNSTON WATSON. 'bow Prices.—Satisfaction Guaranteed.</p>
        <p>84,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL</head>
        <p>TRADES AND LABOUR COUNCIL</p>
        <p>Annual Meeting.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this council was held in the -Independent Labour Party's rooms on Tuesday evening ,Tr. T. Plumb presiding. There was a very good attendance.</p>
        <p>IMMMI</p>
        <p>the nation, and these were continued to their successors. The question demanded their attention by voice and vote.</p>
        <p>UNIFICATION. As was mentioned in last year's report, a ballot had been taken as to the best mode of procedure to bring the Council np-to-date and in touch with the forward movement. Acting upon the mandate given, the Executive Committee of the Council formulated resolu- tions and forwarded them to the affiliated brtnches,with the result that the Council was instructed to proceed as a trade union body only, with powers to take politioal action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE. Correspondence was read from the Manahaster and Salford Women °Trades and Lahonr Council which ran: —ln view of the feeling among certain Trades Unionist against. any partial measure of Womens Suffrage, short Of actual Womanhood Suffrage, we venture to ask you .to consider the following proposed policy. We-believe that there are a great many people who would honestly Wish to further the cause of Women's Enfranchisement _for the sake of the heavy industrial grievances of work- ing women, if they could do so in a manner that they felt to he absolutely democratic. If this represents Your feeling on the matter, we venture to appeal to you to pass a resolution urging the Labour Party to ke action next Session by proposing an amendment to the Address of a kind that would be supported by 'adult suffragists and women snffragists alike, such as the following :—" That the House regrets the omission from the 'Ring's speech of any mention of the claims of %vomen to the franchise." We earnestly request you to send a reqolntion of this kind to the Labour party as soon as pogsible. We hope that all those who have -sympathy with the fierce industrial struggle of their 'women comrades will help them in this way. After much discussion it was decided to consider the -matter next month. MR. A. H. CIROgPTELD M.P.. AND THE UNENTPLOYED BILL. A letter was read from Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., in reference to the Unemployed Bill. He said that the -Rill would have his most. careful consideration. The Member described Mr. McDonald as a man whose -eloquence and ability in the House he fully recognised and Apprec;rited. Mr. Crosfield did not in all matters 'see eye to eye with Mr. McDonald, but he could assure the Council that no little difference of opinion between them—for insta.nce. as regards the authorship of the 'Trades Disputes Bill, in the passing of which the main body of the Progressive Party had so important a share --would prevent him giving careful and unbiassed attention to Mr. McDonald's Bill. !MB FORTITCONITNG DEMONSTRATION. A letter was received from Mr. R. Pierce Williams -informing the council that he had received a postcard from Mr. Pete Curran, M.P. saying that he could not come on November lßth to address an Old-Age Pension meeting in the Parr Hall. Mr. Williams said he that Mr. Stephen Walsh, M.P., had kindly consented to come in Mr. Curren's place. THE ANNUAL REPORT. _</p>
        <p>- - The secretary (Mr. E. PLINSTON) read the thirty second annual report which Stated that they commenced the present year with twenty-six affiliated branches 'Which was the same as last year. Although the Iron- founders Society had withdrawn the Operative 'Plasterers had joined the council. He was glad to report that several societies were in communication with them in reference to the affiliation and he hoped all would work and try to obtain a larger increase of Membership. It was to be regretted that so many changes had taken place last year amongst delegates; there had been no fewer than twenty since last October lie was convinced that their deliberation and work 'would be much more efficient if branches could retain their delegates for longer period. It necessarily, took 'some time for a representative to understood and become interested in the council's work, but in many CRAPS men came and went. so quickly as to make it impossible for beneficial work to be accomplished. TRADE AND DISPUTE. It was satisfactory to report the absence of disputes is the district, work had been, and was fairly good, and the unemployed fewer than for some time past and their sincere hope was that this might continue to be so. One could not, lose sight of the fact that several serious disputes had taken plane during the last few months notably at Belfast, which stood out as an object lesson to prove that further legislation was necessary re Trades Disputes Bill. Military and Police were still Ilse(' as a menace to. and the breaking no of labour forces in times of troubles. They abhored strikes but they ask what other method conld organized labour adopt to obtain and retain improved conditions, When as in the Case of Belfast, employers absolutely miectsd all offers at conciliation. Re hoped that the time would speedily come when employer and emnloyed would realise the dependence of one upon the other and uhen conciliation would replace fiction.</p>
        <p>_ FREE EDUCATION. The council took action against the attemnt to enforce school fees at the Beamont Council School and appealed successfully to the Board of Education to Prevent, same. They were sorry that the resolution to free \all schools in the borough had not been passed. They believed that the State should hear the engt- of both Elementary and -Secondary education. They Proteated succe.sfolly against the Exploityinn of Pauper Labour. Strong action had been takfn by the shop ftssi tan ts. assisted by their council against the iniquitous radius agreement, of, some firms resulting in the withdrawal of many such agreements: OLD-AGE PENSIONS. At the request of the Independent Lahonr Party, the Council had co-opted with that body for the purpose of holding a demonstration. kThe necessity for such action must he potent to each on of them. Trades unionists must have felt disappointed at the inaction of the Government upon this subject. They were told that it was only demanded by idle dirty ne'er-do-wells, who wanted to live on other people, hut they could point to the thousands of the deserving poor and demand redress. It was said that the cost would ,he too great R strain, but there was, no such cry raised when vast 'Millions were voted for war. Pensions and grants were 'Mute without comment to the members of the Govern- Ment upon their enforced retirement.; the Duke's and 'Lords for services sometimes of a doubtful benefit to</p>
        <p>, _ , fr-dr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 12, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 12, 1907.</p>
        <p>authorities found that of 1,522 men out of work only I two were in that position through drink. The lour party was also going to doll next yetr with the CAre of the aged poor. There was NOTHING MORE DISGRACEFUL</p>
        <p>to us than the manner hi which we dealt with the aged poor to-day. It was impossible for the man who received £1 a week and had to support a wife and family to he thrifty. The people who were poor to-day were poor belause of the robbery of the rich who took two-thirds of that which the workers produced. (Applause.) These people were poor from the start and they were kept poor, so that they could not provide for old-age. The present Government, as shown in the Budget, only thought about relieving the poor income tax-payer. Socialists wanted to reverse that order of things and ratke the people who were pving income tax, particularly those with 21.000 a year and up xards, PAY AN INCREASED TAX</p>
        <p>for the purpose of providing the money for old-age pensions. It could be done if the workers were pre- pared to force the hands of the Government. The question could be solved next year if the Government was in earnest, but they had grave doubts whether it was. The Socialist members had resolved that next year in regard to the unemployed and old-age pengions questions, they were going to fight to the last ditch. (Applause). gr. Asquith would not get through his next Budget so easily as he got through his first one, unless he was prepared to put on a tax that would give old-age pensions in a year and a half from now, and unless an attempt was made to solve the unemployed problem. Socialism was not what their opponents represented it to be, and Socialists advocated nothing that would not tend to the benefit of the workingclass community. (Applause). MR3. SNOWDEN'S SPEECH. cams ['UNITY AND SOCIALISM-.</p>
        <p>Mrs. PHILIP SNOWDEN delivered an eloqusnt address. Socialists, she said, were delighted that their enemies were at last coming into the open. The great thing which Socialists in time past had had had to fight, the thing which had been the greatest drawback, had been not opposition but indifference, for indifference Was the foe of every reform. The great mass of the people had been so apathetic that their possessions and their rights had been wrested from them, and but for their apathy it was highly probable that those who desired a better state of affairs would not have been forced on to Socialist platforms. Now they would probably have serious and organized opposition, and she was delighted at the fact. They could wish that their opponents would fight cleanly---(applause)—that they would attack them in the matter of their principles, but they would not do that. That was not the way their opponents fought at Kirkdale. She never was in an election where the opposition played such A _DIRTY AND DISCREDITABLE PART.</p>
        <p>She never felt so proud of being —a Socialist- as she did in that election, for at any rate the Socialists fought in a clean way. (Applause.) Socialists had begun to think they might be constructive, that they might cease to trouble to answer all the absurd criticisms of Socialism, such as the dividing-up business, and the immoral theory. They thought people had 'begun to understand what Socialism really was and that they could go forward with great schemes of social and moral reform. But they were forced. back by those si ho wished to play upon the ignorance and stupidity of those whom they had made stupid and ignorant.- They were bound to answer that it was not necessary in order to be a Socialist, to be either an atheist, or an advocate of free love, a thief or a skunk, nor were they Weary Willies and Tired Tims. (Laughter.) She had found nothing in Socialism which had made it necessary for her to sever her connection with a Christian church. (Applause.) But she had discovered that to make her PROFESSION OF CHRISTIANITY</p>
        <p>suffrage which meant that every man or woman, irrespective of property coalifloation, should on reach- ing a certain age, have the vote. The Inlet:bend mt Labour Party, had. however, agreed that a gooi step towards that would be to establish a condition of things where women should have the vote on the same terms as it was given to men. Alderman BENNETT£I9k33 if Socti-iliarn was hostile to the Empire? Mr. SUM M gRIIELL : Hostile to the E noire ? Certainly not. How can it he? Alderman. BE \MM. : FRS it no v imperial policy ? Mr. SUMMERBELL said Socialists had an Imnerial Policy : they were not ptronhial politioians.. They stood for fair nl iv to the people all round, bezinninl with the peonle of our own country and thei pursuing the same policy with regard to the peorile of other lands. They did not believe in the Tain.rial policy of the Tory or Liberal parties. Th 47 Wieverl in a policy that would re:'-ire to the people of all lands that of which they had liNin robbed for a long time. The meeting then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>SOCIALIST MEETING.</head>
        <p>SOCIALIST MEETING.</p>
        <p>MR. BLATCHF3RD, M. T. SIMMERBELL, M.P., AND MRS. SNOWDEN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Socialism and Christianity.</p>
        <p>A Socialist demonstration promoted by the Warring- ton branch of the Independent Labour Party was held in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. There was a large attendance, Mr. Robert Blatchford, editor of the "Clarion," presiding. The principal speakers were Mr. T. Summerbell, M.P. for Sunderland, arid Mrs. Philip Snowden. Amongstthose present on the platform were Conncillor Stott, Mrs. Wilkinson (member of the Board of Guardians), Messrs. W. Pook (secretary of the Gas Workers' Union), E. Horrocks (secrsetary of the Labour Representation Committee), J. Haley (Distress Com- mittee), E. Reeves (member of the Wiredrawers' executive), J. Podinore, E. Osborne,W. Pierce Williams, C. Lee, Bagot (Mara) and J. Hampson (secretary of the Right to Work Committee). The proceedings began with the singing of the hymn "Sons of Labour," Mr, W: Pearce being at the oigan. THE CHAIRMAN'S UNBELIEF&amp;</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said he hoped Warrington people would be able to fight as well as they could sing, because all the indications at present pointed to the fact that before long they would have to try. There was at present an organised attack being made upon Socialism all over the country. It would he a very serious attack conducted by the honourable Tory Press and their methods would not be scrupulous. They would attack Socialists publicly and privately. They would attack their characters and beliefs and mis- represent both, as they always did. They had a taste of the quality of the opposition in London last Nlarch. At that time they were particularly concerned about the burden of the rates. They shrieked and howled about it, but they never said a word about the burden of the rents. (Hear, hear.) It was rates, rates, rates all the time. As Fred Bramley said, they seemed to be anxious about every Possible kind of rate except the death rate. They won that time and they had got the rates in their care. Now they were anxious about the sanctity of the home; they were writing columns every day about it. They did not seem to be aware of the fact that a very large proportion of the British people had not got any homes, but simply lived in hovels. (Applause.) They were anxious about the sanctity of the one room tenement where a whole family pigged together in order to pay exhorbitant rent to these honourable persons. They were anxious also to secure to their half-starved and over-worked sister the sacred right to make a free bargain with the sweater. They were anxious to point out to the twelve millions of free British citizens who existed always upon the verge of starvation that if the wicked Socialists got their way they would take their wealth from them. (Laughter.) ( r. Blatch fo rd) supposed it was not necessary for him to tell his hearers that Socialism was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>NOT A THIEF BUT A POLICEMAN</head>
        <p>NOT A THIEF BUT A POLICEMAN</p>
        <p>and that their object was not to rob anybody of his wealth, but to prevent the rich from robbing the poor. (Applause). Socialists wished to prevent the strong from making slaves of the weak. They believed with William Morris that no man was good enough to be another man's master; and they believed with John Ruskin that whether there be one God or three, no God or 10,000, children should be fed and their bodies kept clean. (Applause). If the audience had not heard pretty often that he (Mr. Blatchford) was a very wicked man, they would be likely to hear it a good many times in the near future. (Laughter.) Their opponents said he was a very wicked man who did not believe what he was told. Well, he did not believe in many things. He did not believe in the House of Lords for Instance --(laughter)—and, what was even worse, be did not believe in the House of Commons. (Laughter.) He did not believe in the Liberal party nor in the Tory party, and he ditl not believe in tariff reform. (Laughter.) What he believed in was Socialism. (Applause). He had believed in it for twenty years, and be did not think be believed in much else. He did not believe until they got Socialism that they would ever get anything that was worth having. (Applause).</p>
        <p>MR. SUMMERBELL, M.P., AND THE UNEMPLOYED. A WARNING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <p>square with her practice of it, she could not escape from becoming a Socialist. (Applause.) At present the great mass of the people could not he Christians if they wanted to be. She discovered that she dare not in these days advocate universal sobriety. She dare not advocate universal thrift. She discovered that the sober man did not neoessarily get on because he was sober; but because the other fellow drank. If all the men in the world were sober and the women refused to drink, they would not all get work under the present system. (Applause and a Voice: "I would like to see it tried.") She was not an advocate of drink and she contended that every individual who wanted social reform to go for- ward should not hesitate to speak aginst thE ill-effects of drink. They could do anything, perhaps, with a sober democracy, and the great hope of getting imnple sober would be that they might persuade them that Socialism, was a good thing. The thrifty workers deprived their wives and children and themselves of the beauties of intellectual life in order to save money. "Why have some of us " continued Mrs. Snowden "ever said anything which appears to be anti-Chris- tian? There are some of us of course, who do not believe in Christianity, and I want to point out the reason for that. All men and women cannot believe equally easily. They have not been gifted with that faculty. I contend that the man who denies God with his lips because he sincerely cannot reach out to Him, but preaches God and the Kingdom of Heaven in his life has infinitely more of the spirit of true religion within him than he who spreads forth (the remainder of the sentence was lost in applause.) lam proud to be on the same platform with Mr. Blatchford, because no man or woman can say anything about the conduct of Mr. Blatchford—(applause)—a man who stands by the poor, suffering and weak where- ever they are, a man who has given the best of his energies and his life to uplifting the common people. Are we going to quarrel with him because he thinks differently from us? (" No.") We say that nearly 2,000 years of supposed Christianity do not seem to have made much effect and some of us, perhaps, have got i into the way of thinking that Christianity sat fault. Others of us say that REAL CHRISTIANITY</p>
        <p>Mr. T. SUMMRRBELL, M.P., said he believed people were more sympathetic and enthusiastic about Socialism than they had ever been in this country before, in spite of the misrepresentations that had taken place in the party Press. A certain Marquis had been saying that the Socialists were going to confiscate all property and money, that they were actually going to take the funds of trade unions, friendly societies, and other organiza- tions and share them out. Such nonsense as that would not appeal to the working classes, as was evidenced by the large numbers who were joining the Socialist move- ment. "If you have got £10,000," said Mr. Summerbell, "we don't want to touch a penny of it. If you have got a street of houses we are not going to confiscate them. You can keep them until sucit time as the State or the municipality may arrivd at the conclusion that it is necessary to take them and they will pay you a fair and legitimate price. With regard to the £lO,OOO the position we take up is this. You have got to keep it and live upon it. We are not going to permit you to take your £lO,OOO and put it into an undertaking and make slaves of the men who may be employed there. (Applause). If you want to increase it you will have to turn to and do a bit of honest work." (Applause). Continuing Mr. Summerbell said Socialists were charged with being irreligious, but they were nothing of the kind. For over 1900 years we had been saying the Lord's Prayer but what effort had been made to bring about Christ's kingdom upon earth? Was there not too much of saying the Lord's Prayer without any attempt at its realization? Was there not too much of the</p>
        <p>has never yet been seriously tried. (Applause). We want to stir the imaginations, hearts and minds of the Christian people and teach them that it is not singing hymns and praying from comfortable church pews that is going to save the world, but it is by trying in every possible way, by voice, pen and vote, that we are going • to bring about that condition of things for which we pray every day of our lives. (Applause.) No Christian has a right to criticise Socialists who has not done everything he can to remove the slum landlord from his property, or remove the property from the sum landlord, who has not raised his voice at every opportunity against the public house so temptingly displayed to catch the people and take them in, who has not pronounced that the people ought to have the land which in their own Text Book we are told was given to all the children of men, who has not protested in season and out of season against a system which sends women into the streets to get their hying. . No Christian who has not done these things has the least right to criticise Socialists." (Applause.) Mrs. Snowden made a powerful appeal for the granting of votes to women which, she said, was a matter of politics to a certain extent, of economics to a far greater extent,and of morals to a deeper extent still. They wanted a condition of comradeship between men and women and a condition of brotherhood for all men and women. They.did not want one man to batten on the dishonour and degradation of a thousand men. Socialism was simply an extension of the principle already in operation that Society had the right to interfere by legislation when the great mass of the people were suffering an injustice. The community could best know what its own needs were and could best use whatever it possessed to supply those needs. It was A WRONG STATE OF THINGS when a man had to depend upon another for the bread that he ate. It was a wicked state of things when 1,000 women were struggling against each other for a paltry job at 2s. 6d. a week. It was a wrong state of things when hundreds and thousands of little children. had to go to school without sufficient food. It was a wrong state of society altogether and she appealed to all present. Christian or non-Christian—but particularly if they were Christians—that it was their duty to use every faculty they possessed in order to uplift the great nIP</p>
        <p>ONE DAY IN SEVEN RELIGION? Socialists said no effort had been made by the people connected with the religions movement of to-day to bring about Christ's Kingdom upon this earth. Socialists wanted it to be brought about and unless religions people were prepared to make a stronger effort they would have to stand the criticism, so far as their religion was concerned. Socialism had come to stay. It was growing and nothing was going to keep it back. It was the only" ism" that was likely to do any good for the workers. He was more than ever convinced that neither the Liberal nor the Tory party in the House of Commons would do anything so far as the economic emancipation of the workingmen was con- cerned. The unemployed were increasing month by month. Twelve million people were always on the border of starvation and twenty millions were always poor. He defied any working man, no matter what his political opinions were, to prove that TIIE UNENIPLOYED PROBLEM would get any better if industry were run as it was at the nresent time. tt absolutely could not. Over 20 million acres of land were lying uncultivated in this country, waiting for the labour of the unemployed to produce the food for the starving people. The House of Commons could not face that simple problem because whenever the Lind question was discussed the sole concern of the party politician appeared to be that his rights in regard to the preservation of pheasants should have firs:. consideration. The only way to face the problem was by the people deciding to use the machinery of Parliament to see that the land was brought into cultivation. The land owners said Socialists were going to confiscate. The gentlemen in possession to-day ought to be the last in the world to talk about confisca- tion, because practically all the land held by some of tbe wealthiest families of the country was confiscated in byegone generations. Socialists were</p>
        <p>.. . . NOT GOING TO BE CONFISCATORS. I mass of the people. They hoped to bring about aeon- They were prepared to pay a legitimate and fair price ! dition not of rigid, hard equality, because' they never with a view to bringing the land into cultivation and; could have that, but equality of opportunity so that to ultimately restoring it to the people. They each individual might develop within himself all the asserted that whether a child was born in the slum or perfections of character of which he was capable. in the mansion, the right of that child was to live upon (kpplaase.) They *anted more of the spirit of ,the the earth. (Applause.) At present the wants and Crucified Christ applied to their daily occupations and reqnirements of the people did not count at all. It ' their conditions of living, that spirit of self-sacrifice, of was always a question of profit. The unemployed . losing His life for the common life, of losing Himself problem was the most serious question we had to face . for the common good. (Applause.) . , at present, and as one who had been sent to Parliament 1 " ' QUESTIONS. , by an industrial constituency he said as a warning to , the people who were occupying high and comfortable ! ALDERMAN BENNETT AND ENIPIRE. places in the land, that if they were not prepared to 1 Questions were invited and a gentleman in the gallery heed this question sonAthing more serious might take who declared he was a Socialist and wanted to act the place than any of them wanted to see. If he were part of a candid friend, asked if Socialism and all its unemployed to-morrow and he had his wife and children bearings could not be achieved without all the means of and his Old mother at home starving he would : production,distribution and exchange being Rationalised? DEFY EVERY LAW IN THE LAND. ! Mr. SIIMMERBELL said it could not posipily be so. (Applause.) His feeling on the matter was beginning to ' They`did not say theyswere going to carrY out all they be the f