      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>NATURE'S WAY TO THE LUNGS.</head>
        <p>NATURE'S WAY TO THE LUNGS.</p>
        <p>Whera Coughs and Colds Start.</p>
        <p>Wonderful Medicine in Solid Tablet Form.</p>
        <p>The day has ago long passed when people had to depend on drug-laden cough liquids taken into the stomach to treat that nasty cough, or that unpleasant tightness of the chest. Throat and lung troubles have their origin in the organs we breathe with,and these have no con- nection whatever with the stomach, which is where all liquid medicines go. Hence, while temporary benefit may appear to come from the swallowing of mixtures of opium, chloral, and other dangereous and useless drugs, it is only temporary, and the ultimate effect Of these drug-laden liquids is to aggravate the very ailments they set out to care. Science has now furnished the means for an absolute and safe cure for throat and lung troubles in a wonderful medicine. compressed into solid tablet form and known by the short distinctive title, Peps. This wonderful medicine is based on the Unique principle of locking up in handy tablets a certain volatile'agent, which is' sealed and preserved in a silver-paper wrapping till required. This sooth- ing and healing agent (directly a tablet is removed from its covering and crushed in the mouth or merely dissolved on the tongue), passes in form of a vapour dcwn the throat, and, by way of the delicate bronchial tubes, into the lungs. As.the fumes pass over the sore or inflamed membranes of the throat, these membranes are soothed and strengthened, and that huskiness so common during this changeable weather is dis- pelled. Disease-proiuping germs are destroyed and the breathing tubes onward to the lungs are also cleared and reliev .d. Phlegm is loosened and that troublesome sensation, as if you want to cough something up, is ended. Peps carry their wonderful work of healing to the furthermost corner of the lungs, far beyond the reach of any known liquid medicine ; and any threatening chest trouble, the result perhaps of , toiling or dwelling in a close or dust-laden atmos- phere, is cut short. The Peps fumes come into direct contact with the air-passages into actual touch with the inflamed, irritated membranes.</p>
        <p>The value of this powerful soothing, germ- killing and healing agent, which is found only in Peps cannot be over-estimated. Peps unlike any cheap lozenge or opium-laden liquid medicine, will absolutely cure chronic bronr;hitis which has got worse year by year, and end the sleep- destroying coughs of the aged. A. box of Peps kept by the bedside is a comfort and a cure in one ; and chronic sufferers will act wisely if they always keep Peps handy while out-of-doors, and are never without a supply in their homes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <p>Peps differ entirely from ordinary shop lozenges. Victims of throat and lung trouble cannot be too strongly warned against such cheap and useless substitutes,which are often merely crude chemicals gummed together with bad glue, and loaded with opiurn,mophia,laudanum,chloral or other narcotics, which only aggravate the evil and injure th. 3 nerves. Laudanum is often present in liquid cough mixtures, which therefore, besides failing to reach the seat of throat and chest disease, inflict real injury on the system. • Peps are universally approved and singularly successful because they contain no drowsy or harmful drug ; and because they follow Nature's way to the lungs for the safe and certain cure of all, throat. bronchial and lung troubles. Of all • chemists, in handy boxes at is. 11d. and 2s. 9d. Printed on every genuine box, and in raised letters on every tablet is the short, distinctive 'title —Peps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMI.MANCE ASSOGIATION.</head>
        <p>BRITISH WOMEN'S TEMI.MANCE ASSOGIATION.</p>
        <p>The Influence of Environment.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the British Women's Temperance Association was held on Tuesday at the Winrnarleigh Cafe, Miss Bessie Dntton in the chair. An address was delivered by Mrs. Shilston Watkins in which she stated that there had been through the ages a vague idea of- the workings of heredity. She however thought the characteristic features passed down from generation to generation. The sins of the fathers were visited on children to the third and fourth generation. It remained for the end of the last century to really realize what was bound up in the word "heredity." At first a pessimistic idea arose, as it was thought useless to try to fight against it. But presently the thought arose that not only was the evil inherited but the good was also, and there was thus an opportunity of checking the evil and moulding the inherited forces of each individual. There was also another force, that of environment, which moulded and formed the child's character. The longer she lived the more she was inclined to increase the amount of influence of environment in the life of the individual. She was inclined to beiieve that environment, rightly used, counteracted the evil force of heredity, and especially in the case of intemperance. Their association had to face the question of intemperance, and how best to combat it. It had touched every family in the nation. They could do little as a nation or as individuals on the heredity side, though she believed that in the days t.o come idea would pay just as much attention, care and thought in the breeding of human beings, as they did of horses, dogs, and other animals. She believed the new culture of this century would be the culture of human beings. If they could not alter heredity they could alter environment. She had an idea that the most powerful thing in their environment, and, especially in that of young children, was the thoughts of the individuals with whom they lived and came in contact. Her message that afternoon to them was' Think truly, think strongly." One of the objects of the British Women's Wemperance Association was to purify the thoughts of the people of this country. Another was the prevention of the sale of strong drink to children, which was detrimental to the moral well- being of the whole community. She hoped to be alive when the time would come that alcohol would be excluded from the sick-room. She always exoluded it from her own sick-room. She asked those who were not members of the Association to join, and increase influence by increasing their knowledge. Begin at once to try to induce others to join them in the work. (Applause.) Miss DUTTON said they had been delighted with Mrs. Shilston-Watkin's address, and she bad given them much to think of. People required something to think of during their idle moments. She (Miss Dutton) was of the opinion that environment was stronger than heredity. Heredity could be conquered by environ- ment, and the work of that association W3s calculated to improve environment. Mrs. OWEN, president of the local branch, proposed a vote qf thanks to the speaker,and said her words would help`thera on with their work daring the coming winter. Mrs. FYLDES seconded, and the motion was heartily carried. The musical portion of the afternoon was provided by Miss Griffiths who delighted the audience with her rendering of "0 Divine Redeemer "and the "Moorish Maid" and Mrs. Jackson Holmes who ably discharged the duties of accompanist and gave a pianoforte solo.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>Theft from a Pawnshop.</head>
        <p>Theft from a Pawnshop.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Court on Monday, Prank Dooley, Litherland's-yard, Latchford, pleaded not guilty to stealing a man's Cardigan jacket, value 2s. 11d., from the pawnshop of Thomas Domville, Knutsford-road, on Saturday. night. —The Chief Constable said the ;prisoner was seen at about 5.30 on Saturday evcning by two independent witnesses, endeavouring to cut a gardigan jacket; down. Eventually he did cut it off, and go down an entry with it, and was lost sigtht of. Information was given to the police, and prisoner was arrested 'at his house. No trace could be found of the jacket.—Evidence having been given, prisoner denied taking the. jacket but he was fined 10s. and costs 11s). .</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at the harvest thanks- giving services at this church on Sunday. The choir acquitted themselves admirably in the special music. In the morning the anthem was" Ye shall dwell in the land "(Stainer), the solo being creditably taken by Mr. C, H. Bate. In the evening Maunder's "Sing to the Lord of harvest " was chosen, and on this occasion the soloist was Miss Kershaw. Mr. Frank Hickman presided at the organ. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. W. Mellor, and in the evening the Rev. T. Miller, .the newly-appointed minister to the Warrington circuit. Mr. Miller took for his text the words," He healeth the broken in heart and bindeth up their wounds. He telleth the number of the stars. He calleth them all by their names." The speaker said he wondered whether people had any definite conception of God. He did not mean a vague, dim sense of some far-off deity, but some one who was very real and very near to them. When they read of the wonderful facts connected with this universe—when thsy read that the sun was 93,000000 miles from the earth and that it was much 'larger that this globe; when they grasped tbe fact that I there were millions of other subs, each as large as ours, land each with systems of world revolving around them —they felt what a wonderful Being God was. Some people were quite ready to believe in God as the Maker of the universe. They believed in an Almighty Power which kept the stars in their courses,which counted and called them by names, but they would not admit the presence of a Loving Friend and a Heavenly Father, who took a real and living interest in their lives, who sympathised with them in their sorrow, and who would willingly share their joys. Yet if they believed in en All-powerful Creator, why not in a loving Heavenly Father? If in a great Architect of the Universe, why not in one who healed up the broken in heart? If God cared so much for the stars, would He not care for them ? If He provided for the sparrows, would He not provide for them?</p>
        <p>SOLD'EIV ER? 1 V HERE at le. 14d.. 2s. 9,1.,4a.6&amp;, and //s Obridcie LUNG TONIC, WORLD'S 77.• FOR COUGEIS, COLDS, BRONCHITIS. ASTHMA, Etc</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>TETE PARTSII CHUftria.</p>
        <p>The thanksgiving services were nintinned nn Sprl .v when there were large congregatinns. The nether in the mnrning mac the itay. Ste.rensnn, who defended harvest festivals frnal et.acks which had been lately rnade on them, gni eintendei that it %,,as their duty in this way to shsnk Gni for -lis mernies. The nnlnit Iraq occupied in the awning by the RPV. .T. Taherwoni, vicar of Bp.:wick. Shremshurv. and for -nerly of Warrington. where his parents still reside. &amp; very creditable rendering, of Beethoven's "ETelleluiah Chnrns" was given by the choir, \Tr. F. It Darby, Nins. presiding at the organ. The offertories, which were in aid of the elergr fund, realized £35 12s. -</p>
