      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>T. PAUL'S CEIIIRCFI.</head>
        <p>T. PAUL'S CEIIIRCFI.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at the closing services of the harvest festival, at this Chnrch on Smulay. The preacher in the morning was the Rev. G. Peters. vicar of St. Thotnas's,Wavertree. in the afternoon a children's service and milted Rible, Class sgrvice v7era held, the preachers being resnectively the Vicar and the Rey., E. R. Tarbuck. of Altrincham..VhO also preached in the evening to a large congregation, many of the visitors having to he accommodated with swats in +beside aisles. Special music was effectively rendered. The collections at these services and at that, held on the nrevions Wednesday evening amounted to .C4l, and are in aid of the renovation fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CAIRO-STR E; El' CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STR E; El' CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. E. Willinms, who is snpplving the nulpit during the absence of the pastor, the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, who is touting in America for the benefit of his health, and who has also taken advantnee of the opportunity which has thus presented itself of attend- ing the religious Free Church Conference at Boston, oondneted the harvest thanksgiving services at this chapel on Snnday. The decorations had been artisti- cally carried out by various lady members of the con- gregation end were much admired. Special music was rendered by the choir, Mr. Muir presiding at the organ. The preacher, ;n his sermon in the evening, alluded to the recent, bonntifol harvest, and asked the congrega- tion not to forget the Great Giver. The harvest was a gift from the Creator and was not the product of our endeavour. The collections were in aid of the War- rington and District Nursing Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE-STREET BAPTIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>There were large congregations at this church on ; Sunday on the occasion of the harvest festival. The ' preacher morning and evening was the Rev. R. E. Letheren. of Northants. At the evening service Miss Linn. Plinston rendered " Lead Thou me on "in credit- able style. In the afternoon a service of song entitled "The Joyful Harve.ters " was sweetly rendered hy an augmented choir. The Mayor had kindly consented to be present and glve an address on the good lessons which could be derived from the harvest, and imparted some valuable advice to the children. Re said that on many occasions when he had been pass- ing fields rine with golden grain he had seen children thoughtlessly pluck the wheat, carry it away with them and afterwards throw it away in the lanes on their way home. But that was wrong, as the farmer had had to wait 12 months for his harvest, and it did not show gratitude to God for his mercies by wilfully destroying wheat intended for the food of man. It was their duty, when they saw children committing wilful damage, to stop it by all means that lay in their power. The collec-. tions at all the services were in aid of the church funds BOLD-STREET WESLEYAN CEIURCH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>aNDIANUEL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>aNDIANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>. The harvest thanksgiving services were held on ' Sunday last at the aboye Church, the Rev. Frank Vaughan being the preacher There were good con- gregations. Special music was rendered by the choir, the anthems were "0 Praise the Lord" and ," God is Love." The morning sermon was based upon Psalm lxv. 11. "Thou crownest the year with Thy goodness." The preacher showed how every material thing is but a picture of a spiritual. Whether we gazenpon a harvest field or the simplest flower, we are faee to face with the incoming of God. He is most clearly revealed in the commonplaces of everyday life ; it is to the simple soul that the Great Gad makes Himself felt. The harvest which had just been gathered in is a return of earls, hopes and expectations which in the spring were Divine prophecies which the services of to-day show have been fulfilled because God's waves and " God's paths have dropped fatness " and if the hope of a natural harvest is God implanted, is it not a fact that the truest hope of our truest self shall likewise be fulfilled ? Every longing for a better life, every desire to know more of God is a good seed containing a germ of Divine life and surely the harvest will come. Speak- ing again in the evening from Ruth ii. 14 " At mealtime come thou hither and eat of the bread and dip thy morsel into the vinegar" the rev?.rand gentleman said "That not to philosophers and scientists as such does God reveal the secrets of His love but often to those following the lowliest occupation does He make Himself known. A harvest thanksgiving service is a special public demonstration of our thankful- ness that God is continually bringing order out of chaos, continually clothing bar renness with beauty, and although in the spiritual life when the plough goes thruugh our hearts it is difficult to see that coming beauty, yet surely we learn from the natural hsrvest that God is behind the scenes, revealing though unseen His gracious kinship, His heart of love, His sincerest affection ; calling His children to draw near that Great Father heart, and find fullest joy and blessing in so doing. The Gospel Message sounds clearly to those who like Ruth are outsiders who have no right to any privilege ; those who hsde no+hing to commend them ; those who are feeling the burden of poverty and loss. To such as these comes God's invitation to share His special meal-time, to enter into fullest fellowship and friendship, and highest association of privilege and blessing. Jesus Christ Himself companies with His labourers, and still moves about His cornfields. Let us see to it that we are not too busy with the sheaves to hear His voice. Let not the life become so tense as to crowd out our times of meditation and communion. " At meal-time come thou hither and eat of the bread and dip thy morsel into the vinegar."</p>
