        <p>Really it is impossible not THE NEXT to admire the energy of ELECTION, some of the rank and file</p>
        <p>of the Unionist Party, who, seeing that their titular leaders are making no headway, are hard at work manufacturing a new issue for the next election. Nothing is more certain than that the people who are most delighted with the strenuous efforts which are:being made by a section of the Unionist Press to frighten the electors with the bogey of Socialism are the Socialists themselves. Never before have they had such an advertisement. To wake up and find themselves taken seriously is a new experience for them, and if Socialism is made] a living problem within the next few years it willibe due, not to the Socialists, but to those who are now raising ' the bogey for party purposes. To the serious student of politics the attempt to label the whole of the supporters of the present Governmen t as Socialists is ridiculous in the extreme. Those who are doing so must be ignorant of the principles of both Socialism and Liberalism, or, which is far more likely, they are presuming on the ignorance of the British public. Now we make bold to say that the greatest defence against Socialism, properly so called, which this country possesses is the Liberal Party. That makes no difference. Liberals are to be called Socialists and to be accused of predatory designs upon everybody's belongings, of a desire to abolish the family life, morality, and even religion itself. These things are to be preached between now and the General Election at street corners by means of paid agitators; and strong speeches are to be ground out by gramophones—which, by the way, are useful in the modern electioneering because they are not expected to answer awkward questions from inquiring electors. Money is being collected already for these purposes, and is cheer- fully given, not because the givers have any real fear that Socialism has to be fought, but because they imagine by this means to get a Government in power which will tax the necessaries of life.</p>
        <p>It has been obvious for some time that the old electioneering was out of date, but these new methods are a source of moral danger because of the unscrupulous character of the directing hands. In the old days the two party organisations looked after the registration, kept their lists up to date, and just about three weeks before the election opened a committee-room and started active propaganda. The new electioneering is continuous. The Labour Party has shown the way. Without newspapers and without money they have done their propaganda work at street corners and in the parks. What is more, they do not give up as soon as an election is over, but go on putting their views before the people. It is that lesson which the Unionists have learnt, and that is why they are organising vans and gramophones and that sort of thing for their campaign against Socialism. But Liberals will make a huge mistake if they allow the issue at the next election to be chosen by these gramophone experts. Liberals stand for social reform, not for impossible Socialistic theory, and the greatest enemy of social reform is the House of Lords. It is that House which blocks the way, and the practical problem for the electors who desire to see progress made is so to limit their powers that the will of the people shall pre- vail. It is this lesson which should be preached at street corners by convinced Liberals, who will be wise to point out that the very people who are now. organising a campaign to fight an enemy which is of their own creation are the individuals who attempted before the last election to foist Tarifl Reform on the country. They found money for that because it meant taxing the poor for the benefit of the rich. That frontal attack failed, and the same people are now trying a "turning move- ment." If Liberals only do their duty that will fail also.</p>
        <p>Indications point at present THE ELECTIONS, to only one municipal con- test in Warrington on November Ist. Councillor Wilding, one of the Non-Trading representatives of Town Hall Ward, is being opposed by Mr. W. Rogers an ex-member of the Council who was rejected by Bewsuy Ward in 1904. On that occasion Mr. Rogers came out as an "independent." Prior to that</p>
        <p>he had been a Conservative • and now he is Conservative once again. We do not anticipate he will have any better chance of success this year than he had when Councillor Skelland beat him so easily. It goes against Mr. Rogers that he is a publican, even though Manchester, Bolton and Stockport are appointing members of the " trade " as their 'respective Mayors and even though Warrington's ' Mayor of a year ago was a brAwer. The "trade" has too great an influence in Warrington, and its representation on the Town Council is more than adequate already. We hope to see Councillor Wilding retutned by a good mkijority. He is an industrious member and much more independent in his attitude than we could imagine Mr. Rogers being. Fairfield Weed has, we understand,acquired Mr. J. T. Locker J.P. unless someone springs a surprise contest on</p>
        <p>Councillor Duncan's popular successor. Rumour couples the names of Mr. J. Singleton and Mr. J. Maginn, J.P. with Howley arid St. John's Wards respectively, but up-to-date there is only one con- test probable—that in Town Hall Ward.</p>
        <p>We hope the appeal signed THE LAWSON by so many members of the CONTRACT. Town Council will influence Mr. Hill, the arbitrator in the case of Lawson v. the Corporation to decide in favour of admitting the Press to the hearing in London which opens on Monday. Ever since the contracts in conne3tion with :he extensions at the Water Works were let, difficulties seem to have arilen and they have not been improved by the secretive policy of the Water Committee. There has never been a straightforward story of the trouble between the Corporation and Mr. bawson. A special and voluminous report was prepared by Mr. Garside, an expert on water engineering, but it has never seen the light of day, though we should say every member of the Town Council has an unquestionable right to peruse it. We look to the arbitrator to throw light into several dark places, for in addition to the interests of Mr. Lawson, the general public have much at stake in this matter.</p>
        <p>There is no mistaking the THE gravity of the position in RAILWAY CRISIS, the railway world. The directors declare that they will not recognise the men's union, the members of the union say that they will force recognition. Warrington, in constuon with all other industrial centres, will watch the dispute with anxiety. Judging from the tone of the men's meeting in the Co-operative Hall on Sunday there is a determination to press their claims to the utmost, and a resolution approving the at:tion of the committee in deciding upon a ballot was passed with enthusiasm. It was impossible to take excep- tion to the tone of the powerful speech delivered by Mr. J. G. Wardle, M.P. for he emphasised the seriousness of an industrial conflict and the fact that such a struggle should never be lightly undertaken. His recital of the men's griev- ances, however, shows that he feels very keenly on the question, and he appealed to the men with earnestness and vigour to prove that they were in earnest. The critical stage is approaching and the question of the moment is whether matters will be pushed to an extremity and the notices of the railwaymen handed in. That the ballot cf the men now pro- ceeding will shew a large maj -may in favour of handing in their notices cannot be doubted, and the problem then will be whether Mr. Bell, havaig the result of the ballot to support him, can so far frighten the companies as to compel recognition before the notices are sent. Meanwhile the directors sit tight. It is to be hoped that long before the strike stage has :been reached some method of avoiding a stoppage will have been found. Almost every industry in the country would be seriously affected, and we see that, in the event of the com- panies refusing to meet Mr. Bell to discuss what recognition means, there is to be a joint meeting of the Trades Union Congress Parliamentary Com- mittee, the Labour Party, and the General Federa- tion of Trades Unions, "to consider what action is necessary." That will add an element of strength to the position of the railway servants, for it is manifest that the trade union thought of the country cannot allow Mr. Bell's union to be smashed. Indeed, it is rather late in the day to talk about smashing trade unions, and probably the directors realise that as much as anyone else. But they see a movement behind for increased wages and improved conditions; and that is, of course, what they are really fighting, believing, as they do, that the shareholders cannot afford to give way to it. If they proved that, public opinion would no doubt support them; but public opinion is certainly not with them in their refusal to recognise the right of the man to be represented by their union officials. Trade unions have to be recognised in other industries, and the general public refuse to see why they should be denied recognition in thib.</p>
        <p>On page 4of the recently WAGES AND PItiCES issued Consular Report on IN THE UNITED the Trade of the United STATES. States (Cd. 3283-137) it is stated that "in 1905, which are the latest statistics available, the wages per hour were 18.9 per cent, higher than the average of the ten-year period from 1890 to 1899 inclusive. The average earcings per week in 1905 were 14 per cent. higher than the average earnings per week during the ten years from 1890 to 1899, and the average hours of labour per week were 4 1 per cent. lower." , This will not improbably be quoted by Tariff Reformers in support of their contention that Protection brings with it high wages. It may, therefore, be well to place side by side with it another statement, which appears on page 62 of the same Report, to the effect that "business is likely to be carried on in a more cautious manner than has been the case hitherto. High prices, to a certain extent, check consumption. There is a large class of both producers and consumers who can no longer fill their requirements on account of the increased cost. This, in time. will mean that consumption is restricted, and the demand for commodities twill fall off while production con tinues. There are signs that a time of over-supply is approaching, which in due course will mean a reduction of prices. It is true that the average increase of wages during the last ten years has been 20 per cent., but the increase in the price of commodities has b3en 40 per cent. The cost of living of many has, in consequence, gone beyond their means of obtaining income. The limit of high prices appears to have been almost reached. and the cost of living has not been surpassed during the last twenty years."</p>
        <p>The attack on the Hon. THE PROBLEM OF Stephen Coleridge's evidence VIVISECTION. before the Royal Com- mission by a member of the British Union for the Abolition of Vivisection reveals one of those splits which, unfortunately, so often make their appearance among reformers of all sorts. The National Anti-Vivisection Society, to which Mr. Coleridge belongs, would permit vivisection only in the presence of an inspector whose reputation for humanity shall be the ground for his selection. This, of course, does not suit those people who believe vivi- section to be essentially immoral; but, leaving them aside, everyone will realise the truth of the remark by Miss Beatrice Kidd, the spokesman of the "abolitionists," that "Humanity is one of the virtues always assumed until dis- proved." Probably the public are not yet col.- verted to the view that vivisection is either useless or necessarily wicked, although everyone would be on!y too glad to prohibit the infliction of ' unnecessary nein. There can be very little to be said really for the performance of the same experiment over and over again merely to let students see with therr eyes the behaviour of this or that organ under such and such conditions. Vivisection surely ought to be restricted to original research, and then, provided some really useful purpose is served, even if pain must be inflicted not many people would complain. It would, therefore, seem that the 0011103012-sense way of dealing with the problem would be to make it obligatory to obtain a licence for every experi- ment, which must thou be conducted in the presence of an inspector. The licence should be subject to any conditions considered necessary, and should be refused except when the end seems to justify the means. The power in whose hands the granting or refusal of a licence should rest is the most difficult matter to decide. Unfor- tunately, althoUgh medical men are undeniably best able to judge of the usefulness of their experi- ments, their profession tends to make them used to the sight of pain, and they necessarily become to some extent callous. On the other hand, the authority must not lie in the hands of people who would view the matter from the other standpoint exclusively. Whatever the authority, it should be to some extent amenable to the pressure of public opinion, and not only its decisions but its reasons for arriving at them should be made public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Somervell told a good story at the Leeds Pro- fessional Musicians' dinner on Tuesday night. A. flautist, he said, played so badly as to incur the wrath of the oonductor who had engaged him. "I thought you could play the flute," exclaimed the conductor. "And so did I,"replied the man; "it's a suck for no both ! "</p>
        <p>A speaker at a Warrington meeting this week had discoursed at some length and rather drearily. When he resumed his seat the Chairman, no doubt quite uncons2iou.sly but not without a touch of appropriate- ness, said " We are now to have a song by--to cheer us up a bit !"</p>
        <p>Warrington has been p-rett.v- well represented at the Hague this week. Mr. Crosfield, the Borough member, was over on a short visit, and Lord and Lady Acton have been staying:there for sometime owing to the former's official duties. Mr. and Mrs. Lyon of Appleton Hall (Lady Acton's parents) are also on a visit to the Hague. .</p>
        <p>* * * Our tariff laws are responsible for the swollen fortunes that have resulted from the monopoly in production and transportation, and the whole condition has grown up under the fostering protection of our tariff laws since the War of Rebellion. Those who have got protection, and now have the swollen fortunes, have since the war paid the campaign expenses of the Republican party upon condition that they should have such protection.—" New York Age." * * * the discussion at the meeting of the London County Council', on the question of the suggested aboli- tion of school attendance medals, a member told a story of a boy who refused to go to his father's funeral because it would mean the loss of his medal, while another member said that last week a boy broke his leg in the playground, and lost his medal on the ground that he had not given two days' notice of his intending absence. All the same, the Council declined to approve the proposal to abolish the giving of medals.</p>
        <p>* * * At the Warrington County Court on Thursday a motorist was mulcted in datra,es and costs to the extent of 11 13s. as the result of running over a prize dog at Lymm. There was rather a conflict of evidence and it was not clearly proved that defendant had been negligent in his driving. The Judge's sympathies were all on the side of the dogs and the motorist had to, pay. His Worship appeared to hold the opinion that dogs had a right to run across the road whenever they liked. If they rank equally with pedestrians in the eyes of the law, then the motorist will have a new terror to contend with.</p>
        <p>In his speech at New -York, General Booth, after hinting at his coming retirement, stated that he intended to devote his remaining years to the. establishment of a University of Humanity. "The present Universities," said the General, 'do not teach undogmatised religion nor the alleviation of suffering and the miseries of the people. I wish to reclaim the criminal and the drunkard, and save the- souls and bodies of all the miserable creatures of the human race. I wish to uplift and educate the unfortunate, and my University will be founded upon, the doctrines of the Salvation Army." The General intends to have branches of this university both in Europe and America.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. The penny popular concerts will be resumed in Bold-street schools this (Saturday) evening. We would remind our readers that the opera " The Yeomen of the Guard" will be performed in the Parr Hall every evening next week by members of the Musical Society. Under the auspices of the Co-operative Society a lecture entitled " A scamper across the Alps " will be delivered in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Thursday„ eveuing by Mr. Henry Hibbert F.R.G.5.,. of Bradford. Admission will be free. A tea and public meeting arranged by the Women's Liberal Association and Progressive Lab mr League will be held in the Parr Hill on Monday evening,, October 28th. Mr. A, H. Crosfield, M.P., will preside, and the speakers will be Miss Florence Balgarnie,, Miss Truda Crosfield, and Mr. J. Tomkinson, M.P. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. Bequest to Penketh Friends' School. Among the many bequebts in the will of the late- Mr. Francis Williams Dymond of 3, Manston-terrace, Heavitree, Devon, who left property sworn at £94,077, is one of £5OO each to the Friends' Schools at Penketh,, Saffron Walkden, Sidcot, Rawdon, Ackworth, Sibford, Wigton and Ayston, Waterford, Mount Mellick and Brookfield. Personal. Mr. J. S. Place, pay-master R.N., H.M.S. Antrim,, stationed at Gibraltar, and a son of Mr. E. Place, market inspector, is among the winners of prizes of ten guineas, offered by the proprietor of" Badminton" for the best photograph of any sporting event. The subject of Mr. Place's photograph was a naval one,. entitled "Hands to Bathe." Mr. Stanley C. Jones, son of Mr. H. C. Jones, of; 192, Wilderspool-road, has succeeded in obtaining a pass in the second division in connection with the examination for matriculation held by the London University at London early in September. He was educated at Arpley-street Schools under Mr. E. Wallington, and later at the Technical Schools under Mr. E. S. A. Robson and recently obtained a scholar- ship with four distinctions entitling him to a place in. the Wesleyan Training College, Westminster, where he is now preparing for his final examinations for a. schoolmaster. WHITE TOASTING CFEEEsE,6d. per lb.at ASHLEY'S.—Advt The Catholic Young Mens Society. The Literary and Debating section devoted Thursday- evening to the reading and discussion of sholt papers. Ten minutes was the time alloted for the readings and eight minutes to the discussion. The papers proved to be very interesting and comprised the following: J. Roddy on" Edmund Burke," P Delaney on "The Legalising of Betting," E. Rigby "The Autobiography of a promise." J. Dolan" Science and Religion." T. Rigby " Catholics and Charitable Institutions and Undertakings" and T. Newns on " Socialists and Socialism." A paper by W. Kilduff on "Catholics and Trade Unions" was postponed. The Rev. Father Buggins, 0.5.8., who occupied the chair, commented on and complimented the writers of the papers, and he pointed out that events of this description could not fail to broaden the outlook and sharpen the intellect of those who took part in them. The society has arranged a programme of lectures for the season. The first will be given on Tuesday, October 22nd, by Father Marrs of Our Lady's, Lafehford, and the subject, the "Passion Play of Oberamergan." It will be illustrated by lantern views. PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK .FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Death of Mrs. L. W. Reynolds. We deeply regret to announce the death of I.rs. Edith Reynolds, of Millington House, Thelwall, widow of the late Mr. Lewis William Reynolds, who passed away at Liverpool on Monday evening. The deceased lady had only been seriously ill a very short time, and the end came suddenly. She was held in the rhighest esteem by a large circle, to whom her death, following so closely upon that of her husband, came as a shock. She I was a daughter of the late Mr. John Rylands, who was at one time president of the Musical Society, and like her late husband she took a deep interest in the work of that organization, being a member for many years. The funeral took place very quietly at the Manchester Crematorium yesterday afternoon. The Rev. T. P. Williamson, Vicar of Thelwall, conducted the service very impressively. The chief mourners were :—The Rev. F. G. Reynolds, Messrs. J. P. Reynolds, E. W, Reynolds, L. G. Reynolds, John Blackly, W. H. Bleckly, Paul Rylands, W. P. Rylands, J. Bleckly, jun,, Blomfield (Liverpool), R. Lewis and W. E. Fowler (deceased lady's solicitor). Messrs. Robert Garnett. and Sons had charge of the arrangements which went, superintended by Mr. W. C. Nicholson.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>,</p>
        <p>Telephone "Operators." At Daresbury on Tuesday two boys from Stockton Heath named Robinson Mortimer and Herbert Williams were fined 2s. 6d. each for damaging tele- phone insulators at Stockton Heath on August 18th. It was stated by Mr. W. D. Jeans, solicitor, appearing for the National Telephone gompany, that the offence was a very serious one, as the breakage of the insula- tors caused an interruption to the telephone service. DOULTON TOILET WARE and Dinner Sets. See Windows.—J. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Shorthand. Notwithstanding several counter attractions, there was a good attendance at the weekly meeting of the Warrington and District Shorthand Writers' A.ssocia7, tion, on Wednesday evening, at the Winmarleigh Cafe. After the usual speed practice, Mr. W. Carter read his paper entitled "The Study of Shorthand. Its Benefits.' He said that the three chief advantages to be derived from a practical study of shorthand were : 1, the saving of time; 2 mind gymnasticism ; 3, the taking down of speeches verbatim. The paper was listened to with great attention, and an interesing discussion ensued, a hearty v.)te of - thanks to Mr. Carter terminating an interesting and enjoyable meeting. It is worthy of note that next week the Rev. Canon Stevenson, M.A., will deliver a lecture before this Association, the subject being • ." Tennyson's 'Gareth and Lynette,'" when there should be a good muster of members and prospective members. KEIL BUTTER, is. per Ib.—ASIILEY'S, Sankey st.—Adv Mr. G. L. Dewhurst's Will. Among the latest wills proved is the following : George Littleton Dewhurst, of Blakemere, Sandway, Chester, D.L. J.P. for Che hire, and High Sheriff in 1904, (formerly of Lynam), a director of the great mercantile firm of G. and R. Dewhurst, Limited, Man- chester, and of Williams Deacon's Bank, 1,311,292. Mr. Dewhurst leaves to his wife £l,OOO, 1,600 worth of furniture, the use of his pictures, his Scotch resi- dence, and £lOO a year ; to his son and daughter £5OO each, and to his three sisters, Frances Eva, Ethel Ada, and Harriet, 1,500 each. Ile, gives a horse to each of his three brothers, £4OO a year to his daughter Margaret Eva, £2O to his executor, William Gerard Finch, 2,000 shares in G. and R. Dewhurst, Ltd„ upon trust for his wife for life and then for his children ; 2,000 shares in trust for his daughter Margaret Eva; and 2,000 shares in trust for his other children, except George and Margaret Eva. All his Teal estate to go with the residue of his personal property he leaves to his son George Charnley Littleton Dewhurst. FOR PURE PORK SAUSAGE, try ASHLEY'S.—Advt. St. Clement's Men's Club. On Thursday evening, a " smoker " in connection with the distribution of the summer billiard handicap prizes was held in the club room, Bank-street. Mr. H. 0. Rhoden presided over a good attendance of mem- bers and friends. During the evening, an interesting `- presentation was made to Mr. John Wainwright who r has been treasurer of the club for 20 years. This took t' the form of a silver-plated tea set bearing the following inscription :—" Presented by the St. Clement's Men s Club to John Wainwright, Esq., for long service as treasurer on the occasion of his silver wedding." A pair qf silver sugar tongs was also included for Mrs. Wainwright. The presentation was made by Mr. P. H. Simkin, secretary, who alluded to the great services rendered by Mr. Wainwright and to the interest he had taken in everything connected with the club. Messrs. Thughton, H. Broadhurst and C. Gandy also added their congratulations. The recipient very feelingly responded on behalf of himself and his wife. The following members received their handicap prizes: —J: Wolfenden, J. Whalley., J. Wainwright, H. C. Rhoden, B. G. Little, P. H. Simkin, A. E. • Burdett, Jesse Gray, F. S. Walker and C. J. Sheath. A good programme was rendered by Mr. Brobbin's concertina party, Messrs. C. J. Sheath, J. Fleming, C. Gandy, J. Wolfenden, Ditchfield and P. H. Simkin. A number of gramaphone selections were provided by Mr. G. B. Little. Mr. S. Wolfenden acted as • accompanist. PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Horse and Jockey Bowling Club. The annual prize distribution and smoking concert in connection with the above club was held at the Horse and Jockey Hotel on Wednesday evening. There were about 50 members present and the concert was presided over by Councillor John Wilkinson,J.P., (president of the club), who was Fnpported by Coun- cillor Owen Toole, Councillor E Cunnington, Messrs. W. McNeill Sloane, John Holden, Phillip Darbyshire, and others. The Chairman proposed the toast The King and Queen and members of the Royal Family," which was received with great enthusiasm. Mr. Councillor Toole proposed the toast of the evening, "The health of the President," and in doing so referred to the sportsmanlike manner in which the President interested himself in the various pastimes of a recrea- tive character, and also to the fact that he had occupied the Presidency of the Horse and Jockey Bowling Club for more than twenty years. Mr. Sloane supported the toast which was received with musical honours. The Chairman then presented the prizes to the succes- ful competitors : —lst prize, Joseph Holbrook, jun., 2nd James A. Rushworth, 3rd A. Eckersley, 4th, E. Millington, sth I. Swindells, 6th Joseph Gleave, 7th H. Perry, Bth Joseph Holbrook, sen., 9th S. Holloway, 10th W. Muskett, 11th R. Holbrook, 12th A. Harper, 13th W. Mees, 14th R. Lowe, 15th J. Adams, 16tE, C. Holloway. Mr. Rushworth proposed a vote of thanks to the donors of the prizes, and Mr. E. Millington seconded.— Mr. John Holden responded on behalf of the donors.—After the presentation of prizes songs were gven by Messrs. Shelley, Muskett, Perry, Hindle, Lee, Millington and Probert, which greatly enhanced • the enjoyableness of a pleasant evening. Mr. Dick ' Scarisbrick's humorous sketches were highly appreci- ated. Mr. Dooley officiated at the piano. Mr. Coun- cillor Toole proposed a vote of thanks to the host and hostess, (Mr. and Mrs. J. A. Rushworth), for the admirable way in which they had catered and Mr. Sloane seconded. V PETROLEUM OIL IN BARRELS and Drums delivered free. Ask prices.—J. S. GARLAND, Iron- monger, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Beautiful Warrington Society. The annual meeting of the School Children's Branch was held on Friday last. The following report of the secretary was approved. "The number of members for the year ending July 1907 was 2,519 compared with 2,322 of last year. All the senior departments with one exception have school societies, and the interest and enthusiasm shown at the time of the forma- tion of the society have been well maintained. The committee has met on eight occasions during the year, the attendance of the members on the whole being very good. Through the kindness of the Executive Committee of the society each scholar who was a mem- ber of any school society received a plant for home cultivation, and prizes to the value of £4 10s. were awarded at the shows held in the various schools. The following is an extract from the judges report. "We visited in all 23 departments of the various schools, and 1,350 plants were submitted to us against 1,200 last year. We find on the whole a very great improvement in the quality of plants, some of them showing great attention on the part of the growers. The details of culture which we gave last year in many of the schools seem to have been borne in mind, for the plants are better staked, the pots were cleaner, and the soil has been better attended to." The school • cdildren's bulb show held in March proved a great success. Over 700 pots of bulbs were sold to the members, and of these 334 were entered for competi- . tion. The Committee have to thank the Co-operative ' Society and many friends for their kind assistance in making the show a success. The two senior departments of the Beamont Council School have formed school societies, and it is pleasing • to know that an excellent start has been made. The Secretary has been invited to - represent this Society, and to explain its schemes of work at meetings of teachers held at Oldham and Salford. Although the school children's branch was only formed two years ago, the committee think that good work has been done, and excellent progress made, and thanks are due to the head teachers, the staffs of the schools and especially the school secretaries for their work in interesting the children in preserving and loving all that is beautiful, and in fostering that desire for cleanliness and beauty,'-which in time will bear fruit. The Executive Committee for • the ensuing year was elected and Mr. Longshaw re-appointed secretary and treasurer. The distribu- tion of bulbs for the March show is now taking place and in addition to the prizes offered last year Mr. Edden has kindly promised to give a special prize. Much of the success of the branch is due to the work or Mr. Longshaw and it is hoped that before long the one ontstanding school will have fallen into line and become an active agent in promoting the objects of , the Society. TABLE, FLOOR AND HANGING LAMPS., Now Designs now showing.—J, S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge- street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Concert at Latchford.</head>
        <p>Concert at Latchford.</p>
        <p>A. highly successful concert was given in the Baptist schoolmoms, Wash-lane, on Monday evening, the Mayor (Alderman Smethurst) presiding. There was a large attendance and a most enjoyable programme was submitted. All the items were well received. Songs were given by Miss Hayes, soprano; Miss Mary Davenport, contralto ; Mr. Alfred Hall, baritone ; and Mr. Edward H. Pearce, bass. Mr. E. A. Sanders delighted the audience with humorous recitations, and the laughable songs and sketches by Mr. Alfred Grounds proved capital features. Miss Gladys Wynne gave a pianoforte solo, and she, and Miss Gladys Hall acted as accompanists. Farm Fire at Croft. At 10.55 on Monday morning the Warrington fire brigade received information of an outbreak of fire at the farm occupied by Mr. S. Thomason, Heath-lane, Croft. Before half-past eleven the brigade, under Deputy-superintendent Turner, with the engine Major, had arrived on the scene. They found the fire had got a good hold. The hose was quickly laid, and a plentiful supply of water being forthcoming from an adjacent pit, the fire was soon mastered. It was some hours afterwards, when the firemen were able to withdraw. The hay 'which was destroyed contained the produce of about five Cheshire acres of wheat, and the damage is roughly estimated at ;.£l3O. The cause of the fire is at present unknown. Unlawful Possession of Game.</p>
        <p>At Daresbury Petty Sessions, held at Stockton Heath, on Tuesday, James Higham, described by the police as "a poacher of no settled abode," was charged with having on September Ist at 4.50 p.m., at Ackers- lane, LatAtford Without, been found in unlawful possession of game, to wit a hare.—P.C. Sumner, of G-rappenhall, said he saw several men together and, suspecting they had been in search of game, he went towards them. All ran away and the defendant was the only man he was able to catch. He examined him and found a hare concealed under his shirt. Defendant said he had found it at Lymm, but subse- quently admitted he had taken it from an adjoining field.—Defendant said that if the Bench convicted him they would "convict him on a former character." He was surely allowed to find a hare as well as anyone else and to pick it up. - The Chairman said there was a black list against the defendant who would be fined 20s. and costs.—Defendant: I will pay nothing.</p>
        <p>" HERALD " KITCHEN RANGES and " Peveril Fire Grates are unsurpassed. Inspection invited. —J. S. GARLAND, 51, 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —Advt.</p>
        <p>P.S A. Mr. William Beswick presided at the weekly meet- ing of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday, and spoke from the Bible history of Joshua, the great leader of men. Mr. J. Harrison (Liverpool), gave an address on " Enthusiasm," and asked what it meant? To him it meant the indomitable zeal for the cause they had at heart. They were becoming too indifferent and apathetic, Church and P.S.A. alike. What a power for good they would be in the world, if they put the same enthusiasm into Christ's work, they did in sport and wealth-seeking. Let them look upon men as Paul did, who sacrificed life itself, in the service of Christ? He knew that to live was Christ, and to die was gais. Let them live a noble life and spend and be spent in the Master's work and receive his "well done." Mr. W. H. Payton rendered an organ solo in a skilful manner. The Rev. J. Yonge and Mr. R. Lewis also took part in the meet- ing. Seven new members joined the Brotherhood bringing the total up to 418. —The monthly open meet- ing of the Buckley-street Society was held on Sunday. Mr. J. Urmston presided. The speaker was Mr. Truman (of Liverpool), who gave an address on " Choice." The soloists were Miss Alice Griffiths, who gave a sweet rendering of "0 Divine Redeemer," and Master Arthur Oakes of St. James Choir proved himself a good soloist in "I will pour out My Spirit." Mr. A. Bates gave two violin solos in an excellent manner, " Aria " and " Kuchen-Arie." Miss M Plinston was the accompanist. Mr. H. Shaw also took part in the service. SPLENDID PURE BIITTER,IO3. per lb.at ASIILEY'S.—AAV Local Choirs at Blackpool. - -</p>
        <p>At the Blackpool Musical Festival on Saturday, our two local male choirs took part in the male voice com- petition. There •were only three entries. The four pieces chosen, which were very difficult tests, were Patriot's Vow," (Cornelius) ; "Sorrow's Tears" (Cornelius) ; " The War Song " (Granville Bantock) ; "The Crusaders" (McDowell). Two pieces were sung at the afternoon,and two at the evening meeting. The adjudicators were Dr. NcNaught and Mr. Gran- ville Bantock. Mr. Lionel Benson and Mr. Ernest Newman who awarded the first prize, with challenge shield, to the Manchester Orpheus Glee Society (con- ductor, Mr. S. Hassan), and the third to the Warring- ton Apollo Male Voice Choir (conductor,Mr.H.Berrey). Warrington Tradesmen's Association. The annual meeting of this Association was held on Tuesday evening in Toft's Assembly Rooms, Mr. G. P. Gorst presiding.—The annual report presented by the Secretary (Mr. H. Woolhouse) showed that the Associa- tion had been very active on public questions. The committee had endeavoured to gain a concession for shop assistants so far as return tram fares were con- cerned, but the Tramways Committee did not seem disposed at present to alter the existing arrangements. The committee felt that it was a great hardship to shop assistants that they should be deprived of benefits conferred upon another section of the public, and they expressed the hope that before long they would be able to convince the Tramways C immittee that an injustice was being done to a very deserving portion of the community. The Association was legally, represented at Local Government Board inquiries con- cerning the proposed new street from Rylauds•street to Sankey-street and property purchasing. Letters had been addressed to the Mayor and Corporat; on on various subjects. The committee objected to the Electrical Engineer being allowed two pupils, and that objection was upheld by a majority of the Town Council. A letter was addressed to the Corporatioa expressing the gratitude of the executive that the 1 Council were taking into consideration the question ' of increasing the salaries of certain workmen in the employ of the Corporation; also a further letter was sent complimenting the Council on the great saving effected in the erection of the Bolton Council school. —A letter was read from the retiring President (Mr. William Roberts) thanking the committee and member- ship for their unfailing kindness and cordial support during the year. The following officers were elected: -President, Mr. J. E. Richardson ; vice-presidents; Messrs. W. Roberts, F. Pendlebury, H. N. Houghton J. Critchley, W. T. Williams, and F. P. Gorst ; secre- taries, Messrs. H. Woolhouse and T. Proud ; treasurer, Mr. J. Burke; committee, Councillor Lord and Messrs. W. Isherwood, C. Toft, J. W. Hoult, H. C. Rhoden, W. Taylor, W. Cooper, J. H. Houghton, T. Shaw, F. Hickman, 'P. I,Vhitehurst and James Carter.</p>
      </div>
