      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>COMING ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>THE COMING ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>THE NOMINATIONS.</p>
        <p>Nominations of intending candidates must be handed in before 5 p.m. OR Thursday the 24th inst. The Mayor will hear any objections the day following. TOWN HALL WARD. Mr. William R,ogctrs, the licensee of the Feathers Hotel, Bridge-street, and a former member of the Town Council, has decided to accede to the request of the Conservative Committee of the Town Hall Ward to oppose Mr. G. I'. Wilding. one of the present repre- sentatives of the ward, at the forthcoming municipal elections.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD.</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee for Whitecross Ward was held in Thewlis-street schools on Wednesday evening. Councillor James Hvans presided over a large attendance which included Councillors Barton and Hawthorn. Messrs. J. T. Barton and R. W. Cook (Ward secretaries) and J. Howes, jun., (Liberal agent).— Councillor Hawthorn gave an address dealing with the work of the Council, and at the close a vote of thanks was accorded to him fer his past services, and he was invited to stand again. He consented to do so and the announcement was received with enthusiasm, the members at once signing a nomination paper on his behalf. LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>_ The Liberal Committee for this ward met on Wednes- day evening in the Primitive Methodist schoolroom, Knutsford-road, under the presidency of Mr. W. Roberts, J.P. There was a good attendance including Councillor Ellison, Mr. Fred Atkinson (ward secretary) and Mr. J. Howes, jun. Councillor Ellison gave an account of his work in the Council and was thanked and invited to stand again. He consented to do so. ST. AIOZTIN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Liberal Committee for this ward was held last evening in Thewlis-street Schools, Alder- man Tinnion presiding over a large gathering which included Councillor Burton and Mr. J. Howes, jun. The members cordially thanked Councillor Burton for his services and at their request he again consented to be nominated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NON-TRADERS AND THE ALDERMANIC VACANCIES.</head>
        <p>NON-TRADERS AND THE ALDERMANIC VACANCIES.</p>
        <p>A largely-attended meeting of the Warrington Muni- cipal Association was held on Thursday at which it was decided that the Non-Trading members of the Council should take no action in regard to Aldermanio vacancies, until after the nomination of candidates for municipal contests.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 12, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 12, 1907.</p>
        <p>! OCCIDENT AND ORIENT.</p>
        <p>[BY MR. H. WHITIIAMI At last the expected has happened,and the advanced guard of the Western Hemisphere has said pretty plainly" Asia only for the Asiatic." Law, or no law, treaty or no treaty, we will not have the Mongolian or the Hindoo on the Pacific coast, I nor yet will the Canadians allow them to settle on Canadian soil. The whole of Nerth America must be a white man's country, or some thousands of these white men will know the reason why. At the present time of writing the Chinese and Japanese quarters of Vancouver, are practically in a state of siege. Hundreds of revolvers and guns have been sold to the Orientals. Every Chinese merchant goes about with a body guard armed to the teeth, and beside the entrance to every Chiuese stores stands a Mongolian with a rifle. All the Chinese cooks, millmen and labourers and all the Japs working in the city, and in the country within a radius of fifteen miles from Vancouver have been summoned to the Oriental quarter. Firearms, knives, stones, bricks and bottles are in stock to repel what is at present called the "mob," and what will shortly be called the "people." Damage to the extent of some four or five thousand pounds has been already done, and the British Government will, it is thought, pay the amount claimed and apologize. Oriental labour is on strike and from restaurant to the shipping dock all is tied up, the whole business carrying with it a number of cracked skulls. Before giving a brief sketch of the beginnings of what I think will be a historical episode in the world's history I want to show my English reade‘• whare the late Government placed him in his relationship with the Japanese. The Japanese Consul General Nosso claims that Cana:da, that is Great Britain, is bound under article 2 to give the Japanese the same entire freedom of entrance, residence and busines occupation as the Dominion extends to any of the European nations, to say nothing of their position as an ally of Great Britain to which bond Canada is herself a party. The Consul goes on further to say, "The Japanese Government, moreover, would never enter- tain any idea of allowing their people to be dis- criminated against." The Pacific coast has for thirty years been the scene of strife in all the various phases of the Asiatic question. It has always stood stoutly by the slogan, "the United States for the white man." In the earlier days it was but the Chinaman who had to be dealt with, and people from the outside wondered why such a persistent kick should be made against him. As a rule it is the wearer of the shoe who knows best where it pinches. John could live cheaply and he could work cheaply. They came in crowds and formed Colonies in all the towns and villages of the Pacific slope. Their women folk came as slaves, and the festering sore of a Chinese town was seen in its dens of prostitution. John is a sober man and, as a business man he can give the white man points in honesty, but in every move he makes he proves him- self a born gambler. The Chinatown of San Francisco was a district of the city through which its visitors must be taken by a guide, and then the globe-trotters thought they had seen one of the world's sights. The initiated knew there was always a lower depth no mere visitor ever saw anything of. All the possible arguments against John coming to the United States availed little or nothing. He was wanted to help build railroads. He could work long hours and he could work for little, rice being cheap. The steam ships made money bringing him, and to-day the Chinese dominate in all lines of service, satisfied to take a few days off now and again with a visit to Chinatown and a lounge in the opium den. I have given John credit for a sort of honesty; to give him his due he has another of the virues to his credit, he is fairly civil if one lets his que alone. But the scene is changing and changing fast. While John is quietly plodding along another kind of a Mongolian has come to the front and with a look of contempt at John and an insulting sneer at the white man he lets one know that he has all the qualities needed in the make-up of a full-blown Jingo. John had been content with the various Mission schools provided for him and with his pigeon English he managed to get along. Not so the new corner; the Jap must have all. As a stalwart of five feet nothing and the years of maturity on his side, he wanted to sit in school beside the sons and daughters of the white man and eat of the tree of knowledge. A school for the brown man and the brown man alone was provided. " Hubert" in the "London Chronicle" says : —" It is impossible to get a standard by which we can measure brown against white or yellow against either. The Japanese possesses in the highest degree, some of the finest qualities; the Indian brain is of a subtlety compared to which the European brain is coarseness itself. The difference is a difference beneath the skin, deeper than the heart, in the very soul itself. Our view of sex, for example, may be a wrong view, at bottom an immoral view, but it is our view; it is different from the Asiatic's, and there is nothing that separates man from man so inseparably as a difference in the view of sex. The barrier which keeps East fro n West, is impalpable, but it is impassable too." The quotation just given is all that is required to be given to the question why do we kick against the brown man? I want to say this also to my readers. Our trouble is your trouble, and it will not pass away until that wretched treaty made with the Mongolians is torn into shreds. Alliance or no alliance, should war break out the white man will be found by white man's side, for blood is thicker than water and prejudice will make reason take a back seat. The stay-at-home is apt to take the globe-trotter's descrip- tion of these people. He finds them in the hotels as servants, or in the stores with something for sale. Those who are used to the service of others find obedience in these people, but I say, keep in mind the greatest fact of all, the Chinese and the Japs are business men to the core. I am not speaking at random, I have a Jap who spends two hours in my house every day. Yes, our trouble is your trouble, and the British Government is up against it now. The late Govern- ment sent Chinese to South Africa. The make-up of the present House of Commons is British opinion of such policy, and now in Vancouver you have the Mayor warning the captain of the steamship Monteagle, , which has on board .114 Japanese, 149 Chinese and 941 Hindus, that he could not guarantee him a safe landing at this port. The unionists have expressed their determination to resist the immigration of any • more Asiatic labourers. The steamship authorities , deemed it wise to heed the Mayor's warning, and the , Monteagle was turned back to Victoria, where the , Asiatics were landed.</p>
        <p>Two other ships, the Indiana and the Woolsvich, I both carrying Asiatics, are expected soon, and trouble ' is feared if they try to land their passengers here. You may comfort yourselves by saying all this is merely local, and Earl Grey may insist upon law and order being kept at all and any cost, but no body of white men could see the following and keep the peace : "An outrage by two Chinamen yesterday inflamed the people in Vancouver. The Mongolians, while passing along one of the main streets, saw a white baby sitting on a doorstep. They snatched it up and threw it into the middle of the street, which was busy with traffic. It fell among the horses' feet and narrowly escaped death. Beyond being bruised it was not much the worse. An angry crowd started in pursuit, but the Chinese had a good start and escaped. The fact that no arrests have been made has not tended to allay the temper of the people." lam not now writing as a Socialist. If I were I should write as if I considered the Asiatic a man and a brother. The great majority of my white friends have not thought about the Asiatic as a competitor in the labour market, nor as likely to become one, there- fore I am looking at the question from a proletarian point of view. The Jap easily stands first in the Eastern problem. Ile has appropriated to himself the commercial side of the civilization of the Western World. China and Korea will be absorbed in spite of all appeals to the Western world. All that goes towards making a militant nation will ly3 well attended to by the Japs. While Japanese travellers make nice speeches the Government at home will plan for war, and then their allies must look out. War with the United States is sure to come in the near future and then as allies of an</p>
        <p>Asiatic power England, or I should say Great Britain, will be in Queer-street. This is not merely a Japanese</p>
        <p>question. I see every day a group of Hindoos witli their black visages and towering turpans ; straight from India they come bringing all their peculiar caste ideas and lam afraid something worse. They are not clean and they come from the land of the bubonic plague. These men, no women, are spreading themselves through the country. Touching this strange business of races, allow me to say, just here, that since I started this letter I have had two missionaries spend- ing an evening with me.As husband and wife they have spent at least ten years in Korea, and their opinion, strongly expressed, was" the Occidental must keep the Oriental in Asia," and I add to this, that we are sure</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>I EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON WIDNES YOUTH'S TERRIBLE DEATH. ; Joseph Aldridge, aged sixteen years, residing at 12 ; Nelson-street, met with a fatal accident at the Widnes 1 FURIOUS CYCLIST FINED. Foundry, on Wednesday morning. Just after breakfast: he was working a small travelling hand crane, loading. ; A Warrington cyclist named John Turner of Leicester- The lifting-chain, which is an endless chain, runs on a street, appeared before the Newton-le-Willows magis- grooved wheel. By some means or other the wheel tmees on Thurday on the charge of furiously riding a bicycle at 9.50 on the night of September 21st. The came off and fell on his head. It weighs three-quarters of a hundredweight. The unfortunate lad's skull was evidence showed defendant was free-wheeling down a smashed to a pulp. He had been talking to his father hill near Winwick Church in the direction of Warrington only a minute or two before. The body was removed on the wrong side of the road. He ran down a 'young to the mortuary at the Accident Hospital. : woman, who was rendered unconscious, and was after- JOHN wards found to be suffering from a fractured skull and HOOLIGAN. ' concussion of the brain, and had to be removed to the At Widnes Petty Sessions, on Thursday, John ' Warrington Infirmary, where for two days she lay Hooligan, a labourer, was sent to gaol for three months' ~ between life and death, and was still an inmate of that hard labour for wife desertion. He left his wife and institution.—He pleaded guilty, and was fined El, and children chargeable to the common fund of the Prescot costs, and a guinea advocate's fee. Union. Mr. Radcliffe relieving officer, said that 1 prisoner left his wife and children destitute. They had 1 CONGREGATIONAL SOCIAL. been in the workhouse from March 28th to the present , Newton Congregationalists held their annual harvest day, and had cost the guardians .tl7 10s. Prisoner had home "social," last Thursday evening in the schoolroom. never written to his wi