ar, and the change of front will I ninth South-West Lancashire group of associations held lead to excited contest. in the afternoon, followed in the evening by the sixty- A THIEVING LODGER. second inaugural meeting of the Liverpool association 1 At Widnes on Monday, before the Mayor, Alderman for the forthcoming winter session. At the afternoon F. Owen, a Ditton youth, with a bad character, named conference the chair was occupied by Lord Kinniard, Henry Stock-en, was charged with stealing a silver president of the English National Council of the watch, gold chain, medal, and Gs. 6d. in money, the Y.M.C.A.'s who heartily welcomed representatives property of a fellow-lodger named Robert Hyslop on present from Ashton-in-Makerfield, Bangor, Birkenhead, September 30th. Mrs. Owen, the landlady (Stocken's Carnarvon, Colwyn Bay, Chester, Eariestown,Liverpool, mother), stated that on the day in question she came to f Llandudno, Ormskirk, Pert Dinorwic, Prescot, St. Widnes. As usual she left the pantry window open, so Helens, Wallasey and Wigan. After devotional exercises that the lodgers could get in. Whilst Hyslop was at the Rev. H. Elwyn Thomas (Norwood Church, delivered work, Stooken got in and took the articles. Next day an address upon" The Value of Young Life." Subse- the prisoner wrote to his mother asking her to meet quently Mr. W. A. Gilby (Ashton-in-l\la,kerfield), the him in Marsh-lane' and bring his working clothes with Rev. D. H. M`Pherson (Liverpool), and Mr. Oliver H. her. Instead of the mother going the police were aleCowen (Y.M.C.A. representative in Burmah) spoke informed and he was arrested.—Mr. S. Guyler, pawn- respectively upon energy as applied (1) to Y.M.C.A. broker, London-road, Liverpool, stated that prisoner life generally (2) to Y.M.C.A. life intellectually and offered at his shop the above articles in pledge. Not spiritually, and (3) to life in the foreign field. It was suspecting anything witness allowed him 25s.—Sergeant decided to accept the invitation of the Earlestown Gerken, who apprehended the prisoner, said he charged delegates to hold the next quarterly conference of the him at the police-station. In answer to the charge he group in their town in January or February. said: "It's all right; you won't find the stuff."— MR. J. A. SEDDON, M.P. AT BOOTLE. - A Li 1 Quarter</p>
        <p>Prisoner was comniitte, Sessions for trial. RUNCORN LADY AND HER HUSBAND. FROM MANSION TO COTTAGE. At Runcorn, on Monday, George Edward Aston, butcher, was summoned by his wife Helena Aston, of Beaconsfield, for a maintenance order on the ground of desertion.—Mr. H. N. Linaker appeared for the com- plainant, and said she was ill and unable to attend, and he applied for an adjournment. Mr. Knowles applied for costs of the day to be allowed. The defendant had offered his wife a home in a cottage. The magistrates might know the lady, and at the present time she lived in a mansion, and it was not a suitable place for the wife of a working butcher, who was only getting 28s. a week. The defendant would furnish the cottage with his own furniture, whereas for some years he had lived in a house where everything belonged to the wife.—The magistrates' clerk said the bench were not dealing with that. The question was should there be an adjournment? —Mr. Littler (chairman) : I suppose you gentlemen have exhausted all means of trying to make an arrange- ment ?—Mr. Knowles said he always believed in bring- ing man and wife together.—Mr. Linaker : So am I where it can be done.—The case was adjourned until October 21st, and the magistrates made no order as to costs.</p>
        <p>to the</p>
        <p>riverpoo</p>
        <p>Addressing a Socialist meeting at. Bootle on Wednes- day, Mr. J. A. Seddon, M.P.,in supporting a resolution congratulating the Labour Party on the legislation they had secured for the working classes, and demanding the right to work, as laid down the Unemployed Bill, said that during the last few weeks, they had been living in stirring times. They had been regaled to Smithsonian soot and NUArthur mud, and they had survived the ordeal. The Tories were quite entitled to the celebra- tions which were going on, because if the Labour Party in Kirkdale perfected their machinery he was convinced that the seat would be represented by Labour after the next election. (Applause.) He noticed that Mr. F. E. Smith had been to Glasgow, and acting the budding politician, and he (Mr. Seddon) wondered if it was the spectre of coming events. Mr. Smith bad probably recognized that for his labours in Kirkdale, Walton would know him no more after the next election. The , Labour patty were attacked not only by friends and foes, but by various and double forces. Some of their friends said they were too slow for anything, others that they were too fast, and another section said they vere too costly ; but they of the Labour party said , that they stood first and foremost for the weak. They looked upon it as a joyful burden to carry the helpless children and the aged veterans of toil, and to secure for every worker who was willing to work either by brain</p>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL' CORPORATION'S FINANCIAL POSITION. His Worship the Mayor (Alderman S. Owens) presided over the monthly meeting of the Widnes Town Council at the Town Hall, on Tuesday. In moving the minutes of the Health Committee, Mr. Clarke said that a severe epidemic of measles had caused the medical officer, in coujunction with the committee to close some of the departments in the Simms Cross School. The co-operation of the Sunday schools authorities was asked for, and he thanked them on behalf of the committee for the way they had responded. He also called attention to the fact that in the corresponding month of last year there were six cases of scarlet fever and twenty-six cases of typhoid in the hospital. At the present time there were only seven cases of each. On the Horses,Carts, and Stables Committee's minutes an amusing discussion took place regarding the purchase of a couple of horses for which the committee had made no provision in their minutes. The Chairman explained that the committee had pur- chased the horses out of the Emergency Fund, and they had paid £5B for each of them. The Mayor, referring to the Finance Committee's minutes, said that the town's facilities for raising loans would stand comparison with those of any non-county borough in Lancashire. The gas and water properties, and the fact that the town had unexhausted borrowing powers, were great attractions for invertors. The over- draft at the bank of the Council's loan account seven weeks ago was £78,901, but since that time £52,010 had been borrowen, and arrangements made to pay £28,001 to the bank this week, so that the whole overdraft on the loans account was provided for. At the close of the meeting the Mayor expressed his acceptance of the Council's invitation to continue in office during the ensuing year.</p>
        <p>dry Vi7Orger or muscle, a seat at the banquet of life. (Applause.) NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL. Mr. T. Ormand presided at the monthly meeting held in the Council Chamber at the Earlestown Town Hall on Monday evening. There were also present :—Dr. J. N. Patterson and Messrs. M. Seton, A. Tunstall, F. Harwood, W. Thompson, C. B. F. Borron E. T. Evans, G. F. Clarke, C. Cole (clerk), A. Bowes (surveyor), and R. T. Surtess (water engineer).—At a meeting of the Sanitary Committee, held on September 16th, circular letters were submitted from the Local Government Board with reference to— (1) The sale for human food of diseased cattle and especially of cows suffering from tuberculosis, in which they stated that the officer engaged in administering the provisions of the dairies, cowsheds and milk shop orders should observe particularly the conditions of the cows which came under his notice, and, when any animal was clearly diseased and presumably unlit for food, should take such steps as were practicable to ensure that, in the event of theanimal being slaughtered the carcase was disposed of properly, and (2) as to their regulations, for preventing the spread of cholera,yellow fever and plague. It was resolved that the first named letter be referred to the Inspector of Nuisances and the latter to the Medical Officer of Health. The minutes of the committee were approved.—On tae proposition of the Chairman it was resolved : (1) That for the purpose of executing works of private street improvement a sum of £1,500 be borrowed from the Trustees of the Liverpool Victoria Legal iriendly Society, of No. 18, St. Andrew-street, Holborn Circus, in the City of London, on the security of the general improvement rate, at interest 3t the rate of .0 18s. 9d. per centum per annum; to be repayable by seven equal annual instal- ments of principal and interest combined from the date of borrowing. VULCAN WORKS INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>The largest and most important building to be erected in Earlestown in recent years is, without doubt, the fine new Works Institute at the Vulcan Foundry, on the site adjoining the new offices. The erection of this building was only commenced a few months ago, but it is rapidly approaching completion, and it is expected the premises will be ready for opening about the end of ,Novembn. Passengers will not fail to notice what an imposing structure the Institute will be when finished. The ground floor consists of a large dining hall, 110 ft, long by 60ft. wide, affording accommodation for 1,200 persons, with two coffee bars, served from the kitchen,which occupies a position in the centre of the north side of the dining hall. It will readily be seen that this is the most com- modious room in the district. The foremen's dining room is at the south-west corner of the building fronting the highway. The main entrance hall is of striking proportions, 30ft. by 23ft., and on the right of the same are the lavatories and shower bath. On the left is the main staircase to the upper floor, with cloak rooms, &amp;c. On the first storey is the Recreation or Physical Drill Hall, to seat 400 people, with committee and retiring rooms, &amp;c., and an emergency exit from the hall near the platform on the south side, leading direct to the open space between the offices and the Institute. In the tympanum panel is inserted the seal of the Vulcan Foundry, Limited, viz. : Vulcan at his anvil, surrounded by an arabesque scroll with the inscription, " 1907. Vulcan Locomotive Works Institute." The general contractor is Mr. Harry Fairclough, of Warrington, and the architects, Messrs. Robert Curran and Son, of Earlestown and Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>OLD AGE PENSIONS.</head>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Past Officers' Society of the Warrington District of the Ancient Order of Foresters on Nfonday evening, the President (Bro. Peter Peacock P.D.C.R.) gave an address an old-age pensions, and the position of the Ancient Order of Forester with respect to the same. He said. that according to the latest Government returns,there were to-day over two million men and women, over 65 years of age, and of these about two-thirds were in want. The present system of dealing with the needy aged was through the poor law. There were good points in the same, but it was admitted to be a cruel treatment to the aged, and an unscientific failure. It disfranchized and disgraced the decent, steady poor, and at the same time practically put a premium on careless living and vice, and rewarded the thriftless and ne'er-do-well. In contrast statistics gave them the annual income of the United Kingdom as £1,800,000,000, and 5,000,000 of our population out of 43,000,000 took half of that money, the other half was divided, more or less unequally amongst the other 38,000,000 people. Great wealth and sickening poverty were side by side, and that poverty took up a most cruel form when accompanied by old age. It was quite possible for them to put in place of their present system of dealing with the aged, a national system of old-age pensions, which shall carry with it no stigma of pauperism. It must be given as a right and not a favour. The accumulated wealth of the nation was the joint product of all its people, and not of one particular class. The question of earmarking any particular source of revenne, as was proposed by the present Government, was perhaps, correct, as a beginning, but if taxation was spread over, and as wide as possible, so that all should contribnte, hen there would be no feeling with any one in the right of claiming the pensions. The problem was simply readjusting the incidenoe of taxation, which must come sooner or later. They could not get over the fact, that the State received everything it got from the people. A pension of ss. per week, after the age of 05, would be au indirect influence ao encourage thrift. Put hope into the lives of the poor and they lifted them immediately out of that carelessness which hopelessness created. A pension of which thcy were sure would certainly do that and brighten their lives. (Applause.) By adopting the principle of old age pensions they would increase the solvency of the order. It would be the only honest way of exchanging the dangerously growing sickness liability for a well-defined liability, calculated with almost positive certainty, and each member would have a stake in the order which would not be so easily and readily surrendered as at present. A debate followed in which several of the prominent members took part, and the meeting terminated with a hearty vote of thanks to Bro. Peacock, who suitably replied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>RENIARItABLE FREE CHURCH GAT HERING AT Sr. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A large gathering of members of the St. Helens Evangelical Free Church Council was held on Monday night at the V.M.C.A. for the purpose of welcoming to the town five new ministers, who during the past week or two have been settled in the district. The ministers are the Rev. Everard Baguley, Prescot Congregational ; the Rev. J. Bradley St. Helens Primitive Methodist ; the Revs. W. W. Collett and A. Hobart Stacey,Wesleyan; and the Rev. J. Gwilym Jones, Congregational. Mr. , William Smith, president of the council, was in the chair. On behalf of the whole of the Free Churches he accorded a most hearty welcome to the new ministers. Colonel W. W. Pilkington and the Rev. IV. Holroyd, secretary of the council, also spoke, and said that the 1 council recognized the almost simultaneous advent of five new ministers as an event fraught with possibilities of the greatest blessing to the town. The new ministers i acknowledged the kindness and warmth of the welcome, A Cow Transaction. land expressed their pleasure at the good feeling which At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, existed among the Free Churchmen of the town. before his Honour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., HenrY ALLEGED SHOPBREAKING AT ST. HELENS. Stringer, farmer, Park .Moss farm, Crowley, claimed Patrick Blaney, a young collier, of 38, Silkstone- -5 from Robert Brooks, jun., cattle dealer, formerly of street, was charged at St. Helens on Monday with Antrobus —SA being for loss sustained on the sale of a breaking into tho Eccleston-street branch of the St. cow which defendant purchased from the plaintifl,but Helens Co-operative Society's premises and stealing tobacco, cigarettes, &amp;c., to the value of £2 2s.—Chief afterwards refused to take delivery of and 1:,1 for five week's keep of the cow.—Mr. Browne, for the plaintiff, Inspector Goodall said that on the morning of Satur- day, September 28, it was found that the shop had been said the defendant bought the cow on May 14th for broken into. A large glass skylight bad been taken out ;4110, and promised to take it away the next day. of the roof. Detective-Inspector Dunn made exhaustive Defendant, however, did not fetch the cow, inquiries, the result of which was that Blaney was and after elapse of four weeks defendant arrested in Liverpool-road on Saturday night by sent word that the plaintiff must sell the Detective Curran. A jacket and a pair of boots and cow, and if there was any loss he would make it good some of the stolen tobacco were afterwards found at Ilolaunnedytishe tigOollosiSs. oNnVtVen railway nheebeshaiindd their he o house. If there was any profit on the sale it was agreed that they. should divide. The cow was sent to Mr. Main- Prisoner was remanded until Tyuesday of next week. waring's auction sale and was sold at a loss.— Defendant admitted that he sent word to the plaintiff to sell the cow as he could not do it. At the time of CYCLIST KILLED. the sale his wife was seriously ill and she had since On Thursday morning, at Edmonton, a young man died. He was unable to leave his wife, and he had was riding a bicycle along the main thoroughfare, when not tlie money for the cow. Witness knew plaintiff the wheels skidded, owing to the greasy state of the would not let him have the cow unless he paid the road, and he was thrown from his machine. The wheel mone7.—Judgment was given for the plaintiff with of a heavy motor-car passed over his head, and caused injuries from which he died while being taken to costs. hospital.</p>
