      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>to fight hard to keep them out of the United Statese The same in Canada. May I now in conclusion ask thg Editor of the "Examiner " to print the followinn editorial relating to the latest mix-up we have o - hand of the British, American and Japanese question It is taken from the leading paper of the Pacific Coast, the" San Francisco Chronicle."</p>
        <p>ORIENTAL COMPLICATIONS. BRITISH SUBJECTS DRIVEN OUT OF ONE OF OUR</p>
        <p>SISTER STATES.</p>
        <p>The Hindoos employed in the mills about Belling- ham, in Washington, have been forcibly expelled by the white labourers with the evident approval of a controlling majority of the community. It is unnecessary to say that the " Chronicle " is utterly opposed to violence of that kind, wherever it occurs, and no matter against whom directed. No one, however, who is familiar with conditions as they are in California, lowa or Massachusetts can doubt that such outbreaks are certain to occur wherever the masses of white men and masses of Orientals compete in the same community for the same work. They may be punished, but they cannot be prevented. It is for that reason that we strongly insist that before it is too late all immigration of Oriental labour shall be stopped—not by any " treaty," heaven forbid—but by an act of Congress, signed by the President and rigorously enforced. It is the only way to keep the friendship of Oriental nations. The " Chronicle " believes the presence of these people in this country wholly undesir- able from all standpoints, but even if it were not so ordinary prudence and rational statesmanship demand that Oriental and Occi- dental labour shall keep each to its own continent. We object to dealing with this subject by treaty because it is not a proper subject for the treaty-making power to deal with; because if by treaty the United States permits such immigration its Government is utterly powerless to prevent riots or to punish them. All it can do is to tax the nation to pay "indemnity." The treaty-making power works in secret. The people do not know what it is doing until it is done. It is discredited in the eyes of a large part of the public as under control of special interests. The control of immigration should be dealt with by statute, freely conceding the same right to all others. This attack upon the Hindoos places the British , Government in a situation of unheard of embarass- ment. The British and American people and governments are on the most friendly terms. And yet the Hindoos expelled are British subjects and if complaint is made and it takes no action its already weakening hold on India will be still further endangered, for there is an active and virulent political vernacular Press in India. But what took place at Bellingham is likely to be any day repeated , in British Columbia and other Canadian provinces, , and drastic measures by the Imperial Government I are likely at any time to start a separatist move- ment in Canada, Australia and South Africa, where the popular feeting does not in any way differ from that on our Pacific Coast. In South Africa the contest is rather against Indian tradesmen than labourers, but it is equally persistent and effective. There is but one way for the Orient and the Occi- dent to get on peacefully and that is to keep their manual labourers apart. A mob in Vancouver, 8.C., has assaulted Japanese and destroyed much property belonging to them. It will be interesting to note whether Japan will send any war ultimatum to its ally, Great Britain, as it did to Washington over the school question in San Francisco.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE. Annual Meeting of Warrington Branch.</head>
        <p>NATIONAL SERVICE LEAGUE. Annual Meeting of Warrington Branch.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Branch of the National Service League was held in the Assembly room at Messrs. Crosfield's works on Thursday evening. The Rev. W. Bracecamp presided over a good attend- ance which included the Rev. H. B. Blogg (Frodsham), Major Strachan, Major Dutton Fairclough and Mrs. Fairclough, Captains Crosfield, Owen, Broadbent, and Waring, Lieutenant Owen, Mr. G. A. Beck, Mr.and Mrs. G. H. Kingdon, Mr. T. Mosso, Mrs. Edlesten and Mrs. Bracecamp. The CHAIRMAN asked the members of the League to copy the Labour party who through their perseverance and earnestness had risen to such a powerful position. If they did so, in less than ten years they would be able to say to any man who came forward and wanted to enter Parliament "What are your views on the National Service question ?" and if he did not satisfy them they would be in a position to say "You may go somewhere else. We don't want you." People had expressed surprise that he as as a parson should take an interest in that movement, because they understood every cleryman should go in for peace. He answered that every man, whether he was a parson or not, should be a patriot and love his country. The best way to show that love was to care for one's country and the best way to ensure peace was to be prepared for war. The Rev. El. B. 13LoGG, moved the adoption of the report, a summary of which has already appeared in the "Examiner." He said the deduction to be drawn from the reference in the report to Mr. Haldane's new regdla- tions affecting Volunteers, was that the Volunteer system would become increasingly unpopular. He maintained that would be a very good thing, for the reason that the more people came to recognize the injustice of the Volunteer system that was to say imposing on a small minority the duty of defending the majority—the sooner would the objents of the League be attained. There was a great dread of what was called militarism in the country, but that was a pure bogey. He believed national service would be a grand thing for the men of the country on moral and religious grounds. Mr. A. E. BECK seconded. He said the trust they held was a sacred one and they must not run the slight- est degree of risk. They must make assurance doubly sure and they could only do that by pushing forward the principles of the League. The report and balance sheet were adopted. Major STRACEIAN (organizing secretary of the League for Lancashire) proposed that the Executive Committee for the ensuing year consist of—Mr. T. H. Lyon, presi- dent; Dr. K. E. Markel vice-president; Miss S. Broadbent, Rev. W. Bracecamp, Mrs. Edelsten, Mr. H. Field, Mr. E. J. Hall, Lady Lilford, Mrs. Lyon, Mr. H. Owen, Mr. H. Roberts, Mr. J. H. Smethurst, Rev. Canon Willis and Rev. Father Wilson; Captain G. R. Cros- field, hon. secretary. And that the General Committee consist of the above together with:—Mr. A. E.Beck,Mrs. D. Fairclough, Mrs. B. Fairclough, Mr. C. Has lam Mr. F. W. Monks, Mr. W. P. Rylands, and Dr. R. lam, He reviewed the progress of the organizing work of the League, and delivered an interesting address on its objects. Mr. J. H. J. HAMPSON seconded and the resolution was carried.</p>
        <p>During the evening Messrs. Crodield's works orchestra under the conductorship of Mr. F. J. Lord, played selec- tions in admirable style. Mrs. Edelsten gave powerful recitations, a song was creditably given by Mr. Rose, and a duet by Mr. and Mrs. Hannan.</p>
        <p>A vote of hanks to the Chairman and artistes con- cluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>Services for Sunday. 7.3 Wycliffe Church Bewsey-street : Morning 10.30 and evening 6.30, the Rev. J. Yonge. Bold-street Wesleyan Church: morning 10.30, Rev. Thomas Miller; evening 6.30, Rev. E. J. Gawne.: Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church: Morning 10.30 and 6.30, Rev. E. Davidson. In the afternoon Mr. Davidson will deliver a lecture on "Moody, the Prince of Evangelists" and Mr. R. I. Jenks,of Penketh will preside. Baptist Church Golborne-street : Morning 10.45 and evening 6.30, Mr. George Evans (Manchester). Baptist Church, Latchford Anniversary Services : Morning 10.30 and evening 6, "preacher the Rev. J. F. Matthews. In the afternoon the services will be attended by the Apollo Prize Male Choir under the conductor- ship of Mr. H. Berrey.</p>
        <p>STAND WEAR AND TEAR. A great point that commends Columbia Records to all users of talking machines is their unusual durability. They are "just as loud as the best of the other kinds, just as clear. And there is a musical quality of tone about them that even an unskilled ear can detect. To all these good qualities add that other—durability—and it is not surprising that many talking machinists will have no other kind. A new catalogue of Columbia Gold Moulded Record a has just been issued, consisting of 80 pages, with handsome lithographed cover. It is provided with both a numerical and an alphabetical index and comprises an astonishing variety of instru- mental and comic, standard and sacred selections. A copy will be sent free to all applicants. Those who use disc, talking machines should apply for the October supplement of single and double-faced records. Address Columbia Phonograph Company, General, Room 20, 64 and 66, Oxford-street, London, W.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>EDNA</p>
        <p>CAKES.</p>
        <p>TETE LITTLI CAKE WITTE THE RASPBERRY CENTRE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman.</p>
        <p>Readers of this column are invited to assist in making it interesting and helpful to social workers by contributing themselves. Correspondents must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
        <p>The local branch of the Independent Labour Party is to be congratulated upon the excellent Socialist Demonstration in the Parr Hall on Monday evening. The meeting was splendid. The speeches were of the best, and the audience of a representative character. Doubtless, a full report of the proceedings will be found in another pal t of the paper, so I will content myself with making a few observations on one or two relevant matters.</p>
        <p>In the first place, I want to say that I observed nothing particularly "wicked "in the Chairman's remarks. True, Mr. Blatchford did refer to his unbeliefs. He said something also as to what Socialism meant. But you must read his speech. One wonders how the political parties will take it. The Tories, if they follow the example set them by headquarters, will ignore his speech, accuse him of outrageous designs upon Society, and, of con rse.damn him for his agnosticism. The Liberals will tell us probably, that the best of what he advocates will be found in their programme. Anent agnosticism, what would the Tory party say if it was accused of irreligion because of the agnostics who follow its lead? I know a few locally, and if I named them you would recognize in them at once " respectable " citizens, men of intelligence and business acumen. I have little hesitation in saying that the Tory party would be better off if there were more men of their ilk associated with the cause. At least, it would be more likely to fight fair.</p>
        <p>—And what would the Liberals do if they were attacked because the Cabinet contains Ministers who are agnostics? lam hopeful that intelligent people will study Socialism for themselves and not be guided by the remarks of unscrupulous party-hacks. Honest criticism is helpful and welcome. But, please, let it be honest.</p>
        <p>After the meeting, I was chatting with a worthy Alderman and a respectable Town Councillor. Both were delighted with the meeting; the latter remark- ing that he would give a thousand pounds if he could speak like Mrs. Snowden. Pointing to his breast, he remarked, "it's all here but I cannot get it out." Mrs. Snowden, in the course of her brilliant speech, said: "All men and women cannot believe equally easy. I contend that the man who denies God with his lips because he sincerely cannot reach out to Him, but preaches God and the Kingdom of Heaven in his life has infinitely more of the spirit of true religion within him than he who spreads forth -- " one could not hear the finish of the sentence owing to the applause Is it not a fact that there are men who call them- selves" Christians" who have not as much right to the title as General Booth has to that of King of England? Do not their lives denounce them and their "title "commends them ? But there will be a weeding-out some day and he rubbish tip will speak for itself.</p>
        <p>Thole is a fight against Socialism raging in the columns of a London Tory newspaper. The Editor has written to certain public men inviting replies to the query "How best is Socialism to be met? ' Some of the replies are interesting but are not altogether in favour of the " cause." For instance, the Bishop of Croydon writes: "The best method of fighting Socialism ? Why, I don't want to fight it. I believe that Socialism is a coming force, and that we must apply ourselves to directing it into wise ways II Christian Socialism, or something in that direction; but I don't think that it represents the mind of the Church. I feel equally pure that the general principle underlying Socialism is a Christian principle, and that the Church must bend her thoughts more that way. The difficulty is as to bow to apply the principle. I do not think it will be achieved by a sudden revolution. It will have to be the outcome of quiet, steady thought, and earnestness of determination to accomplish good." I shall have something further to say about this next week.</p>
        <p>The " Nation " has a note on Kirkchle bye-election. It says: The election, instead of being fought upon Tariff Reform, Old Age Pensions, the House of Lords, or any specific Liberal, Labour, or Conservative pro- gramme, resolved itself into a violent discussion as to the nature, meaning, and scope of Socialism. Into such a discussion, of necessity there entered those religious interests which have for so long dominated the polities of Liverpool, and unedifying recrimina- tions, on the one hand, denounced Mr. Hill as an atheist ; on the other, inquired pointedly whether Mr. Balfour was a Christiau, ,and why Mr. F. E. Smith never attended prayers in the House of Commons. The Conservatives cannot be exonerated from the charge of originating such deplorable invec- tive. Assailed by the sudden and almost bewildering activity of the new Labour propagandism, they seemed to have sought to turn the flank of their attack by branding them all as atheists. It is the old charge, which once served so well as a cry against Radicalism when some of its advocates appeared to be blas- phemous assaiants of the Established Church. It will be said,' wrote Dr. Jelf to Maurice, in a famous controversy, Mr. Maurice is identified with Mr. Kingsley, and Mr. Kingsley is identified with Mr. Holyoake, and Mr. Holyoake is identified with Toni Paine. There are only three links between King's College and the author of the Rights of Man.' A similar frantic logic appears to have been exercised at Kirkdale. . • . The Liverpool Tories have just saved the seat by such tactics and it is doubtful if they have any large cause of congratulation at such a. result."</p>
        <p>JOHN WORKMAN.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY AND SOCIALISM. Mr. C. E. Parker's reply to John Workman will be published next week. Mr. Parker has been in Paris for some days and the M.S., posted from there, did not reach us until too late last night for insertion in the current issue.</p>
        <p>DON.--I will endeavour to deal with your letter neat week..—J.W.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Alderman Bennett on "Fundamental Christianity."</head>
        <p>Alderman Bennett on "Fundamental Christianity."</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Bold-strek Wesleyan Bible Class 1 on Sunday Alderman Bennett read an interesting paper , entitled " Fundamental Christianity." He said that he had often wondered how it was that nineteen hundred , years after Christ had come to preach His Gospel of peace on earth and goodwill towards men they found Christianity had made such little progress and that the kingdom He came to establish seemed to be far remote. If they looked out upon the world to-day they would find that Christianity was divided into a large number of sects, many of which seemed to be animated by a principle of mutual distrust and jealousy and that followers of the Master, instead,ot regarding each other as brethren, looked upon each other with suspicion and as exemplified in practice many of their char- acteristics were the exact antithesis of what Christ did. Moveover, if they looked at their large towns they were amazed to find such a mass of poverty and suffering, while the Christian nations professing to be followers of the Prince of Peace could not find any better way to adjust their quarrels than by murdering each other. He thought the fault was not in Christ's teaching but in the way in which men misinterpreted it. They had strained at the gnats and swallowed the camels and neglected the wider matters of justice, mercy and truth. They had b..en I worrying about doctrine instead of practice.As far as he had been able to understand the four gospels the whole teaching of Christ was summed up in our duty to God and our duty towards our neighbour. Fundamental Christianity to him was expressed in the phrase of brotherhood and love. If these were exemplified in our daily life it seemed to him that Christianity would draw all men to it, that the sects would all unite, that poverty and suffering would all disappear and that the spirit of the angers' song of Bethlehem would be realised in our daily life. If they wished to make sure of a place in the Kingdom of Heaven beyond the grave they must do their very best to establish a Kingdom of Heaven on earth. Mr. J. C. Holland, who presided, said they were all aware of the very active part taken by Alderman Bennett in public life, particularly in regard to educa- tional and social questions,and on behalf of the class he gave him a very hearty welcome. Many of the members took part in the discussion which followed the address. A hearty vote of thanks to Alderman Bennett, with hopes of another visit from him at some future date, concluded a very pleasant meeting.</p>
