      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Damages for Prize Dog's Death.</head>
        <p>Damages for Prize Dog's Death.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT CASE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrineton County Court on Thursday before his Honour :twice Shiress Will, K.C., Harry Sam Saville. ironmonger, The Cross, Lymtn, sued Joseph G. Watson. 103, Carter-street, Greenheys, Manchester, for £l7 7s. dames for the death of a prize dog and for injuries sustained by his son, who was his servant, in conseqnence of the alleged neeligence of defendant while driving a motor enr.--Afr.Arthur Browne appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. Senribach, barrister (instructed by Messrs. Root". Edgar and Co.) defendedi Mr. Browne said the claim was made up of £lO for the death of the clog—a very small amount for a prize terrier—three enineas for the doctor's bill for attend- ine the son. and £4 4a. for a fortnight's loss of the eon's serviees. Proceedine to state the case, Mr. Browne said that comine from Lymm Church to the Dam Head there was a very steep gradient, something like 1 in 9 or 10. Plaintiff's two sons had finished their work on the evening of July 18th and took the fox terrier for a walk. They were standing near the Darn Head when a motor ear passed towards Al tri ncbarn. Tmrnediately afterwards another car came from the opposite direction, driven by the defendant. The doe was under perfect control and was eating erns!: within five or six feet of the railings, there beine no footpath on that side of the road. The car came alone without any warning and the first near wheel ran over the dog. The second wheel threw the dog against one of the plaintiff's sons who put down his band to get hold of the animal which bit him, inflieting 26 marks. Defendant did not pull the car up until it had gone 60 yards. The road- way was 9 yards wide from the kerbstone on the opposite side of the road to the railings where the young men and the doe were standing. The car would not be more than five or six feet wide, and although it had just met the other car coming from Warrington it had ample room to pass. If defendant had used any diligence, he most have seen the dog. The car came so close to one of the Yonne men that he said bALI might have touched it. and he only lust escaped being rillh over. After the aceident defendant expressed his reeret and said he wonld compensate for the value of the dog. The question had never vet been settled in a Court as whether the defendant would he responsible for damages for the ininries to the on caused by the doe. Harry Sam Saville, plaintiff, stated that the dog had taken twelve prizes and clO was a very reasonable estimate of its value. Cross-examined he eaid be•bought the dog in question and another one for kelt Harry Sam Seville, iun., hove out. Mr. Browne's description of the accident and said there was ample room for the car to pees. it was goine at the rate of shout 14 miles an hour, which he considered negligence at such a daneernne corner. in answer to Mr. Sandhech, he denied that the dog ran underneath the car and was caught by the hack wheel. Tom Seville corroboreted. After the accident defendant. said be was sorry he had run over the doe, but he bad no time to stay and must get back. He gave bis address and number of the ear. There was nothing to prevent defendent seeine the dog if be had exercised due diligence. Witness' admitted that be did not tell defendant he had been negligent. Defendant did not say he was not to blame. Robert William Cheetham, painter,Lymm, who arrived on the scene immediately after the accident, said defendant expressed his sorrow and said that if the dog was a valuable one he would compensate plaintiff for the loss of it. Dr. De Jong described the injuries inflicted on Harry Seville by the dog and said the young man was not fit to work for a fortnight. Frederick A. Latham, dog fancier,said £lO was a very small sum at which to value the dog. For the defence Joseph G. Watson. the defendant, was called and stated that he had had the car since June of last. year and this was his first. accident. The Rev. H. R.Tiopwood, Rector of St. James', Manchester. his wife and daughter were peesengers in the car on the date in question. The speed at which the car was trevelline was eight miles an hour. Witness first saw the Savilles eight yards away when the car turned the bend. They had two dogs and both were standing still. Just when the CAT got opposite to them, one of the dogs ran out in frotn of the car and the near back wheel passed over it. Witness told ,Torn Saville that it was not his fault that the accident happened and he did not say be would compensate him. Saville made no accusation aeainst him whatever. Cross-examined. defendant admitted that the Motor Union was defending the action. TI the dog had not rushed under the car there would have been no accident. The Rev. IL R. Hopwood corroborated and said defendant could not, in his opinion, have avoided the accident. Mary Hopwood. daughter of thelast witness, said the •car was going quite. slowly. Mr. Sandbach submitted that plaintiff could not recover for the injuries to his sen,as no reasonable men could have foreseen that the dog would bite Saville. The only negligence suggested WS fl that defendant drove the car at 14 miles an hour,hut the law allowed 20. The Savilles made no suggestion of recklessness at the time of the accident and people were not generally so tender towards the feelings of motorists that they would hesitate to arty they were in the wrong, if they thought so. He further submitted that the dog was not under control. His Honour said that in cases of this kind it often happened that the most righteous and honourably- intentioned witnesses might differ And he was far from saying that in such cases any intention of misrepresenta- tion was to be suggested at all. The public were -entitled to the use of the roads just as ranch as any driver of a motor-car and no passer-by could be accused ' of negligence merely because he was not on the foot- path. These young men with the dogs hal a perfect right to he on the side of the road where the railings were. When defendant saw the dog be made no effort of any kind to • avoid running over it. Even If the dog did ruin across the road in front of the car, was that an unnatural thing for a dog to do? In such a case he would not acquit the car driver of any negligence, 'because such a thing might reasonably happen with any -dog. Defendant ought to have taken care. and in his (his Honour's) opinion he did not. Therefore' he held that the negligence in the case rested with the defendant He would place the value of the dog at £7. and also allow £3 3s. doctor's bill for the son and £1 10s. for loss of his services. Judgment was entered for £ll 13s. and costs.</p>
