      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</head>
        <p>SCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>5-9 59 5-8 5_9• CHRYSALIDS AND CARBON DIOXIDE. 5-11 In the Comptes Rendus of the Biological s'll Society of Paris, an account is given of the 5-8 6-0 absorption of carbon dioxide by chrysalids 5-10 by Marie von Linden. Chrysalids of two 6-1 species of Lepidoptera were placed in a moist atmosphere charged with carbon dioxide, and were found to have gained in weight instead of losing as they normally do at this ,stage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>STRIKING ENTERPRISE at Boots, Cash Chemists</head>
        <p>STRIKING ENTERPRISE at Boots, Cash Chemists</p>
        <p>Novel display of a cure for NERVES, STOMACH AND KIDNEYS.</p>
        <p>The striking enterprise which has always been a feature of Messrs. Boots', the well-known cash chemists, has found further outlet in the novel window display which they are now making at their shops in Warrington and District of a new and simple remedy for Nerve, Stomach, and Kidney affections, called Dr. Cassell's Tablets. Tbe outstanding feature of this artistic exhibi- tion is a magnificent plastic model, illustrating in a harmony of royal blue and gold the essential merits of these famous Tablets, which merits are summed up in the medicine's wonderful power in banishing all such troubles as nervous prostra- tion, anvemia, thinness, indigestion, kidney weak- ness, wasting, shattered system, children's weak- ness, and all forms of physical and nerve exhaustion. The nominal sum of 101 d. will purchase a box of Dr. Cassell's Tablets, and we heartily c,ngratulate Messrs. Boots on their enterprise in making this novel show and being able to supply a suffering public with a reliable remedy at such a low figure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THEFT OF A RING.-At Daresbury Petty Sessions,held at Stockton Heath on Tuesday, a girl named Lilian James. 12, Leonard-street, Stockton Heath, was charged with having between the sth and 26th August stolen a ring, of the value of £1 Bs. 6d., the property of John William Harmer, of 21, Gaskell-street, Stockton Heath. Florence Worrall,another girl,living at Barlows-cottages Lower Walton, was charged with receiving the same knowing it to have been stolen.—Mr. Steel, who appeared for the girls, admitted the. offence. When James saw the ring in a drawer at Mrs. Harmer's house Rhe gave way to a sudden temptation and stole it, Worrell pledged the ring in Warrington. He thought that the justice of the case would be met if the prisoners, who were very sorry for the offence, were dealt with under the First Offenders Act. The Rev. H. V. Pigot and Mr. John Hallows were in court and were willing to testify to the previous good character of the girls —The Bench, however, did not call upon these gentlemen to give evidence.—The girls were bound over to come up for judgment if required.</p>
        <p>THE NEW COUNCIL SCHOOL.-At a meeting of the Cheshire Education Committee on Monday, Mr. Beswick. the county architect, reported on his examina- tion of the 36 sets of plans submitted by architects in competition for the proposed new Council school at Stockton Heath :--" I am of opinion that the plans No. 23, scheme A, fulfil the conditions of the competi- tion most satisfactorily. These plans provide for the future extension of the building in such a way as to permit of a satisfactory sqhool being built at present for 500 children. When extended to 800 children the lower school is so arranged that it can be divided into separate departments for the infants and juniors. If it should be desired to keep these departments distinct, which, considering the large number of infants it may be advisable to do. The building is economically planned, but the estimate of £9,595 for the complete building for 800 scholars, including the cookery and manual instruction rooms, playgrounds, itc., appears to me to be too low." The plans were approved, the com- mittee concurring with the architects' observations as to cost.</p>
        <p>" SUNRISE LAND."—This was the title of a play pre- sented in the Church institute, Stockton Heath, on Wednesday evening under the auspices of the Warring- ton Branch of the Young Helpers League. There was a large audience and the performance was thoroughly appreciated. The play opened with the Festival of the Amanagawa, which the Japanese keep on the 30th June in honour of the conjunction of Aquila and Verga (two separated lovers, a Weaving Maid and a Shepherd Lad, who meet only once a year). A party of Japanese schoolgirls come for a picnic into a forest glade. One of their number. Lotus Blossom, has recently returned from a v)sit to England, and describes to them the strange customs, Bcc., on that "funny little island in the west where everything seems topsy turvy." The scene is a forest glade near Chien, a village in Japan. The following musical programme was gone through : introduction and chorus, "Sunrise, beautiful Sunrise Land " ; song, "The Japanese Umbrella " ; minstrel's song," The Legend"; butterfly dance; song,"Sayonara"; soldiers' song," Banzai ! Banzai !" ; chorus," Hail, tea so fragrant" ; soldiers' return,"Banzli ! Benzai !" ; duet, "Wilt thou be my flower girl " ; finale, "Ring the Merry Joybells." instrumental music was rendered by Mesdames Willett, Haviour, and Jackson Holmes whilst Mr. McClure contributed several songs. The characters in the play were taken by the following: Lotus Blossom, Miss Lucy Ridson ; Apple Blossom, Miss Bertha Shakeshaft ; Yum-Ynm, Miss Emily Pollitt ; Sing-sing, Miss Gladys Smith; Ogo-bo-go, (father of Lotus Blossom), Mr. W. Broadbent; his wife, Mr. L. Roper ; Langtoy, Miss M. Pollitt ; Minstrels, Miss Adshead, Miss M. Berry and Miss B. Wright: Fightee- Fightee, Mr. Jack Kidson ; Soldiers. Messrs. R. Hillier, S. Hillier, E. Brookes, N. Shakeshaft, V. Collins and D. Collins ; Geishas, Marianne Hill, Beatrice Adshead, Hilda Perrin, M. Shakeshaft, M. Merton, Maud Nuttall Constance Nutall, Maud Smith, and 0. Warham ; Butterflies, Doris Latham, Bessie Nuttall, Vera Smith, Gladys Brown, Marjorie Smith, and D. Adshead.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>LIVELY SUPPER PARTY.</head>
        <p>LIVELY SUPPER PARTY.</p>
        <p>Argument' Leads to Blows.</p>
        <p>A sequel to the lovemaking of a young bricklayer named Samuel Taylor, of Dudley-street, Warrington, was heard at the Warrington Police Court on Thursday. Taylor was charged on a warrant with assaulting Mrs. Farrell, mother of the young lady with whom he was keeping company, and also with assaulting Mrs. Waldron, sister of the complainant. It was stated that on Saturatnight prisoner called on Mrs. Waldron, and after tamper a quarrel arose. In the scuffle which ensued, it was alleged that Mrs. Farrell was struck by the prisoner in the face with a pair of fire tongs, causing two black eyes. Mrs. Waldron who went to her sister's aid, also received a blow in the eye. _</p>
        <p>-Prisoner's story was to the effect that he went to the house to arrange about his marriage with Mrs. Farrell's daughter. After supper an argument arose about politics and religion, which resulted in a quarrel and a free fight. He did not remember striking either of the complainants.</p>
        <p>On the suggestion of the magistrates to adjourn the case for two months so as the parties could arrange a marriage, prisoner remarked "If lam the breadwinner wept my own way." The magistrates, after endeavouring to arrange a settlement, imposed a fine of 10s. and costs and to pay the damage done to the furniture, or one month's imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>SIMPLE REMEDY.</head>
        <p>A SIMPLE REMEDY.</p>
        <p>Said to Relieve Rheumatism, and Anyone Can Prepare It.</p>
        <p>I Thousands of men and women who have felt the I sting and torture of this dread disease, which is no respeetor of age, person, sex, nation or rank, will be interested to know, that while for many years rheumatism was considered an incurable disease, now it is one of the easiest afflictions of the human body to conquer. Medical science has proved that it is not a disease in itself, but is a symptom caused by inactive kidneys. Rheumatism in uric acid in the blood and I other waste products of the system, which should be filtered and strained out in the form of urine. The function of the kidneys is to sift these poisons and acids out, and keep the blood clean and pure. The kidneys, however, are of sponge-like sub- stance, the holes or pores of, which will sometimes either from overwork, cold or exposure, become clogged and inactive, ibd failing in their 'function of eliminating these poisons from the blood, they remain n the veins, decompose, and settling about the joints I and muscles cause the untold pain and suffering of rheumatism, and backache, often producing complies- ttons of bladder and urinary disease, weakness, etc. The following simple prescription is said to relieve,</p>
        <p>the worst cases of rheumatism, because of its direct action upon the blood and kidneys, relieving, too, the most severe forms of bladder and urinary troubles : Tincture Gentian Compound, one ounce; Syrup Rhubarb, one ounce ; Liquid Barkola Compound, one ounce; and Syrup of Ginger, five ounces. Mix by shaking well in a bottle, and take one teaspoonfu after each meal, and one before retiring for the night. The ingredients can be had from any good chemist, and being purely vegetable, are entirely harmless, are safe to use at any time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>'HE KING AND THE PAGE.</head>
        <p>'HE KING AND THE PAGE.</p>
        <p>Mr DEAR CHILDREN, • I am glad to say new members are joining our League in large numbers. You will re- member that I made a special appeal a week or two since for all of you to do your utmost to induce your friends to become members. Well, some of you evidently took that little chat to heart, and have sent me good lists. The mem- ber sending me the largest number of names and addresses during October and November will receive a beautiful casket of Fry's Choco- late. Some of nix prize-winners, by a little oversight, gave me their holiday addresses in the recent Holiday Postcard Competition, hence a slight-delay in the delivery of prizes. I almost wished I was a child again when. I heard of the doings at Cardiff. Perhaps some of you did not hear about the visit of the King and Queen to Wales. Well, on September 20th, 40,000 school chil- dren were presented with beautiful boxes of chocolate to commemorate the visit of the Queen. Out of all the big chocolate makers; Messrs. Fry and Sop, of Bristol, were selected to provide the treat to the children. This firm designed and supplied the special boxes of chocolate sent out to_our soldiers in Africa a few years since.</p>
        <p>Only think of it, 40,000 beautiful enamelled boxes, printed in red; white, blue, and gold, made specially for these little ones. I really think the Cardiff children are very fortunate in getting such a nice preeent. These boxes were filled with the very best chocolate. I think many of you know what Fry's Chocolate is from the various prizes that I have given you during the last year or two. I don't intend that the Cardiff children shall be the only fortunate ones. The other day I was at Bristol, and visited some of Messrs. Fry's big factories—and I can assure you they are big—and then I selected a large lot of fancy boxes of chocolate, for I remembered that Christmas was coining on, and, also that I was making a special appeal to you to send me, during the next two months, some nice warm things for the poor. Now you see the point. don't you? That lot of chocolates and pretty boxe6 is intended for my Nieces and Nephews. Well, I hope a large number of you will try and win a prize. With love, from ____</p>
        <p>UNCLE NED,</p>
        <p>Once when Frederick the Great, of Prussia, rang his bell for his page to come and wait on him, there was no answer. He rang agAn, and still there was no answer. So he went out into the ante-chamber, and there he found his page fast asleep. The step of the King did not awaken him, so soundly was he sleeping. A letter stick- ing out of the boy's pocket caught the King's eye, and he was curious enough to take it out and read it. Not any more honourable that for a King than for any one else. But the boy had no reason to be afraid o'r ashamed of the King's curiosity, for it was a letter from his poor mother, thanking him for sending her his wages, and praying God to reward his kindness and attention. After reading it, the King went softly back to his chamber, took a bag of money; and, with the letter, slipped it into the pocket of the boy. Again going to his chamber, he rang the bell loud enough to arouse the sleeper, who immediately answered its summons. "You have been fast asleep," said the King. Fright- ened and confused, the poor boy put his hand into his pocket, and found the bag of money. He took it out, and, looking up to the King, burst into tears. "What is the matter?" asked the King. " .Ah, sire," cried the poor fellow, throwing himself on his knees before Frederick, "somebody is trying to ruin me. I know nothing about this money which I found in my pocket." "My young friend," said the King, " God takes ctifferent ways of helping us. Send the money to your mother: Salute her from me, and tell her I will take god care of both her and you."</p>
