      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>"GOD BLESS THE BAIRN."</head>
        <p>"GOD BLESS THE BAIRN."</p>
        <p>Standing at one of the street crossings of Edina was an old blind man. I noticed he had no dog with him, and seemed uncertain which way to go.</p>
        <p>Presently, along the road, came a little girl of about seven or eight years. Her pretty fair hair peeped from beneath her Tom o' Shanter cap, which was drawn well over her head to keep out the wind. The sweet childish face was a little sad, ad there was a far-away look in the big blue eyes. Some school books were under her arm, while her hands were plunged deep into her coat pockets. I saw her eyes rest for a moment on the old man's face, and a little smile of pity made her lips tremble. Then she approached him, and I heard her say: "Can I do anything to help you, poor man?" He turned his sightless eyes towards her, and answered: "I would thank ye if ye wid lead. me across the street."</p>
        <p>I saw her place her warm gloved hand in his, and very gently lead the trembling feet over the pavement. And I followed. "Do you think you can find your way now ?" I heard her ask, as they reached the other side in safety. "Oh, aye," he answered, " 'tis but a straight line, thank ye." "Then, good-bye," she said, brightly, and tripped across the street again, with a happy smile on her baby face; while the old blind man turned away, and I heard him murmur, while a tear ran down his withered cheeks, "God bless the bairn."</p>
        <p>And I thought of how that little act of kind- ness, prompted by the loving heart of a little child, would help to lighten one weary soul, and send a thrill of joy through the kingdom above.</p>
        <p>NORA D. GARDNE</p>
        <p>A TRAVELLER'S INGENUITY. °</p>
        <p>A striking instance of ingenuity in taking advantage of the resources of nature in an emergency is found in Sir Samuel Baker's account of his travels in Abyssinia. His stock of soap had become exhausted; and, as he pos- sessed abundance of various kinds of fat, including that of elephants, hippopotami, lions, and rhinoceros, he determined to convert a quantity of this grease into soap. For this purpose he required both potash and lime; and how were these to be obtained? The negleek- tree, he found, was exceptionally rich in pot- ash; he, therefore, burned a large quantity and made a strong lye with the ashes. which he con- centrated by boiling. There was no limestone; but the river produced a plentiful supply of oyster-shells, which, if burned, produced excel- lent lime. What was next wanted was a kiln in which to burn the shells, and this he con- structed out of one of those great ant-hills which rise to ten feet high, common to those valleyi, and which possess a very hard external crust Two natives hollowed out one of those hills; a draught-hole was made below from the outside; the hollow was loaded with wood and filled with some six bushels of oyster-shells, which were again covered with fuel; and, after burning twenty-four hours. a supply of excel- lent lime was obtained. Then commenced his soap-boiling, which was effected in a large copper pot of Egyptian manufacture. - The ingredients of potash, lime, and fat were then carefully mixed; and, after boiling ten hours, and having been constantly 'stirred, Sir Samuel obtained excellent soap, of which he had in all about forty pounds.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <p>-...--4.--.-..</p>
        <p>In San Domingo there is a remarkable salt mountain—a mass of crystalline salt almost four miles long, which is 6aid to be so clear that printed matter can be read with ease through a block a foot thick.</p>
        <p>Novel postal facilities have been introduced in the village of Rotherfield, Suseex. The nearest post-office is some miles away. and the village postman, while making his round, blows a whistle to warn the inhabitants that he is collecting letters. He also carries a tin box containing postage stamps for sale. Any one needing a postal order gives him the money and he leaves the order on his next round.</p>
        <p>__+_.</p>
        <p>The church of Towyside is the most curious place of wor6hip in Wales, for it is composed of two dwelling-houses knocked into one, Of course, the building is not palatial, but a church was badly needed, and funds were low, so two cottages were joined together. The building stands close to the river, however, and it some- times happens that the church gets flooded.</p>
        <p>EVERY TUESDAY during NOVEMBER. DECEMBER and JANUARY we will give away SIX SPLENDID FOOTBALLS for the six most interesting Postcards we receive during the preceding week. YOU CAN PUT ON THE BACK OF YOUR POSTCARD either A GOOD JOKE, STORYETTE, CONUNDRUM, PUZZLE, SKETCH, or anything else that you think is interesting—ANYTHlNG BUT A LIMERICK—and if your postcard is among the SIX best received in the week in which it is sent, you will receive a FOOTBALL. To every competitor, be he or she PRIZE WINNER or not, will be sent a sample bottle of</p>
        <p>MASON'S COFFEE ESSENCE.</p>
        <p>ADDRESS YOUR POSTCARD TO-DAY: Newball &amp; Mason, Nottingham.</p>
        <p>Good ! It's Mason's Coffee Essence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>200 YEARS A SECRET</head>
        <p>200 YEARS A SECRET</p>
        <p>GRANNIE OINTMENT FOR SKIN TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>For 200 Years the Secret of Grannie Ointment ha, been kept in one Family. Its curative action is wonder/11 in all cases of WOUNDS. CUTS, BRUISES, BAD LEGS, and all SKIN TROUBLES. It at once soothes and heals, and cannot hurt the most delicate skin, In many cases of very long standing it has quickly cured, even when doctors have despaired. Frae for 14stamps. GRANNIE COMPANY. NORWICH.</p>
      </div>
