      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ANIMAL FOOD.</head>
        <p>ANIMAL FOOD.</p>
        <p>Pat was not feeling well, and so consulted a physician, who said: "You are troubled with indigestion; you had best confine yourself to animal food for a while. I will see you again in a week or so."</p>
        <p>Pat sallied forth, and, meeting his friend Mike, said: "And now, Mike, me friend, what is animal food?" _ _</p>
        <p>"Animal food, is it? Why, you fool, what would it be but food that the animals ate?" Some time afterwards, the doctor called to inquire how Pat was coining on. "Well, doctor, I got along.well with the oats, but the cut hay was a bad mess to manage."</p>
        <p>MLRRY Copp.</p>
        <p>DAME DARBY'S BOY.</p>
        <p>Dame Darby sat at her cottage door, Her cat lay by her side; While Charlie, over the old stone floor,</p>
        <p>His hobby-horse did ride. Dame Darby loved her little boy— He was her daughter's son; She bought him many a pretty toy—</p>
        <p>His little heart she won. Dame Darby said to him one day: "Come here, my dear, to me; When you are grown a big, strong mau What would you like to be?" "I'll be a sailor bold," said he, "And sail upon the main; I'll see the world, cross every sea, And then come home again." Dame Darby now was very old, And Charlie had grown big; He had crossed the seas—a sailor bold--</p>
        <p>In a taut-set little brig. His face was tanned, his eyes so blue, Were clear and bright and keen; Dame Darby thought no lad more true In this world e'er was seen.</p>
        <p>H. COPP.</p>
        <p>Crows stood so thickly on the main line be tween Paris and Strasburg a short time ago, and such a slaughter was caused by a freight train ploughing through them, that the train itself was stopped, and a passenger train behind was held up for some time. The birds collect in great numbers at the beginning cf the spring in the district in question, and they alighted on the track to pick up scraps of food thrown from a dining car, and did not _move when the freight train came upon them.</p>
        <p>A large Baptist Church that stands in a city in California was constructed entirely from a single tree. Of course, that includes the wood- work of the structure. The tree from which the timbers, lumber, and shingles were cut was a giant California redwood. A considerable quan- tity of the lumber was left over after the church building was completed. The building has a spire seventy feet high, an audience room cap- able of seating 499, a parlour capable of seating eighty, and a pastor's study fourteen by twenty feet, a vestibule and toilet-room.</p>
        <p>.............+...........</p>
        <p>In Nagasaki, Japan, there is a pyrotechnist who manufactures birds of great size that, when exploded, sail in a lifelike manner through the air, and perform many movements exactly like those of living birds. The secret of making these wonderful fireworks has been in the possession of the eldest child of the family of each generation for more than 200 years. ,</p>
        <p>At a wedding that took place at the Chapel Royal, Hampton Court Palace, an enthusiastic blacksmith, whose forge was opposite the resi- dence of the bride, fired salutes" as the bride left for the chapel and returned with her hus- band. The "salutes" consisted of seven blank cartridges on the first occasion and tvienty-one los the second.</p>
      </div>
