      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>DON'T GIVE IT.</head>
        <p>DON'T GIVE IT.</p>
        <p>Some people are never well. Ask' them in a weak moment how they are and they will give you a lucid description of their various ailments and seek your sympathy. Don't give it. In 99 cases out of every 100 they are suffering from a sluggish liver and don't know it. A teaspoonful of the following prescription each day after every meal will give a tone to the whole system and assist the liver to carry out its duties more effectively :—Tincture of Buchu, one ounce; Karox Compound, one ounce; Syrup of Lemon, two ounces. Any chemist will make it up, or you can mix it at home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>WIFE'S SISTER ACT. Local Clergy's Coarse of Action.</head>
        <p>WIFE'S SISTER ACT. Local Clergy's Coarse of Action.</p>
        <p>The public being greatly interested in the course to be followed by beneficed clergy in reference to marriage with a deceased wife's sister under the new Act, the editor of the" Daily Postand norcury " communicated with all the beneficed clergy in the diocese of Liver- pool, with a view to obtaining their opinion as to the following three questions :- 1. Is it your intention to perform such marriages? 2. If not, will you allow the use of your church for such marriages ? 3. Will you admit to Holy Communion persons who have contracted such marriages : Rev. CANON WrEd.rs (Warrington Rectory) 1 and 2, no; 3. Yes, on the usual conditions. Rev. CANON PENRHYN (Winwick itectory) does not wish to bind himself beforehand as to his course of action. Rev. A. M. MITCHELL (Burtonwood).—" This is a matter for the bishops really. They should speak with authority and give definite instructions to their clergy, who should not be a law unto them- selves, nor to be forced to choose between the devil and the deep sea. Until the bishops are prepared to act decisively,l shall obey the law in all particu- lars. By ' I do not mean the law of the Church which, after all, is of no moral value unless it expresses the law of Christ. The question is, does it ?" Rev. J. CARSON (Newton-le-Willows Rectory) will act as instructed by the Lord Bishop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>WHITE CITY, MANCH ESTER.</head>
        <p>WHITE CITY, MANCH ESTER.</p>
        <p>The White City at Manchester offers at all times the most popular form of amusement,attracting the masses, the classes, the sage and the savant. It embraces nearly every form of present day entertainment. It furnishes food for thought. The entertaipments furnished are more abiding than can be found in any combination of entertainments elsewhere. During this and next week some 60 competitors have entered their names as aspirants for the . 9 stone championship wrestling competition, which is no doubt one of the largest and best competitions ever held in this or any other country, for the winner will have the proud dis- tinction .of not alone being the possessor of the John Calvin Brown championship belt, but the 9 stone champion nrestler of Great Britain. The arena has a capacity of holding over 10,000 people and no doubt its capacity will often be tested, as much interest is being manifested all over England in this contest. Among the many added free attractions, the wonderful Zenobias in their daring and perilous mid-air sensation continue to attract, as well as the Meteors in their hazardous comedy trapeze act. The band of H.M. Scots Guards under the conductorship of Mr. F. W. Wood, by kind permission of Colonel F. W. Romilly, C.V.0., C. 8., D. 5.0., were heard here for the first time on Monday, giving ,concerts afternoon and evening, meeting with instantaneous success. All of the numerous free attractions are retained, as well as the many side shows. New and novel features will be added from time to time,all of which are sure to please, amuse and instruct, and will be announced in due course.</p>
        <p>The Home Unhappy.</p>
        <p>DAUGHTER.</p>
