      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MOTHER.</head>
        <p>MOTHER.</p>
        <p>Anemic, "Run-Down." Melancholy. Headaches. Languid. Liver Troubles. Indigestion. Both cured by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills,</p>
        <p>Weak.</p>
        <p>The sunshine of the home fades when mother, wife, or daughter falls ill. Yet women's health troubles are invariably trace- able to poverty of blood and nervous dis- turbances. Therein lies the special value to women of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills tor Pale People; they cleanse and enrich bad blood, steady the nerves, and keep women strong and always well. Two cures in one home by Dr. Williams' Pink Pink 'Pills are recorded by Mrs. Hutchinson and Miss L. Hutchinson, of 7, John-street, Nes,vcastle-on-Tyne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>MOTHER'S 'STATEMENT.</head>
        <p>THE MOTHER'S 'STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>'Three years ago I felt run-down," said Mrs. Hutchinson. "my limbs grew very weak, and splitting headaches seized me. Pains, in my back and shoulders rendered me helplbss. I would often stagger as I walked across the room. My sight became blurred and my tongue coated. My liver grew disordered, and all food became distasteful.</p>
        <p>"I could ri sleep at night for palpitations of the heart seized me, and all day I felt tired, languid and listless. I made the mistake of taking common medicines, instead of realising that the only cure possible was to enrich my blood. A friend who had derived great benefit from Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, persuaded me to begin a course of that medi- cine. I did, and after taking only a few boxes I was a changed woman. My blood became rich, my sallow appearance gave way to a healthy colour, I enjoyed my food and refresh- ing sleep and was compl•-t,ly restored to health after six boxes of Dr. \Ville:4ms' Pink Pills." HER DAUGHU R ALSO.</p>
        <p>"Just when my daughter Lizzie reached the anxious age of young womanhood she became anemic. Her face grew pale and she was no Tf - 'longer bright and cheerful, but became listless , and languid. She caused me no little anxiety until I administered Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. In three weeks a remarkable change for the better came over her. She ItA complained of indigestion also, but all traces of this and amcmia left herrand her veins were filed with rich, red blood b Dr. Williams' Pink Pills." By making Good, Rich, Red Blood—that is how Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People have cured Analnia, General Wealines„ ' Decline,' Backaches, Headaches, Indigestion. Eczema, Rheumatism, Sciatica, Neuralgia, and Paralysis; also wcmen's ailments. Sold at shop:, (but avoid sulat itntes. and take care that ; the full name, Dr. Wifliams' Pink Pills for Pale People, is on every package), or direct front the British Depot, 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, post free for 25. 9d..a box, or six boxes for 13s. 9d, Write for an attractive illustrated pamphlet; of mAny cures, post free.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUM UNION.</p>
        <p>RESULTS. OF SATURDAY'S MATCEIES. Goal of any description... 2 points. I Try 3 points,</p>
        <p>THE COUNTY CHAMPIONSHIP. G. T. P. 2 1 7 *Lancasbire</p>
        <p>G. T. P. 0 1 3</p>
        <p>Cumberland</p>
        <p>NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. Hunalet 2 2 10 *Leeds *Dewsbury .... ..... 0 4 12 Ebbw Vale *Halifax Bramley 2 0 4 Wakefield T. ... 1 0 2 *Runcorn *Hull K. Rovers... 1 0 2 Huddersfield *Widnes 1 5 17 St. Helens , *Wigan *Barrow 1 3 11 Rochdale H 0 0 0 York</p>
        <p>*Salford Oldham 2 2 10 *Warrington ... 2 1 7 Broughton R.</p>
        <p>Oldham Win at )Warrington. • The Cup-final was repeated orlSaturday at Warrington but with a different result, the Cup-holders being beaten ! on their wave heath before 6,000 people. Both teams had absentees at the County match but Warrington suffered most by the absence of their three best backs— Fish. Taylor, and Brooks. Fine weather prevailed and a keen game was witnessed, but the home side failed to play up to form and were defeated in the encL by 10 , points to 7 points. Teams and officials : Warrington—Tilley, back; Jordan, Isherwood, Cart- wright, and Dickinson, three-quarter backs k Lees and Jenkins, half backs; Thomas Shugars, Heath, A. E. Boardman, A. S. Boardman, and Belton, forwards. Oldham—R. L. Thomas, back; Tyson. B. Dixon, • Llewellyn, and Oldershaw, three-quarter backs ; White and Beynon, half backs; Avery, Smith, Topham, Wright, Longworth, and Wilkinson, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Renton, Hunslet. Touch judge. Messrs. J. Gregory, Runcorn, and 4. Hanneford, St, Helens. •</p>
        <p>Warrington opened in vigorous fashion and soon, penned Oldham -in their quarter, but the attempts at scoring were not well-executed. Dixon was penalised for not playing the ball, and G. Thomas opened the Warrington score with a fine goal from the quarter line. Oldham played fine football for the next quarter and i quickly took the lead. White got away cleverly in ; some loose,play and passing to Llewellyn, the latter scored easily. No goal resulted, and Warrington at once rushed the game to the other end and Thomas kicked another fine penalty goal. Again Warrington , were quickly deprived of their lead. Tilley was injured in a collision and had to retire from the game, Heath taking his place. Oldham soon attacked, and Beynon getting smartly round a scrum passed on the line to White, who scored and also placed a goal. The same player also kicked a penalty goal and at half-time Oldham had a lead of 10 points to 4 points. Warrington were still short of Tilley on resuming but they made good efforts to reduce Oldham's lead. The visitors defended well and a lot of punting was seen in which Warrington came off second best. Oldham had little the best of matters and at threequarter time Lees got away cleverly and punting to the line was obstructed by the full-back, the referee awarding the Warrington skipper a try, which Thomas just failed to improve. Near the close, Lees made another fine run but was poorly supported and Oldham escaped. Final : Oldham, 2 goals 2 tries (10 points) ; Warrington, 2 goals 1 try (7 points).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN'S FIRST VICTORY. Runcorn and Swinton met at Swinton, before 1,000 spectators. The home team included Downing, late of Llanelly. The visitors held the whip hand tlitoughout. Runcorn first established a lead, and they were never seriously assailed. The first try was put on by Watton, Riley failing. Off-side play by Runcorn led to Townson kicking a goal with the penalty allowance. . . _</p>
        <p>_ . In the second half Unsworth twice did the trick for the visitors, and Deakin and Williams each negotiated a try, while three goals were piloted by Moran. Result : —Runcorn 3 goals and 5 tries (21 points), Swinton, 1 goal (2 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WIDNES BEAT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>WIDNES BEAT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 4,000 spectators. Both teams were well represented. G. Aspey atarted, and Widnes at once attacked. The backs combined well.and for the first few minutes the visitors defence had a warm time of. it. - A high punt by Alf. Taylor was mulled by C. Creevey, and Barber picking up smartly, scored at the corner. Davies failed at the goal kick. Just before the interval St. Helens got into the home " 25 " and after some scrambling play in front of goal, Drake passed out to Manchester, who scored. J. Creevey placed a goal. Half-time score :—St. Helens 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), Widnes 1 try (3 points).— Early in the second half Barber broke away, and kicked across for Carr to score. The shot at goal failed, and then Davies intercepted a pass and ran in a try at the corner. No goal resulted. Widnes kept up the attack and Sam Aspey scored two more tries, and Davies placed a goal. Result :—Widnes 1 goal and 5 tries (17 points), St. Helens 1 goal and 1 try (5 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION.</p>
        <p>OLDHAM." A" V. WARRINGTON "A."—These teams met at Watersheddings on Saturday. Warrington com- menced, and soon forced the locals to defend. Fine passing, initiated by Daintith, led up to Kenyon com- pleting a try. Oldham retaliated, and Tetlow kicked a goal for the home side. 'turning sound, the Oldham men took the lead, when Taylor crossed the line, but as the game progressed the Warringtonians asserted themselves, and the forwards affording the backs plenty of scope, Daintith scored another clever try, the final record leading as follows :—Warrington, two tries (six points) ; Oldham, one goal one try (five points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL. WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. Fairfield Burtonwood St. Paul's 5 4 1 0 13 6 8 Oughtrington Park _ _ _</p>
        <p>Lymm Warrington Central Monks' Hall A.F.C.4 1 2 1 4 14 3 Penketh Latchford 2 1 1 0 4 3 2 Palatine Buttonwood Social Rylands' A.F.C. 4 1 3 0 5 21 2 Warrington St. Paul's 3 0 3 0 3 10 0 St. Barnabas</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. Orford A.F.O 5 4 0 1 14 4 9 St. Elphin's A.F.C. Warrington Central Has 4 3 1 0 9 9 6 Walker's A.F.C. Warrington St. John's 4 2 2 0 8 6 4 St. Mary's Oughtrington Park Res 3 1 1 1 4 5 3 Fairfield Reserves 3 1 2 0 3 7 2 Monks, Hall Reserves Arpley Results for October sth. Warrington St. Paul's 1, Rylands' 3. Burtonwood Social 1, Monks' Hall 1. Lymrn 1. Burtonwood St. Paul's 2. St. Barnahas 2, Fairfield 4. Fairfield Reserves 0, St. Mary's 1. St. John's 0, Oughtrington Park Reserves 1. Monks, Hall Reserves 2, St. Elphin's 4. Orford 1, Walker's 1. Central Reserves 2, Arpley 1. _ _ .</p>
