      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH 2ND V. WEST DER BY 2ND Stockton Heath Second eleven entertained West Derby Second at Arpley on Saturday last, acd gave the latter team a thorough good beating. It must be said, however, in fairness to the visitors that they only played with 10 men. In spite of this fact, the home team played excellent hockey. "The Heathens "won the toss, and defended the pavilion goal. Immediately after the" bully-off " the home forwards, after good passing, attacked the Yisitors' goal, and Cook was very unfortunate in missibg the net by a few inches. Houghton, however, made a fine effort which resulted in the first goal being registered for Stockton Heath. Smith made a very good run and, from an awkward position, scored. The Visitors' forwards then attacked tbe Stockton goal, but Hawtin and Silcock proved too much for them. Pierpoint after a long dribble centred but the Derby bucks cleared just in time. The visitors scored just before half time. After the interval 11Ieredith made a fine effort and passed to Smith who shot, this making the fifth goal for the homesters. Rathbone played a splendid rush- ing game, and broke through the Derby defence and scored. Cook again scored. After a good Tun Hodgkin- son registered another goal for Stockton Heath. Smith was really in his best form again taking the ball from Meredith and scoring just in time. The play of Stockton Heath throughout the game was far superior to that of the visitors' and the result is certainly not in excess of the merit deserved by the local team. Result : Stockton 9, West Derby 1 goal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>AVIUMN WORK.</p>
        <p>There is something peculiarly rejuvenating about the autumn, and the Churches, in com- mon with the great world around, are not slow to feel it. During the summer things ecclesiastical have necessarily shown traces of the holiday spirit. It is useless to attempt big indoor meetings when the sunshine is "gladdening the heart of man," and waste of effort to initiate new schemes when people are away on holidays. There is one excep- tion to this rule, viz., work in the open air. A great deal of vigour has been put into work in the open air during the last four months. True, the weather for a great part of the season has not been propitious, but, in spite of that, open-air services have met with wonderful success this rear in many places. Open-air services do not seem to have suffered so much as picnics and open-air demonstrations, which in many places have been quite ruined by the unseasonable weather. Many a Free Church secretary has vowed never to arrange another garden fete or summer outing after some un- fortunate experience this season. Even the splendid weather of September has not allayed these feelings. However, this is all over now, and, with the advent of October, things take a new turn.</p>
        <p>PREPARING FOR THE SEASON.</p>
        <p>In many places earnest preparation is being made for autumn and winter work. It is becoming more and more the custom for Churches and Free Church organisations to hold a series of prayer meetings and confer- ences at the beginning of October to inau- gurate the season's work. This is at it should be, and the most casual glance around must convince us Free Churchmen that we have need of all the earnestness •and working qualities at our command for the coming campaign.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>EDUCATION AGAIN</head>
        <p>EDUCATION AGAIN</p>
        <p>It seems that the Education problem is eternally with us. "Tell it not in Gath," but personally we are sick of the business, and surely many more would confess to the same sensation; but, of course, we mu.st continue our efforts until we arrive at a just settle- ment, and Free Churchmen are not the sort of people to desist their, efforts until they are crowned with success. During the past few weeks there has been considerable discussion on the form which the Education Bill of next session would take. For some time past the committee of Free Church Members of Par- liament have been discussing the situation, with the result that a scheme was prepared, embodying the various points which the M.P.'s thought essential to the proposed' measure. Of course, this scheme was issued solely on the authority of• the body from which it emanated. The National Free Churoh Council has since considered the whole subject.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>REV. F. R. MEYER'S MINISTRY</head>
        <p>REV. F. R. MEYER'S MINISTRY</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A.? has Made a splendid start of his ministry in connection with the National Free Church Council. He devotes about a week to each place be visits, and manages during that time to touch on all phases of Free Church life. His first series of meetings was held at Leicester, . and since then he has been meeting with remarkably enthusiastic welcomes in the various centres. Mr. Meyer's arrangements are now booked up for a considerable time ahead, including a visit to Australia next year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>FREE CHLMCH PRESIDENT-ELECT.</head>
        <p>FREE CHLMCH PRESIDENT-ELECT.</p>
        <p>Considerable curiosity has been felt in Free Church circles as to who would succeed Dr. Rendel Harris next March in the presidency of the National Free Church Council. It will be remembered that last year the nomi- nation fell on Dr. John Watson ("lan Maclaren"), but the sad death of that dis- tinguished writer threw upiin the committee the task of finding another nominee for the position. I now learn that the choice has fallen upon Dr. David Brook, M.A. Dr. Brook is the o-nly Nonconformist , minister possessing the Oxford D.C.L., the qualifying thesis being "The Influence of Christianity on Roman Law.," Dr. Brook, who was born in 1854, is an ex-president of the United Methodist Free Church, and has, done con- spicuous service, not only to his own denomi- nation, but, to the Free Churches at large.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>HOW TO LOCALISE</head>
        <p>HOW TO LOCALISE</p>
        <p>The present is a fitting time to consider the question of new ventures, and amongst these many Churches are considering the question of a local magazine. An article in this month's Free Church Chronicle is pecu- liarly appropriate. Mr. B. A. M. Alger, Editor of the Derby Free Churchman, writes on "How to run a Localised Magazine." He shows how, in the case of the Derby Free Church Council, a splendid magazine has been produced and Circulated, With consider- able financial and other profit. It may be mentioned that the National Free "Church Council has just produced an advanced copy of the January issue Of the Free .Churchman, which it issues specially for localising. This, together with the programme for 1908, and full information on the subject of running a localised magazine, can be obtained on appli- cation to the Manager, the Publication De- paftment, National Free Church Council, Memorial Hall, London, E.C.</p>
