      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor a guarantee of good faith. -- Free Churches and Municipal Elections:</p>
        <p>Sir,—Once again the time has come to prepare for the Municipal Elections, upon the result of which so much depends. May I therefore be allowed the hospitality of your columns to urge this matter upon the consideration of Free Churchmen and not less upon Free Church women. For attention needs to be called to the fact that this year for the first time women are eligible for membership of our Municipal Councils. It is important to note,however, that only those whose names are on the Burgess roll can serve in this way. Women councillors can be of special service to the school chil- dren whose welfare increasingly depends upon our local authol ities. General educational adminstration continues to need a watchful eye especially in prospect of an early amend- ment of the Act of 1902. In poor districts the Pro- vision of Meals Act will need to be wisely administered. The Housing Question so intimately bound up with the physical and moral welfare of the people, also requires sympathetic hhndling. It must also be the business of our Councils to main- tain a high standard as employers of labour directly and indirecty. Municipal servants have a right to demand fair conditions of employment from the repre- sentatives of the community. I trust Free Churchmen may be to the front in civic service.—Yours faithfully, THOMAS LAW. Secretary, National Free Church Council, October 2nd, 1907.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Beautiful Warrington.</head>
        <p>Beautiful Warrington.</p>
        <p>My dear Beautiful Warrington Children, Now is the time to begin thinking about the part you will take in the next Bulb show. "Why " you say, "that is in Spring and Spring is a long time off." I know it is,but I am anxious that everyone of you should exhibit a plant at that show, and the ugly brown bulbs have to be hidden in Mother Earth, many, many weeks before the beautiful flowers come forth to brighten your homes with their sweet smelling loveliness. Now is the time to begin growing the bulbs. You will see the seedmen's windows packed with many kinds of bulbs, and in the market at the gardener's stalls they are also on sale. You can plant a bulb in a jam pot and you need not even have soil, sawdust will do. It must be well damped and drained, so as not to be too moist, and the bulb planted twice its own depth. I The substitutes on the home side did not distinguish If you use soil, you must lighten it with themselves, both Jenkins and Jordan playing a sand or cocoa fibre, so that the little prisoner may push its way out. You need not, indeed have anything poor game. The Welshman has not yet got back to at all to plant it in, but just get a pickle jar or jam pot his last season's form by a long way. Cartwright with a mouth about as big as the bulb, and keep it half played a fair game at centre 'considering the few full of water, and place the bulb in the mouth of the opportunities he had of attacking, and I hope be will jar. Then you will see it send its roots down, and its soon have another trial. Isherwood was about the leaves up. And if you cannot afford a bulb you can ask best of the home backs and did an immense amount of your mother to let you grow an onion. But whatever work. Dickenson was the worst and once more you grow must be put in a dark cool place—a cellar if proved my contention that he is no use on the win g. possible—till the shoots are about two to five inches high, or else the green leaves will take all the strength The forwards were beaten all through, and in this out of the bulb, and you will get no flowers or very department the Club could do with some new blood. small ones. What a wonderful thing a bulb is ; such a lot of wisdom is wrapped up in it. After a hyacinth, , It was forward and half-buck where Oldham were for instance, has flowered, you may see the long most superior. White and Beynon were both smart straggling green leaves spread over the woods, behind the scrum, the new man showing up well in carpeting them like thick grass. But those leaves are attacking movements. " Dicky " Thomas was very very busy. They fade away by degrees, but not until, re e aty no means a back and showed that h h ib unseen under the ground, they have stored up inside a bulb, the food for the new flowers. That is why a bulb passenger. will grow with only water—it feeds upon itself. Do -- you not think men and women and children should be The following is the Northern League table as wise as the flowers ? up-to-date : There is a great virtue called " thrift "that boys and girls should cultivate, for you can't begin too young. Money people confuse being thrifty with being miserly, but they are quite different. To be miserly is to store up things just for the sake of keeping them. To be thrifty is to store them for future use and greater beauty. For instance, if your mothers were not thrifty you could not blossom out at Walking Day into beautiful new clothes. It is only by wisely storing things that we can wisely use them. Will you children try to be thrifty like the bulbs ? You can each have a savings bank book and forms on which you can put penny stamps. I know it is a struggle not to spend pennies on toffee, but just think, the pennies will grove into shillings, and some day the shillings into a beauti- ful bright sovereign. Mother Nature saves up for us her children and the farmer has just finished harvesting for our future use, what She has saved. Even the squirrel has been busy storing up nuts. He says : "Winter is coming, and trees will be bare, Yet I shall need something to eat, I store up some nuts in holes in the woods, In winter my nuts are a treat." Yes, children, we have many things yet to learn from flowers and animals.—Yours affectionately, V.M.T.</p>
        <p>For. Agst. Per- P. W. L. D. Pts. G. T. P. G. T. P. c't'ge. Hunslet 5 5 0 0 10 11 11 55 3 4 18 10000 Oldham 6 6 0 0 12 18 21 99 5 7 31 10000 Wigan 6 5 1 0 10 11 15 67 8 7 37 8333 ; York Broughton 5 4 1 0 813 13 65 8 7 37 80'00 Bramley Barrow Halifax Salford Dewsbuty 5 3 2 0 6 710 44 11 10 52 6000 Hull K. R 5 3 2 0 613 10 56 5 6 28 60.00 Keighley 5 3 2 0 6 910 48 6 6 30 6000 Warrington 5 3 2 0 610 15 65 5 5 25 6000 Hull 7 4 3 0 819 17 89 10 10 50 5714 Batley 6 3 3 0 6 4 6 26 9 6 36 5000 Leeds ! Wakefield 5 2 3 0 411 16 70 11. 11 55 40'00</p>
        <p>Leigh Widnes 5 2 3 0 4 717 65 816 64 40-00 Swinton Bradford Huddersfield 5 1 4 0 2 4 4 20 910 48 20.00 Runcorn St. Helens 6 1 5 0 2 3 7 27 10 21 92 16.66 Rochdale 5 0 5 0 0 3 6 24 14 13 67 00.00 Ebbw Vale 5 0 5 0 0 2 5 19 14 26 106 00.00 Merthyr</p>
        <p>With two successive defeats it behoves Warrington to pull themselves together. They have a severe task before them to-day in playing the first round of the Lancashire Cup at Runcorn. The fact that they have recently beaten Runcorn with ease is nothing to go by, for the Linnets were then in the dumps. On Saturday they swamped Swinton at Swinton and will no doubt have a hot 80 minutes in store for Warrington to-day.</p>
        <p>I do not at all favottr the selection of the team for to-day's match. It does not seem very progressive to go back to the threequarter line that was in its prime four or five years ago. Dickenson did not earn his place last Saturday, and the Committee are evidently relying on his reputation for cup-tie play. He will have to run more and kick less to do any good at Runcorn. Harris was thought a back number a few weeks ago, and seeing that he has only played one game in about 12 months it seems rather a gamble to place him on the team. However, with Taylor an absentee through injury, the Committee had a diffi- culty to contend with. I hope the result will justify the experiments.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team will be : Tilley, full back; Fish, Isherwood,Dickenson and Harris, three-quarters; Lees and Brooks, half-backs; Thomas, Boardman, Shugars, Heath, Belton and Harmer, forwards.</p>
        <p>While watching the New Zealanders on Wednesday, it occured to me that Fish will have to look to his laurels if he wishes to represent England and do so worthily. There are several men in the" All Blacks" team who could give him a start and beat him on present form. I hope he will do some serious training and endeavour to secure the highest honours of his career this season.</p>
        <p>The " A" team accomplished a fine performance on Saturday by winning at Oldham 6-5, and with such men as Jordan, Cartwright, Boardman, Harris and Jenkins all absent. They played a remarkably fine game, Kenyon and Daintith both scoring tries. Harrison, the new wing, gave a good account of him- self and showed plenty of pace. What he lacks is experience. This fine win should put Warrington " A " well in the running again for the Combination championship.</p>
        <p>To-day the "A " team will oppose Salford St. Bart's with Jordan, full back ; Landor, Cartwright, Kenyon and Harrison, threequarters ; Jenkins and Daintith, half backs ; Naylor, Jolley, Bradshaw, J. Boardman, Hewitt, and Holbrook forwards.</p>
        <p>I hear that Warrington are in negotiations for a Welsh full back, who is highly spoken of in Rugby Union circles and plays with a class team.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up-to-date as follows : Aot. _</p>
        <p>For</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G.T.P. Pts. Warrington 3 2 1 0 614 54 2 210 5 Leigh Wigan St. Helens Widnes 1 0 1 0 0 3 9 3 521 0 Runcorn</p>
        <p>The Northern Union General Committee held a meeting at Leeds on Wednesday night, Hr. H. Ashton presiding. An application was read from New South Wales to send a team over to play Northern Union clubs next season. It was decided to circularise the clubs in membership, in order to ascertain whether such a team would be welcomed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>I WESLEYAN CHAPEL.—The annual services of thanks- giving for the safe ingathering of the harvest were held in the above chapel on Sunday and attracted large con- gregations. The festival continues to grow in popularity. The Chapel had been tastefully decorated by the lady members, giving the sacred edifice a bright and attrac- tive appearance. In the morning the pulpit was occupied by the Rev. Thomas Miller, and the choir gave an excellent rendering of an anthem entitled " Ye shall dwell in the Land." Mr. Frank Ireland sang the bass solo and Miss C. Atherton took the soprano solo. The singing of both, thanks to the training of the organist, Mr. G. Weaver, has reached a high standard. The pulpit in the evening was occupier] by the Rev. E. J. Gawne, and the congregation was so large that chairs had to be put down the aisle. The choir took for their anthem " Sing to the Lord of Harvest" when Mr. H. I Pluronton (bass) took the solo. The collections at both services were in aid of the church funds and were an increase on those realized last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>MR. BIRRELL TO VISIT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>MR. BIRRELL TO VISIT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Our readers will be pleased to hear that the Right Hon. Augustine Birrell, KC., M.P., secretary for Ireland, has consented to address a Liberal meeting in the Parr Hall some time in December. Mr. Birrell, who is an incisive and entertaining speaker, is the son of the Rev. Charles Birrell, Nonconformist minister, and was born near Liverpool in 1850. He was educated at Amersham Hall School, Trinity Hall, Cambridge, graduating at Cambridge in 1872. He became a barrister in 1875 and was appointed Quain Professor of Law at University College, London in 1890. The honorary degree of L.L.D. has been con- ferred upon him by St. Andrew's University. Mc. Birrell is a prolific writer, his works including "Obiter Dicta," " Life of Charlotte Bronte," " ResJudicatae," "Men, Women and Books," "Lectures on the Duties and Liabilities of Trustees." He has also edited an edition of Boswell's " Life of Johnson." His chief recreations are pedegtrianistn, golf, and book-hunting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>BOY'S SHOCKING DEATH. -- Whirled Round a Shafting.</head>
        <p>BOY'S SHOCKING DEATH. -- Whirled Round a Shafting.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. A. Jones, deputy coroner, held an—inquast at the Warrington Police Station on Wednesday afternoon touching the death of John Plumbley Wilicock, aged fourteen, son of George Willcock, wire drawer, 7, Orford lane,who was killed whilst following his employ- ment at the Warrington Wire Rope Works,Bewsey-road, on Monday.—fr. Browne appeared for the firm and Mr. T. S. Steel for the relatives. Mr. R. Eatock, factory inspector, was also present. Mr. Browne said he was instructed by the directors of the company to state that this was the first fatal accident which had occurred at the works for fifty years and no one regretted the affair more than they did. They had done everything in their power to prevent accidents and they would give all the assiatance they could to the jury to elucidate the facts. Frank Stokes, 13.', years of age, 10, Buckley-street, a wire strand spinner employed at the Wiie Rope Works, stated that at 4.5 on Monday afternoon he was at work when the belt which drove his machine came off the bottom pulley. Deceased, who was working at the next machine, came to him and offered to hold the belt. Witness gave him the belt anti deceased shook it to get it off the top pulley. The belt was held in position by a fork or guide. The loose belting got entangled with deceased's legs, and at the same time slipped off the top pulley and began to wind round the shafting. Deceased was drawn upwards, legs first, and wound round the shafting, his head and legs striking the wall many times. Witness rang the alarm bell, the engine was stopped, and assistance came immediately. In answer to Mr. Steel witness said there were three men looking after the machines and they were supposed to put the belts on if they happened to slip off. The boys, however, put them on when the men were not looking. There were two little bars to prevent the belt slipping off •, but he did not consider they were long enough. About three minutes elapsed before the engine stopped and the lad was carried round the shafting twenty or thirty times. Mr. Browne said he was instructed that not a minute passed before the engine was stopped and the boy was pulled down. The shafting only worked at the rate of 60 revolutions per minute. Before Stokes left the box, the Deputy Coroner com- plimented him upon the way in which he had given his evidence. He had given a very clear account of the accident and if he continued to show such intelligence he would make his way in the world. Robert Henry Holford, fitter at the works, deposed to hearing the alarm bell and having the engine stopped and deceased pulled down. The deceased was dead when he arrived at the Infirmary. The shaft revolved 66 times per minute. Mr. Eatock : Do you consider a belt on a revolving shaft dangerous? Witness: There are times when it is dangerous. Mr. Eatock : Could you not put a hanger on the belt to prevent it dropping off the shaft in case it slipped off the pulley? Witness: Not very conveniently. I have not seen anything of the kind in my experience, but possibly that .could be done.</p>
        <p>. Witness alsod amitted that tbe coupling might be fenced, and thus additional protection would be secured.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne intimated that the firm would be pleased to carry out any suegestions of the factory inspector. It was, however, only fair to say that the accident did not occur on the coupling side. The Deputy-Coroner said he did hot blame the deceased, as he was doing a kindly act, and it was also the only way in which ha could learn the workings of the place. He suggested that they might safely leave the matter of preventing future accidents in the hands of the factory inspector and the firm. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned.</p>
        <p>Woods' Great Peppermint Care, for Coughs and Colds ; never fails. ln P.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
