      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Night of the Party."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Night of the Party."</p>
        <p>The title of this play suggests something lively and entertaining, and the patrons of the Royal Court Theatre have not been disappointed in their expecta- , tions during the present week. "The Night of the Party" is surely the funniest piece staged since the never-to-be-forgotten " Charley's Aunt." No one can wonder at it hdving such a prolonged run at the Avenue Theatre, London, for Mr. Weedon Grossmith can he relied upon to get the best out of a good thing —and a good thing in its way is certainly .` The Night of the Party." It abounds in farcical situations which provoke uproarious and continual mirth. Yet there is no buffoonery, but instead an inexhaustable supply of real humour. Briefly told, the tale is this : Mr. Crosbie, servant to Mr. Frank Frayne, invites a few select " Servatorial " friends to a party the night his master goes abroad. Every- thing is going much merrier than a marriage bell when the master returns unexpectedly, having missed his train. A general scramble ensues, and the " party " are hidden away in the room, Crosbie alone being on view, slightly hilarious, when. Mr. Frayne returns in a bad temper. Then the fun begins, and Crosbie is only saved by the arrival of Lady Hampshire, an admirer of Frayne's, at this late hour of the night. An affecting interview takes place in view of the concealed members of the "party," one of whom, covered by a dust sheet on the sofa, her lady- ship sits on in an emotional moment. Discovery is avoided by the ingenious Crosbie, who next appears in the guise of his master at Hadston Hall, a fashionable hotel in the country with shoot- ing and golf attached. Here ensue complica- tions of the most comical order, in which most of the members of the party participate. Crosbie gets into such scrapes that his master, Frayne, has to keep up the illusion and change places with him on returning to town. Here we have the denouement, which is screamingly funny, and shows Crosbie as the consumnate artist of the piece. Lady Hampshire's honour is saved,and also the reputation of poor Frayne, in the most dexterous and laughable manner. Miss Mab Paul and company are to be congratulated on a capital representation of the farce. The fun never flags, and the " business " goes with a fine swing from start to finish. Miss Paul, as Gipsy Vandelier, is immense in more senses than one. A lady of uncommon stature and handsome presence, she adds to her attractions by uery vivacious acting. Her smart attire too, does not escape the attention of the ladies. Chief honours go naturally to Mr. Aubrey Cheshire, the hero of the play, in the fine part of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>BACKHAUS CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE BACKHAUS CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The first visit of Backhaus to Warrington on. Wednesday evening was a musical event of consider- able importance, aud we were glad to note that it I attracted a large and representative audience. The Parr IHall was not full, but the gathering was such as to prove that a classical concert in Warrington need not be despaired of by its promoters. With the rapid growth of more frivolous entertainment in the town, it is essential that the provision of occasional high-class concerts should be encouraged, and we are sure the musical public will be grateful to Mr. R. H. Pearson for having arranged the visit of Backhaus and party. The world-renowned pianist was accompanied by Miss Ellen Forena, soprano, and Herr Adolf Rebner, violinist, with Miss Louise Pekett as accompanist. The presence of three soloists gave a pleasing variety to the programme and variety, we are told, is the very spice of life. Of course, it may be that the pianistic gourmand would prefer to sit and listen to Backhaus for a solid two hours without a break, but we think the majority can appreciate the playing of any artiste much more fully when his appearance on the platform alternates with that of others. We need hardly say that the concert was a success musically ; so much might be presumed from the artistes and the programme. Backhaus played magnifi- cently though without creating quite the furore that Paderewski did on the same platform. Possibly his leonine locks are not quite up to the Paderewskian standard, at any rate though bushy enough they have not quite the same chrysanthemum-like appearance. We do not know how much genius or inspiration is derivable from the wearing of the hair in this fashion, but it seems to be de rigour in virtuoso circles. This persiflage notwithstanding there was an abundance of melody in Backhaus' fingers, and his recital aroused great enthusiasm in the audience. He made two appearances and was enthusiastically recalled on each occasion. Perhaps the work which appealed most to the audience was the Appasionata sonata in F Minor (Beethoven), a tremendous piece to memorise and execute with the brilliance which distinguished Backhaus' rendering. The sonata is over 100 years old, and may be considered an undying creation. Each of the three movements is great in its way, and there is no doubt about the spirit of dignity, passion, and magnificence which the work breathes. Later in the evening the pianist gave a lengthy selection from Chopin, of whose works he is so finished an exponent. No less than eight compositions were rendered in suc- cession,punctuated only by the applause of the audience and the bow of the performer. There was an infinite variety, of course, about the scherzo, fanta:,ie impromptu, mazurka, two studies, berceuse, valse and ballade, which comprised the selection, and the versa- tility, insight and genius of Backhaus were well exhibited. Miss Ellen Forena earned warm praise as a soprano singer. She has a beautiful voice, remark- able for softness and purity of tone, and her rendering of the trio of songs by Grieg, Hofman and J. Rogers I entitled (a)" Hidden Love," (b) " Longing," (e) "At Parting" was enhanced by the artistic conception of the singer. Herr Adolf Rebner, an artiste likewise new to Warrington audiences, captivated all with his technique and harmony on the violin. The Mozart concerto in D. (second and third movements) opened the programme, and Herr Rebner performed this exact- ing selection with distinction,the brilliant finale bring- ing down the house. We liked him even better in the " Garten melodie " (Schuman) and Bolero, op 30, (Sarasate) both of which are full of melody, grace and rhythm, thou4h essentially different. The soprano and violinist were admirably served by Miss Louise Peskett, a most capable and graceful accompanist.</p>
        <p>BACKHAUS AT WARRINGTON. This eminent and world famed pianist uses aChappell Grand Pianoforte at all his concerts. This fact in itself places Chappell's in the forefront of pianoforte manufacturers and their pianos are the chosen instru- ments of the most renowned judges in the musical world The one and only place in Warrington and district where Chappell pianos can be obtained is Wadsworth's, 59, Sankey-street. Inspection is invited. These pianos will be offered at wholesale prices for cash during the season.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>WHALES IN DOVER STRAITS.</head>
        <p>WHALES IN DOVER STRAITS.</p>
        <p>The captain of the Government tug Langdon reports that whilst engaged in towing targets in the Straits of Dover be noticed four immense fish swimming up the channel. Their bodies were about four feet above the water as they swam. The shells from the Dover batteries had apparently scared them, as they were swimming at a great pace. PROFITABLE COW.</p>
        <p>The Dairy Show at the Agricultural Hall attracted a large number of people. Some of the totals made on Wednesday were enormous, one cow belonging to Mr. George Taylor, of Hounslow, yielding 704 pounds of milk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>IE HARRISS.</head>
        <p>IE HARRISS.</p>
        <p>Crosbie. He was the life and soul of the fun, and the prime mover in all the mischief. Mr. Cheshire is to be complimented on his talent for humour and capacity for keeping the ball rolling at an exhilarating pace. Miss Jenny Lynne made a handsome and gracious Lady Hampshire. She excelled in the love passages with Mr. Frayne, who was dashingly impersonated by Mr. N. Carter Slaughter. We cannot refrain from specially praising Mr. G. Edward Hall's creation of the part of Hr. Flamber, the pompous head servant to the Duke of Hungerford. His Grace himself could not have been more exclusive, and Mr. Flamber's skill in illustrating the distinction between the" hinner and the houter circle" was beyond all praise. His was indeed a finishsd performance. Bogle (Hr. Stanley Arthur) was irresistibly funny,and no fault could be found with Mr. William Allison as Alderman Hargen, or Mr. Percy Robinson as Mr. Percy Roundle (footman to Lord Dalston). The part of Paulina (housemaid to Hr. Frayne) is also a good one and it was interpreted in lively fashion by Miss Lillian Williams. We might go through the cast and still fairly distribute praise, but space forbids. Suffice it to say that those who do not see " The Night of the Party" this week will have missed the best laugh of the season.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. "PRINCE OF PILSEN."</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. "PRINCE OF PILSEN."</p>
        <p>An engagement or an unusually attractive nature has been secured for nest week in the shape of BAron Harriss's famous "Prince of Pilsen" Co.,which numbers nearly 70 artistes. When first produced two years ago at the Shaftesbury Theatre, London, this merriest of musical comedies, like its celebrated predecessor, "The Belle of New York," which it closely resembles, become the rage of the London season, by reason of its ear-haunting melodies, its stirring choruses, graceful dances, and its well sustained humorous plot. At the head of the company is Miss Sophie Harriss, a lady of great personal attractions, who has won renown in America and Australia, in addition to having been the bright particular star of many London successes. Rumour bath it that this lady's gorgeous gowns are the very " dernier cri " in the world of fashion, while her collection of jewels includes several historic gems which were formerly the property of the Empress Eugenie. Other principal members of the caste are Harry Kilburn, the famous comedian of "Lady Slavey," "Dandy Fifth," and "Gay Parisienne " fame, and Frank Crimp, the celebrated baritone of D'Oyly Carte renown. Messrs. Lindon Lang, Charles Clyde, Ernest Gifford, and Bernard Russell are other well-known names, whilst Misses Ernestine Desborough, Connie Leon, Hazel Saqui, and Mollie Wynne are well remembered. Miss Kitty Albert, a young lady of remarkable beauty of figure enacts the " Gibson Girl " made famous by Oamille Clifford, and an old favourite, we note, Mr. Basil Horsfall is musical director for Baron Harriss. Mr. Hugh Fleming, so long associated with the Sarah Bernhardt tours, is advance director, and Mr. Alfred Edwardes, the well-known impressario, is general manager for Baron Harriss.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH GARDEN SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH GARDEN SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>Annual Show of Chrysanthemums.</p>
        <p>The third annual show of chrysanthemums under the auspices of the above Society took place at the Church Institute,Victoria Square, Stockton Heath,on Thursday. There was a very good attendance, although the weather was not all that could be desired.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Broadbent," The Latch ford performed the opening ceremony. She said she was very pleased to have been asked by the Committee to open that show of chrysanthemums for she was greatly interested in the Society. The Society was doing much good in making the neighbourhood of Stockton Heath beautiful and in encouraging the love for gardening. Gardening was the greatest home pleasure, indeed it was a refreshment. She understood that it was the third show of chrysanthemums which she had very great pleasure in opening. (Applause.) The secretary had asked her to announce that Lady Greenall had most kindly presented to the Society a silver bowl and Messrs. Eustance a rose bowl. The prizes were each valued at five guineas. She was informed that some children were disappointed because there was no juvenile class this year, but she hoped there would be one at the next show. Children took a very deep interest in gardening and arrangements were being made for prizes to be given for the best collection of leaves and berries collected by children and she hoped to give a prize for a similar exhibit next year (Applause.) Mr. C. Plinston (secretary) proposed a vote of thanks to Mrs. Broadbent for her attendance. Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins seconded and the motion was carried unanimously. A vote of thanks was accorded to the judges (Messrs. I Boulton and Wilkinson) on the motion of Mr. Hickman seconded by Ur. Fawcett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>AWARDS.</head>
        <p>THE AWARDS.</p>
        <p>The following is the list of awards :-Class Bestl- variety of sprays, three of each: 1, J. Owen; 2, J. Mottram ;3,E. W. Whitehouse. Class 2-three varie- ties of sprays, three of each: 1, E. Vernon ; 2, W. A. Summerfield ; 3, 1-1. Lyon. Class 3-one spray, any variety: 1, W. Skitter ; 2, W. A. Timper- ley ; 3, H. Hargreaves. Class 4-six single varie- ties, single stems: 1, A. J. Mottram ; 2, J. Owen ; 3, W. A. Timperley. Class 5-three single varieties, sterns: 1, H. Lyon; 2, A. J. Mottrans ; 3, J. Owen. Class 6-three sprays in bloom, three varieties : 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, G. Williams. Class 7-one spray in; bloom : 1, E. Vernon; 2, D. Wilkinson 3, E. W. Whitehouse. Class 8 -one variety of chrysanthemum: arranged for effect: 1, J. Moore; 2, Miss Plinston 3, Miss Hargraves. Class 9-any number of varieties: 1, J. Moore; 2, E. Vernon; 3, Miss Plinston. Class 10- epergne, ar ranged for effect, any number of varieties 1, Miss Hargraves; 2, A. J. Mottram. Class 11-porn- pome, three varieties in sprays : 1, H. Lyon ; 2, E. W. Whitehouse. Class 12-one spray, any variety: 1, E. Vernon; 2, E. W. Whitehouse. Class 14-any other variety : 1, J. Owen ;2, F. Broady; 3,liss Hargraves; h c, Mynekyme and J. Moore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>Angling Contest and Prize Distribution.</head>
        <p>Angling Contest and Prize Distribution.</p>
        <p>The second angling competition of the season in con- nection with Crosfield's Recreation Club took place on Saturday at Thelwall. The weather, though cold was enjoyable and the sport, considering the lateness of the season, very fair. Out of over 50 entries, no fewer than 21 qualified for a prize, the highest catch being F. Hankinson's, 5 ozs. 14 drms. The next five in order were : —J. Holden, A. Overend, J. Langholm, J. Lawton and A. Brobbin. Mr. J. Newall officiated as weigher-in. There was a good attendance in the dining-hall on Thursday evening when the prizes, some of which were of considerable value, were distributed. Captain G. it. Crosfield, who distributed the awards, in the course of a humorous speech observed that unlike his brothers, he could claim only a limited acquaintance with fishing, his chief practice in that sport having been gained in angling in season and out of season for adherents to the National Service League. He created some amusement by recalling the exploits in that direction of a certain friend of his—who played and eventually landed a real 25 pounder honorary vice-president for life. That was a piece of luck which he (Captain Crosfield) would also like to experience.</p>
        <p>Lay on, Macdnff ! who hasn't read How bold Macbeth was slain? But now he's been so long since dead Why rake him up again? Then let him rest, he's out of date,</p>
        <p>We'll turn to something newer, If you've a cold be not too late, Take Woods' Great Peppermint Cure.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>RAILWAY CRISIS.</head>
        <p>THE RAILWAY CRISIS.</p>
        <p>MR. G. J. WARDLE, M.P. ADDRESSES RAILWAYMEN A f WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Recognition Question.</p>
        <p>STIRRING APPEAL.</p>
        <p>In view of the present crisis in the railway world, great interest was taken in a public meeting promoted by the Warrington branch of the Amalgamated Society of Railway Servants which was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday evening. There was a large and enthusiastic gathering, Mr. R. Bebington, ot Manchester, a member of the Executive Committee, presiding. The principal speakers were Mr. G. J. Wardle, M.P., editor of the "Railway Review," and Mr. A. Bellamy, J.P. The CHAIRMAN said the national movement had been inaugurated to improve the conditions of the railway- men throughout the country. Railway work had too long been synonymous with long hours and low pay and the men were rousing themselves to a sense of duty to themselves and to their wives and families. The national movement was started as a result of many petitions submitted to the Executive Committee by the various grades in the services, and it was discovered that the best way to obtain redress ,would be in a wholesale fashion such as the "all gralles "programme embodied. The chief point of contention in that programme had narrowed itself down to the question of "recognition." Under the old method men who had obtained so-called concessions lost them again in consequence of having no direct means of placing a check on the companies through their society. Men who were badly treated would have to suffer until the whole railway service rose in conflict against it. Mr. Bebington then quoted figures to sho that the wages paid by a certain railway company compared very badly with the amounts paid to the employees on the Corporation tramways—in fact he was informed that on the railway system in question, with the exception of two stations, not a man obtained more than 235. a week. The railwaymen were determined on this occasion. They had had two or three attempts at working up a great national movement, and many of them who had been in the service over a quarter of a century were burning with indignation at the situation. (Applause.) Those who had the courage to stand up on previous occasions were told by the com- panies that there was the boardroom open to them to vent their grievances, and that was the end of it. There were on the railways to-day 100,000 men who received anything from 15s. to £1 a week. And then we spoke of physical deterioration ! (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>RESOLUTION.</head>
        <p>THE RESOLUTION.</p>
        <p>Mr. LOXTON moved : "That this meeting of railway- men expresses its deep regret at the arbitrary manner in which the overture to the railway companies has been received. We therefore heartily support the decision of the Executive in ordering a ballot of the men to obtain their opinions as to whether they will cease work at a given time. While recognising the seriousness of such action we feel there is no other way by which we can obtain our just demands." The workers, he maintained, had a grievance and it must be settled now or never. Tf they would all be of one mind the day was theirs, and success would come to-morrow (Applause.) _ _</p>
        <p>CLAitE seconded. MR. WARDLE'S STIRRING SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. J. WARDLE, M.P., said he could not say he rose with very great pleasure to support a resolution of that character. It was a serious resolution and if it was necessary to carry it into effect—they always lived in hope—it meant war, and wars—whether they were industrial war or wars between nations—were never to be undertaken lightly—(applause)—and without having seriously weighed the prospects of the fight. There were times, however, when in this country they had to face the question of an industrial war. They had no option. All the power to settle these matters rested in other hands than their's unless they took the step proposed. It was true that when two opposing forces were matched against each other, the nearer matched they were, the more likely it might be that there would be a fight. But when one side was strong and the other was weak, the result was a fore- gone conclusion. Hitherto the railwaymen of this country had been weak, because they had been divided. (Applause.) They</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>HAD BEEN WEAK</head>
        <p>HAD BEEN WEAK</p>
        <p>because they had not been organised into one solid and comprehensive union which comprised the whole of the railway service. There were signs, he was thankful to say, that a better spirit vies prevailing, and that the division might come to an end. There were certainly signs that it ought to come to an end. (Hear, hear.) The resolution expressed regret at the arbitrary manner in which the companies had acted so far with regard to the demands of the , men and especially at the way in which they had acted with regard to the request for discussion, conference and recognition. The railwaymen had a programme and they wanted to carry that programme out. (Hear, hear.) But they wanted to discuss it in a friendly spirit. The companies, however, said: " No. We will not hear from you, the Society. We will talk to our own men." Yes, they had been talking to their own men all the time and it was precisely because they had been talking to them, with all the force of organised capital behind them, that the men were in their present position. (Applause.) If they bad to have the olive brandh held out much longer, he was afraid even the patience of the most patient set of men in the universe would be at an end. Certainly it seemed to him,looking back on past history and knowing what he did of the condition of men employed upon the railways, that he had correctly described them when he said they were the most patient of the industrial workers of the country. (Applause.) Far be it from him to say a single word at this stage which would inflame passion or in any way make it more difficult to get a settlement. But it must be evident to everyone that there must come a time when patience would be exhausted, and that time, he ventured to say. was not far distant. (Applause.) The Execu- tive Committee who kad had charge of the movement had in their wisdom—and he thought they had done wisely—asked the men to say</p>
        <p>WHETHER THEY WERE IN EARNEST OR NOT, whether they meant business or not, whether they upon whom the burden would fall, who would have to face the struggle, were prepared for the struggle and were willing to undertake it. (Applause.) That was the position at the present moment. He was not going to tell them which way to sign their ballot papers. (Applause.) Let them use their discretion, but' remember what was at stake. (Applause.) He appealed to all who had not joined the Union to do so at once. They wanted that movement to be one solid movement of the whole of the railway servants, to e.trike once and for all for victory. (Applause.) Speaking entirely on 1 his own responsibility, he considered it would bee very great blessing for the railway servants if all their unions were federated, and would work hand in hand for the national all-grades movement. (Applause.) The 1 federation question would have to be settled, and the sooner the better it would be for all. There was only one way to settle the recognition question, and that was for them all to come inside the union or federate their forces. (Applause.) He was sorry to say his profound conviction was that they</p>
        <p>WOULD NOT GET RECOGNITION until they were strong enough to force it. (Hear, hear.) He was pleased to see that public opinion was with them. The Press was largely on their side, and he believed that if Parliament were sitting it would also be on their side. (Applause.) But against them, let it not be forgotten, was the strongest body of organised capita he believed there was in the world. They were hoping that seeing the Press, the country and the beminessmen were against them, the compsnies would wisely reconsider their decision and would at least hold out the olive branch so that the matter might be discussed round a table. One of the directors' conten- tions was that as yet the Union did not represent the majority of the men, in fact they would like to say it did not represent the men at all. Their reply had always been obvious. They of the Union represented those who were mgmbers of their Association and if they hsd their way they would be glad to</p>
        <p>LEAVE THE cormißs OUT OF ACCOUNT altogether. (Applause.) They would be perfectly willing that the condition of settlement should be that the terms which were given should be given to those who were members of the Union only. (Applause.) They knew, of course, that that was a vain hope. It would be too obviously playing into their hands. Nevertheless it was a complete answer to the statement that they did not represent the men. rheir movement was not for recognition only. It was to get better conditions of work, wages and hours for the men, and he ventured to assert without fear of contradiction that better conditions were never more needed than they were at the present moment. (Hear, hear.) The com- panies had been making bigger engines, bAilding larger trucks, making the men do more work and giving them not the slightest increase of wages. The men who worked on the railways should above all things have a reduction in their</p>
        <p>HOURS OF LABOUR. An eight hours day should be given to drivers, firemen, guards, shunters, signal men, and to motor men, con- ductors and gate men on electric railways. They asked that all the rest, with the exception of the platelayers who already had a 54 hour week, should have a ten hours day. On the question of hours the workers had abundant evidence that their demand was reasonable and it was the one thing that would do the most to sweeten the lives, improve the health, and make life better socially and in every other way for the railway men. (Applause.) Their other claim was for 2s. a week advance to all those to whom the eight hours day did not apply. Mr. Wardle then dealt at length with the dis- advantages attacking to permanency of employinent on the railways, pointing out that the present dispute was concerned with 220,000 or 240,000 men who were so engaged. One of the great things they had to do, he continued, with railways and Government departments</p>
        <p>,</p>
        <p>was to democratise them. (Applause.) They had to sweep away a great many of the prohibitions and bring about in permanency of employment a real liberty and freedom which workinamen ought to possess every- where. (Applause.) He could not tell them to what indignation he was moved by the stories of the lives of some railwaymen. It was positively</p>
        <p>NOTHING BUT SLAVERY from Monday morning to Saturday night—bed and work. In these days when unemployment was rampant, no man should be called upon to work more than his fair share. (Applause.) One of the great needs of the day was an industrial court where the men would get real justice in the matter of fines and punishments. He was convinced that the whole question of the organisa- tion of the railways had to come before the public gaze. There was to be a grand inquest before long into the management of he railways. The public would demand it. +Hear, hear.) There was plenty of money with which to pay the men well and give then reason- able conditions. Who stood in the ? It was not the directors only, or even chiefly in his opinion. The directors were led away by the management and the officials. (Applause.) Mr. Wardle concluded his speech with</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>STIRRING APPICA.L</head>
        <p>A STIRRING APPICA.L</p>
        <p>to the men. I ask you who belong to the Union, he said, in face of this great opportunity, what are you going to do? I ask you who do not belong to the Union, in face of this great opportunity, what are you going to do? Are you going to betray your fellows? Are you going to be content not only to be the laggards, because it does not mean that only—but to be the traitors ? (" No.") You cannot be neutral. If you don't help those who want to strike a blow for freedom you help those who want to fasten the chains on tighter. ( Applause.) In this battle no neutrality is possible. Those who ar3 not with us are against us. Those who join not hands in the fight and come to join the Union, by their passivity,by the fact that they remain at work. help to defeat us, if ever we should be dofeated. And so we appeal to you on every ground of humanity. We appeal to you for your own sakes, for the sake of your wives and children, for the future of industrial England, for the future of democracy, because behind this fight is the great fight for democratic control of all industry in this country. (Applause.) And we say to you now, will you be men or will you be slaves for ever and ever? (Loud applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>WHAT WILL WARRINGTON SAY?</head>
        <p>WHAT WILL WARRINGTON SAY?</p>
        <p>Arr. A. BELL ur, J.P., in supporting the resolution, said the railways ought to be worked in the interests of the community, but they were not. They ought to be of very material benefit to every industry, but at present they were a bar. The reason was that they were worked not in the interests of the public,but absolutely for divi- dends. He appealed to the men to do their duty when they got their ballot papers. Let Warrington sound no uncertain note on the question. (Applause.) If they missed the chance they had tp-day, they put back the clock of progress for fifty years. The opportunity would not be regained until hundreds and thousands of their fellowmen had had to suffer severely. In the interests of those they loved best, it was their duty to be straight in the matter. Lot the enthusiasm that prevailed in that meeting permeate the whole of the railway service in Warrington and from one end of the line to the other. Then he was convinced that the day would not be far distant when there would be no more adult labour on the railways at 15s. or 16s. a week. (Loud applause.) The resolution was carried unanimously with great enthusiasm.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME,</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME,</p>
        <p>The growing popularity of this place of amusement is evidenced by the large audiences which have assembled every evening during the past week. Those who enjoy a first class variety entertainment are admirably catered for, the programme undoubtedly being one of the best of its kind ever submitted in Warrington. Interest is _not allowed to flag for a moment and all who have had the pleasure of witnessing the entertainment have been I delighted with it. The Sisters Godfrey are acceptable I vocalists and very smart dancers, and an excellent turn is provided by the Delomes, duettists and champion dancers of the world, whose speciality "The phantom clogs" is a clever performance and well merits tho applause so unstintedly accorded. Madge Grey is a charming dialect comedienne and her mimicry is highly entertaining— in fact she would be hard to excel in this particular line. Shirley, Liggins and Co.. coloured vocalists, give a capital sketch" Farmyard pastimes," and songs by Rosalie Jacobi are well received. Prince Kokin, the famous Japanese juggler, displays his remarkable skill in a unique exhibition which holds the audience snellbound. Letine's impersonations are very fine, and Wild, Willie and West create great hilarity in their comedy, acrobatic trampoline and trap scene "The bounding navvies." The programme closes with some interesting animated pictures. Widdop and his orchestra are to be complimented upon their finished interpretations of "Light Cavalry" (Suppe) and" La Mousme " (Ganne).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</head>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>The management announce a programme of special interest for next ,veek. A novel feature will be Manrin's wonderful fountains which produce an extraordinary and beautiful effect. 20,000 gallons of water pour on the stage, playing under powerful electric lights. There are constant changes in the shapes of the water and in the colours thrown upon it, and the spectacle concludes with "The water nymphs," living statues being seen in the water. Captain Taylor will introduce his marvellous educated baboons which have been perfectly trained. The Brothers Adderley will appear in their comedietta " The officer's servant," and Willie Gardner, the American skate dancer on ice and roller skates, will provide a splendid turn." The other artistes will include Cullen and Carthy and Little Cluley, the popular comedians, Damerell and Rutland with their comical caper company in " House Full,"and Les Bollingers, the original rag painters of Paris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>INFIRMARY CONCERT.</head>
        <p>THE INFIRMARY CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The annual concert of the Workingmen's Committee in aid of the Warrington Infirmary was given last Saturday evening before an appreciative audience which though it filled the Parr Hall, was a somewhat thankless one f u. the various artistes to sing and play to. That so large a proportion were the merest juveniles is probably explained by the fact that many purchased tickets out of sympathy with the objects of the Concert who had no intention of attending it and that these people had handed on the tickets to whoever were willing to make use of them. Such an arrange- ment is certainly not in the interests of those who go with any serious intention of listening to the music to be presented, for the irrelevant interruptions and disturbances which they as well as the artistes experienced were as obvious as they were irritating. The vocalists were one and all well worth listening to. fie singing of the two ladies Miss Felicia Sutton and Madame Annie Walker in "My heart is weary" and "It was a dream" respectively, being of a character which realised the full meaning of these two fine songs. Miss Sutton, who is, of course, better known in Warrington, is to be especially congratulated up-in a marked advance in style and method and in the evenness of her vocal range. Mr. Ditchburn and Mr. Rabbitt are both old favourites at this and kindred concerts. The first mentioned declaims songs of the type of "The Windmill" in the most appropriate of ba.Bo profando voices and never fails to ingratiate himself with an audience, while Mr. Rabbitt's obvious earne3tness and determination to make the mast of what he sets himself to do vocally,appeals no less effectively and pleasurably to those privileged to hear him. The feature of this annual concert is, and has been for a long succession of years, the contributions of Mr. Down's orchestra. Unhappily this assemblage of instrumentalists was on this occasion in one of its most ineffectual moods, any- thing like brightness or sparkle being conspicuously absent in execution of the various items of the pro- gramme. From the fact that so feeble a volunaue of tone emanated from so numerous a body of string players one is driven to the conclusion that many of them must be drones, while the continued assertiveness of a trombone player sufficed also to shew that the brass section was again out of balance. It is a pity that under these circumstances pieces should be chosen which (as in the case of the " William Tell "overture) serve but to accentuate limitations of executant capacity which are clearly exceeded. Only in one instance the first two of the three Henry VIII. Dances —was there any mitigation of the dire conditions alluded to, for here the strings were not only heard but it was possible for them to chew some semblance of taste because they were not overwhelmed by other sections of the orchestra. The humorous selections by Mr. Fred Walmslev were distinctly inferior in style and taste to anything of the kind heard recently at these concerts, and from some points of view it is to be regretted that the concert executive does not altogether dispense with contributions so utterly incongruous with the rest of the programme. Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bac., made a most efficient accompanist.</p>
        <p>THE BUSINESS FERTILISER.</p>
        <p>Advertising bears very much the same relation to my business as a fertiliser does to the soil in agriculture. The more a farmer works his land, the more fertiliser must he use; so, the more trade I do the greater must be my advertising appropriation.—A. W. Gamage, in the :` Advertising World." ' For Children's Hacking Cough at night, Woods' Great Peppermint Cure. 1/1.1, 2/9.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>LAWSON WATER: CONTRACT.</head>
        <p>THE LAWSON WATER: CONTRACT.</p>
        <p>Appeal for Publicity to The Arbitrator.</p>
        <p>The arbitration on the claim by Mr. Lawson against the Corporation in respect of moneys alleged to be owing on the contract at Delph:lane opens in London on Monday before Mr. G. H. Hill, M.I.C.E. arbitrator. The following appeal for the proceedings to be open to the Press has been addressed to the arbitrator, and signed by a majority of the Council. Dear Sir,—We respectfully approach you in regard to the reporting of the proceedings in connection with this arbitration by representatives of the Warrington newspapers. Deep and widespread interest has been and is being taken in the question at issue, and as . all matters in respect of procedure are in your sole direction, we desire, as representing a very large proportion of the burgesses of the County Borough, to urge upon you not merely the desirability but the essential necessity for the publication of some accurate report not necessarily full or verbatim, but such as would substantially convey the facts and evidence brought out in the proceedings before you, as well as the bases of your ultitn tte award.: We think you will agree with us that there can be no call whatever for privacy ; that on the other hand the more fully the ratepayers of Warrington are ' put in possession of the proceedings of your arbitration the better for them and for all concerned. We are, dear sir, Your obedient servants,- . COUN. T. H. ASHCROFT COUN. BURTON P, J. SKELLAND ff D. JOSEPH „ IV. BENSON /. DICKENSON I t G. T. WILDING ff J. J. NIELD 27 D. S. DUNCAN fl JAS. EVANS J, PEMBER.TON 2 f T. S. STEEL „ T. CFIANTLER n T. BA.RTON • „ W. CLEGG fl 0, TOOLE f 1 J. G. CRAIK ff J. HAWTHORN )1 J. WILKINSON f f CUNNINGTON „ J. LORD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-10-12_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>CHILD'S DEATH.</head>
        <p>A CHILD'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>CORONER AND THE WARRINGTON POLICE</p>
        <p>" A Grave Scandal:'</p>
        <p>Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, opened an inquest at the Warrington Police Station bat evening touching the death of John Kelly, aged 13 months, son of John Kelly, 21, Morris-court.</p>
        <p>EiPlaining the circumstances of the case, the Coroner said he was only going to open the inquest and adjourn it until next Friday so that the facts might be placed fully before the jury. Both of the child's parents had been before the magistrates, and the mother was now suffering a term of imprisonment of three months and the f tther one month, for having neglected the child. In connection with the matter, there was a set of circumstancee that did not appeal to him at all. The child died so far back as 10.30 last Saturday morning and although it was admitted to the Workhouse on the Ist inst and the parents were sent to prison on the 3rd, the child dying two days afterwards, the death was certified by the then medical officer. The registrar fortunately did not register the death. He (Mr. Brighouse) had been off duty from Monday suffer- - ing from illness, but on Wednesday Mr. Jones, his deputy, and Mr. Carr, his clerk, were at Warrington and some one from the Workhouse desired to speak to him (Mr. Brighouse). His clerk communicated by telephone with the assistant master of the Workhouse. who said" What about the case of that child Kelly ? " Mr. Carr said "What about it? We know nothing about the child Kelly," when it transpired that on Tuesday the Workhouse Master was put in com- munication with Police Constable Dennett who in turn was placed in communication with the Chief Constable. What happened he (Mr. Brighouse) did not know. No one had vouchsafed to say anything to him. He knew, however, that on 'Wednesday he knew nothing of the case and nothing had been done in the matter. The Chief Constable was not present at the inquest. He clearly must have had some communication with the Workhouse Master on Tuesday and it was a pecular thing that that com- munication was not passed on to somebody else and he (the Coroner) had to hear of it in this outside manner. When he (the Coroner) received a report of the case on Thursday morning he at once ordered a post mortem. "We have come down here on Friday afternoon " continued Mr. Brighouse "to open an inquest on a child that died last Saturday. Of course, if the police are proud of the achievement they can be proud. If this is a specimen of the new regime, I wish we could get back to the old method. I used to say the Warrington police got up their eases better than • anybody else, but I think they have gone to the other extreme now."</p>
        <p>Margaret Kelly, aged fourteen, sister of the deceased and an inmate of the Workhouse, gave evidence of identification.:</p>
        <p>Mr. J. F. Awbery,master of the Workhouse, was called and asked by the Coroner to state all he knew about the case. He said the child was admitted to the Work house on October Ist and died on the sth. He learned the circumstance,' under which the child came into the House from the Inspector of the N.S.P.C.C. and he also read that the parents had been convicted for neglect of children. On Monday morning, Dr. Harold Joseph was acting for the resident .medical officer, and he (Mr. Awbery) naturally expected him to refuse the death certificate. To his surprise the doctor gave the certi- ficate, but he (Mr. Awbery) should take it as being a back-handed compliment to a medical gentleman if he communicated the facts of a death when the certificate had been placed in his hands. He had always acted on the plan that where a medical officer certified the death there was no cause for him (Mr. Awbery) to give any intimation to the Coroner's officer. Where the medical officer refused to grant a certificate he always promptly notified the Coroner's officer. The Coroner : I have no Coroner's officer now. He'S • on night duty. Continuing, Mr. Awbery said that in the course of Monday morning he saw Dr. Peacocke, who asked him if a certificate had been given. Witness replied "Yes," whereupon Dr. Peacocks said "There ought never to have been one given," and intimated that he believed the cause of death was criminal starvation. He (Mr, Awbery) thought he would make himself clear in the matter, and so he communicated with the Chief Con- stable by telephone and told him the whole facts. He asked the Chief Constable what he was going to do and he replied "I will see the magistrates' clerk." The Coroner: What on earth has the magistrates clerk to do with it ?</p>
        <p>Mr. Awbery : I cannot answer that. I had to leave home on Tuesday and only returned on Thurs- day night.</p>
        <p>Awbery further said he instructed the assistant master to act on any communication from the police. The Coroner said that at the adjourned inquest Dr. Peacocke would be preent and give the result of the post mortein. He would also have the attendance of Dr. Harold Joseph who gave the certificate in the absence of the resident medical officer (Dr. Flitcroft) and be would want to know how long he saw the child and , what made him come to the conclusion that it had died from exhaustion. He did not see what else Mr. Awbery could do in the matter. He hoped to see the Chief Constable present at the adjourned inquiry with his explanation of the matter, as to why he did not act promptly and what the magistrates' clerk had to d!? with it. "I am coroner for Sonth-West Lancashire " said Mr. Brighouse "and the circumstances connected with a death have to be communicated to me as speedily as possible, it is an absolute grave scandal to ' think that a case should have to stand over from Saturday till to-day, through the inability of persons to discriminate as to what their proper duties are." Mr. Awbery said no slight on the Court was intended on his part. The Coroner : I have not a word against you. You did all you could. There was a medical certificate, so what else could you do? I think you did quite right. The inquest was then adjourned until 10 o'clock next • Friday morning.</p>
